    "lem-boundary-compressible-annulus-is-boundary-parallel": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-incompressibility-seifert-fibration-and-jsj-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth $3$-manifold. A properly embedded surface is **two-sided** if it has a product neighbourhood $F\\times[-1,1]$ in $M$. A **compressing disk** for a two-sided surface $F$ is an embedded disk $D\\subset M$ with $\\operatorname{int}(D)\\cap F=\\varnothing$ and $\\partial D$ an essential simple closed curve in $F$; $F$ is **incompressible** if it has no compressing disk. A **boundary-compressing disk** is an embedded disk $D$ with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha=D\\cap F$ is an arc not cutting off a disk in $F$, $\\beta=D\\cap\\partial M$, and $\\operatorname{int}(D)$ misses $F\\cup\\partial M$; $F$ is **boundary-incompressible** if it has no such disk. A surface is **boundary-parallel** if it cobounds a product with a subsurface of $\\partial M$, and it is **essential** if it is incompressible, boundary-incompressible, and not boundary-parallel. $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. An incompressible torus is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product $T^2\\times[0,1]$ with a component of $\\partial M$; $M$ is **atoroidal** if every incompressible torus is boundary-parallel. A **Seifert fibration** is a decomposition into circles with local models given by fibered solid tori; in the model $(D^2\\times S^1)/\\langle(z,w)\\mapsto(e^{2\\pi i/q}z,e^{2\\pi ip/q}w)\\rangle$, where $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, the core fiber has multiplicity $q$. Fibers of multiplicity $1$ are **regular** and those of multiplicity greater than $1$ are **exceptional**. A finite family of pairwise disjoint incompressible tori is a **JSJ system** if every component obtained by cutting $M$ along the family is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. It is **inclusion-minimal** if no proper subfamily is a JSJ system. Atoroidal does not mean hyperbolic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-choice-free-smooth-torus-curves-and-annulus-strips",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "An essential smooth embedded circle in the standard torus $T=\\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$ represents a nonzero primitive integral homology class. A finite disjoint family of essential smooth circles can be carried by a jointly smooth ambient isotopy of $T$ to parallel affine circles. Two essential circles with the same primitive unoriented class are smoothly ambient isotopic. The components between disjoint essential circles have smooth annulus parameters, with prescribed product collars on their boundary circles. No choice axiom is assumed.\n\nLikewise two smooth spanning arcs of a parametrized annulus with the same endpoints and relative winding number are smoothly ambient isotopic relative to any collars on which they already agree. When disjoint, they have a smooth disk-strip parameter; disjoint essential circles in a parametrized annulus have smooth annulus-strip parameters. All isotopies may be stationary near their time endpoints.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-choice-free-smooth-parametrized-ball-windows-and-disk-absorption",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n=2,3$, a given smooth ball embedding $e:D^n\\to N$ into a connected boundaryless smooth $n$-manifold is isotopic through embeddings to a small coordinate round ball, and that embedding isotopy extends ambiently with compact support. For a parametrized smooth $n$-ball $B$ and a parametrized smooth boundary $(n-1)$-disk $W$, the pair $(B,W)$ is diffeomorphic to the standard ball with a hemispherical window. The diffeomorphism can retain the specified window parameter, using stereographic parameters on the standard hemisphere and allowing the necessary orientation of that parameter. No choice axiom is assumed.\n\nConsequently, attaching a parametrized smooth $n$-ball to a smooth $n$-manifold along such a window does not change its diffeomorphism type, provided an attaching collar and corner rounding are supplied. The resulting diffeomorphism is supported in the added ball and an arbitrarily small receiving collar. The inverse assertion holds for removing a ball that meets the boundary in exactly one such window with the supplied collar charts. In dimension two the windows are intervals.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-compact-smooth-boundary-preimage-surfaces-admit-neat-embedding-isotopies",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth three-manifold, possibly nonorientable and with boundary, and let $f:F\\to M$ be a smooth embedding of a compact smooth surface with $f^{-1}(\\partial M)=\\partial F$. Transversality to $\\partial M$ is not assumed. Given an open neighborhood $U$ of $C=f(\\partial F)$, there is a jointly smooth map $(x,t)\\mapsto f_t(x)$ such that each $f_t$ is an embedding, $f_0=f$, every $f_t$ agrees with $f$ on $\\partial F$ and outside $f^{-1}(U)$, and $f_t$ is neatly proper for every $t>0$. Time may instead be reparametrized to be stationary near both endpoints; in that version neatness holds after the initial stationary interval.\n\nThere is also a jointly continuous ambient isotopy of homeomorphisms $H_t:M\\to M$, starting at the identity, supported in $U$, fixing $\\partial M$ pointwise, and satisfying $H_t f=f_t$. The construction makes $H_t$ jointly smooth on $(M\\setminus C)\\times[0,1]$. If $f$ is already neat, the same $f_t$ alternatively admits a jointly smooth ambient extension fixing $\\partial M$. No smooth ambient extension at an original tangency is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-angular-boundary-neatification-preserves-smooth-disk-obstructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F_0=f(F)$ be a compact two-sided smoothly embedded surface in a compact smooth three-manifold, with $f^{-1}(\\partial M)=\\partial F$, and let $F_1,H_1$ be its angular neatification from [[lem-compact-smooth-boundary-preimage-surfaces-admit-neat-embedding-isotopies]]. The ambient manifold may be nonorientable. If $F_1$ has a smooth compressing disk or smooth boundary-compressing disk, then $F_0$ has a disk of the same type. Boundary-compressing disks can also be transported in the opposite direction: if $F_0$ has one, then $F_1$ has one. Consequently absence of either kind of smooth disk passes from $F_0$ to $F_1$.\n\nHere the boundary-compressing witness has one boundary arc in the surface and the other in $\\partial M$, its interior missing both. The construction allows the broader convention of ordinary smooth disk embeddings with tangential endpoint contact, and also gives smooth corner-disk witnesses with transverse surface arcs when that convention is used. No smooth ambient removal of an original surface tangency is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "First apply [F9] to the given surface, fixed pointwise on its boundary. The resulting surface is neat. Incompressibility passes to it, and a boundary-compressing witness passes in either direction; absence of boundary compressions therefore passes as well. Work with this neat representative for the following smooth collar constructions. The boundary-fixing topological ambient extension pulls any resulting boundary-parallel product back to the actual original surface. Thus this preparation does not add a neatness hypothesis to the Statement. Write $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha$ is the essential proper arc in $A$ and $\\beta$ lies in a boundary torus $T$. In an annulus every essential proper arc joins its two boundary circles: cut along a radial spanning arc and use a lift to the strip; an arc returning to the same boundary cuts off a disk there. Consequently both boundary circles of $A$ lie on this same $T$. They are essential on $T$: otherwise choose an innermost disk bounded by one of them in $T$, push it inward off the annulus collar, and obtain a compression of $A$. By [F2] the two circles cut $T$ into two parametrized annuli. The interior of $\\beta$ misses them and joins them, so it spans one of these annuli, denoted $A_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "F9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Push the interior of $A_0$ a little into its inward collar, keeping its rim on $A$, and round the rim. The union $S=A\\cup A_0$ is a torus with a displayed corner before rounding. The curve $\\alpha\\cup\\beta$ on it meets a core of $A$ once algebraically, not merely modulo two: $\\alpha$ is a spanning arc of the annulus. It is therefore a nonseparating essential circle. Compress $S$ along $D$, taking two parallel copies of $D$ in its disk neighborhood; the result is a sphere. Irreducibility supplies a parametrized ball bounded by it. The disk neighborhood lies outside this ball: its strip along $\\beta$ is joined to $\\partial M$ through the gap left when that strip was removed from $A_0$, without crossing the compressed sphere. A ball in the interior cannot contain that boundary collar. Restoring the disk neighborhood therefore attaches one marked $1$-handle to the ball, rather than removing a possibly knotted handle from it. Its two attaching disk windows have the actual parallel-copy parameters of $D$. They can be put in standard positions by [F3]: standardize one window, shrink the other in the connected complement of the first on the boundary sphere, and move it to the opposite standard disk by the finite supported construction in [F3]'s Proof 1.1–2.1, fixing the first. Thus the restored region $U$ is a smooth solid torus with meridian disk the cocore of this handle. Its boundary is the displayed $S$; all corner changes are restored by the original quadrant collars.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The marking is essential. In the product coordinates $U=D^2\\times S^1$ supplied by that ball and handle, let $(\\mu,\\lambda)$ denote the meridian-longitude basis of its boundary. Each rim circle of $A$ meets the cocore boundary once algebraically, because it meets $\\alpha\\cup\\beta$ once. Hence its slope is $p\\mu+q\\lambda$ with $q=\\pm1$. By [F2] normalize the two rim circles to affine circles of that slope. The entire boundary isotopy extends into a radial annulus of $D^2\\times S^1$: in the boundary radial coordinate use the same formula $(r,x)\\mapsto(r,h_{\\eta(r)t}(x))$ as [F3], identity near the inner solid torus. The product diffeomorphism $(z,e^{i\\theta})\\mapsto(e^{ip\\theta}z,e^{iq\\theta})$, with inverse $(z,e^{i\\varphi})\\mapsto(e^{-ipq\\varphi}z,e^{iq\\varphi})$, carries longitudinal circles to that slope, up to replacing $p$ by its negative according to the chosen inverse. Apply its inverse. The two complementary boundary annuli now correspond to the two arcs between two points on $\\partial D^2$, times $S^1$. The disk with one of these marked arcs is a rectangle with its bottom edge marked, with the usual rounded-corner interpretation, by [F3] in dimension two. Taking its product with $S^1$ gives a product between the corresponding annuli: the first is $A\\times\\{0\\}$, and $A\\times\\{1\\}\\cup\\partial A\\times[0,1]$ is the second annulus $A_0$. Restore the retained quadrant collars to get this product for the actual original $A$ and boundary annulus in $\\partial M$. This proves boundary-parallelity in [F1]'s precise sense. The winding-one condition from $D$ is what justifies this product; complementary annuli in an arbitrary solid torus do not suffice. All selected charts, windows and curve moves were finite, and [F2]–[F3] require no choice.",
