            "6.1",
            "6.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-incompressibility-seifert-fibration-and-jsj-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth $3$-manifold. A properly embedded surface is **two-sided** if it has a product neighbourhood $F\\times[-1,1]$ in $M$. A **compressing disk** for a two-sided surface $F$ is an embedded disk $D\\subset M$ with $\\operatorname{int}(D)\\cap F=\\varnothing$ and $\\partial D$ an essential simple closed curve in $F$; $F$ is **incompressible** if it has no compressing disk. A **boundary-compressing disk** is an embedded disk $D$ with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha=D\\cap F$ is an arc not cutting off a disk in $F$, $\\beta=D\\cap\\partial M$, and $\\operatorname{int}(D)$ misses $F\\cup\\partial M$; $F$ is **boundary-incompressible** if it has no such disk. A surface is **boundary-parallel** if it cobounds a product with a subsurface of $\\partial M$, and it is **essential** if it is incompressible, boundary-incompressible, and not boundary-parallel. $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. An incompressible torus is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product $T^2\\times[0,1]$ with a component of $\\partial M$; $M$ is **atoroidal** if every incompressible torus is boundary-parallel. A **Seifert fibration** is a decomposition into circles with local models given by fibered solid tori; in the model $(D^2\\times S^1)/\\langle(z,w)\\mapsto(e^{2\\pi i/q}z,e^{2\\pi ip/q}w)\\rangle$, where $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, the core fiber has multiplicity $q$. Fibers of multiplicity $1$ are **regular** and those of multiplicity greater than $1$ are **exceptional**. A finite family of pairwise disjoint incompressible tori is a **JSJ system** if every component obtained by cutting $M$ along the family is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. It is **inclusion-minimal** if no proper subfamily is a JSJ system. Atoroidal does not mean hyperbolic.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "6.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-smooth-schoenflies-for-embedded-two-sphere-in-s-three",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $S\\subset S^3$ be the image of a smooth embedding of the standard $S^2$. There is a smooth ambient isotopy $H_t:S^3\\to S^3$, with $H_0=\\operatorname{id}$, such that $H_1(S)$ is the standard equatorial sphere $E$. In particular $S^3\\setminus S$ has exactly two components. Their closures $B_+$ and $B_-$ are smoothly embedded standard closed $3$-balls with common boundary $S$. If $D_+$ and $D_-$ denote the standard closed hemispheres bounded by $E$, the labels can be chosen so that the restrictions $H_1:B_\\pm\\to D_\\pm$ are diffeomorphisms with the same boundary restriction $H_1|_S:S\\to E$. Thus the ball identifications are compatible on their boundary and on a common two-sided collar of that boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-smooth-sphere-diffeomorphisms-extend-over-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every orientation-preserving smooth diffeomorphism $g:S^2\\to S^2$ is joined to the identity by a jointly smooth isotopy of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms, stationary near its time endpoints. Consequently every smooth diffeomorphism $b:\\partial B_0\\to\\partial B_1$ between the boundaries of two parametrized smooth $3$-balls extends to a smooth diffeomorphism $B_0\\to B_1$. In the radial boundary collars supplied by their parametrizations, the extension can be required to have the product form $(x,r)\\mapsto(b(x),r)$ on one smaller collar. Its orientation agrees with the orientation of $b$ on the boundaries, using the outward-normal convention.\n\nThis asserts existence of an isotopy for each given map; it does not assert a deformation retraction parametrized by the entire diffeomorphism group.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-smooth-jordan-disk-boundary-maps-extend-to-diffeomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. The bounded region of a smooth embedded\ncircle in the oriented plane, together with its boundary, is a smooth manifold\nwith boundary diffeomorphic to the closed standard disk. Let $D_0,D_1$ be two\nsuch closed regions. Every orientation-preserving smooth boundary\ndiffeomorphism $b:\\partial D_0\\to\\partial D_1$ extends to an\norientation-preserving diffeomorphism $B:D_0\\to D_1$. More precisely, for\nspecified smooth inward collars $c_i:\\partial D_i\\times[0,\\epsilon)\\to D_i$\nwith $c_i(p,0)=p$, the extension may be required to satisfy\n$B(c_0(p,r))=c_1(b(p),r)$ for every sufficiently small $r\\ge0$. In addition, given interior points $p_i\\in D_i$ and an orientation-preserving smooth diffeomorphism $q$ from a neighborhood of $p_0$ onto a neighborhood of $p_1$, the extension can agree with $q$ near $p_0$ while retaining the prescribed boundary collars.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-relative-smooth-mapping-classes-of-annulus-and-torus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "All isotopies below are jointly smooth diffeotopies, stationary near the time endpoints.\n\n1. On $A=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)\\times[0,1]$, every smooth diffeomorphism fixing both boundary circles pointwise is isotopic relative to the boundary to exactly one twist\n$$D_k(\\theta,u)=(\\theta+k\\rho(u),u),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhere $\\rho$ is a fixed smooth function equal to zero near $0$ and one near $1$. If the map initially fixes a boundary collar, a common smaller collar can be fixed throughout this isotopy. Without fixing the boundary, every annulus diffeomorphism is isotopic to one of the four maps $(\\theta,u)\\mapsto(\\varepsilon\\theta,u)$ or $(\\theta,u)\\mapsto(\\varepsilon\\theta,1-u)$, with $\\varepsilon=\\pm1$.\n2. Every smooth diffeomorphism of $T^2=\\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$ inducing the identity on $H_1(T^2;\\mathbb Z)$ is smoothly isotopic to the identity. Consequently every torus diffeomorphism is smoothly isotopic to the linear torus map inducing its integral unimodular matrix on $H_1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-gluing-irreducible-smooth-three-manifolds-across-injective-annuli",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $M$ be a compact orientable smooth three-manifold and let $\\mathcal A$ be a finite disjoint family of properly embedded two-sided annuli. Suppose each map $\\pi_1(A)\\to\\pi_1(M)$ is injective and every component of the cut-open manifold $M\\mathbin{\\|}\\mathcal A$, with its boundary corners rounded, is irreducible. Then $M$ is irreducible. The family may be empty, and its annuli need not be separating or boundary-incompressible.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-relative-transversality-for-embedded-surfaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $F$ be a compact smooth surface with boundary, let $M$ be a smooth three-manifold without boundary, and let $S\\subset M$ be a closed embedded smooth surface. If $i:F\\to M$ is a smooth embedding whose boundary restriction $i|_{\\partial F}$ is transverse to $S$, then there is a smooth isotopy $i_t:F\\to M$ through embeddings, fixed on a neighbourhood of $\\partial F$, such that $i_1(F)$ is transverse to $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
