    "lem-kneser-haken-finiteness-for-incompressible-tori": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-incompressibility-seifert-fibration-and-jsj-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth $3$-manifold. A properly embedded surface is **two-sided** if it has a product neighbourhood $F\\times[-1,1]$ in $M$. A **compressing disk** for a two-sided surface $F$ is an embedded disk $D\\subset M$ with $\\operatorname{int}(D)\\cap F=\\varnothing$ and $\\partial D$ an essential simple closed curve in $F$; $F$ is **incompressible** if it has no compressing disk. A **boundary-compressing disk** is an embedded disk $D$ with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha=D\\cap F$ is an arc not cutting off a disk in $F$, $\\beta=D\\cap\\partial M$, and $\\operatorname{int}(D)$ misses $F\\cup\\partial M$; $F$ is **boundary-incompressible** if it has no such disk. A surface is **boundary-parallel** if it cobounds a product with a subsurface of $\\partial M$, and it is **essential** if it is incompressible, boundary-incompressible, and not boundary-parallel. $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. An incompressible torus is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product $T^2\\times[0,1]$ with a component of $\\partial M$; $M$ is **atoroidal** if every incompressible torus is boundary-parallel. A **Seifert fibration** is a decomposition into circles with local models given by fibered solid tori; in the model $(D^2\\times S^1)/\\langle(z,w)\\mapsto(e^{2\\pi i/q}z,e^{2\\pi ip/q}w)\\rangle$, where $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, the core fiber has multiplicity $q$. Fibers of multiplicity $1$ are **regular** and those of multiplicity greater than $1$ are **exceptional**. A finite family of pairwise disjoint incompressible tori is a **JSJ system** if every component obtained by cutting $M$ along the family is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. It is **inclusion-minimal** if no proper subfamily is a JSJ system. Atoroidal does not mean hyperbolic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-smooth-incompressible-tori-admit-normal-position-in-a-compatible-triangulation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable irreducible smooth three-manifold with a supplied finite whole-star smooth manifold triangulation $T$. Let $\\mathcal S$ be a finite disjoint family of incompressible smooth tori in its interior. There is a jointly smooth ambient isotopy, fixed near $\\partial M$ and stationary near its time endpoints, after which the family misses the vertices, crosses edges transversely in finitely many points, meets every triangular face in finitely many disjoint arcs joining different edges, and meets each tetrahedron in finitely many proper disks whose boundary crosses each of its edges at most once. The boundary patterns are therefore the four triangle and three quadrilateral patterns. The finitely many balls cut out inside each tetrahedron by those disks have supplied ball parameters retaining their disk faces and face-cell corners. All tori are moved simultaneously; the family may be empty.\n\nThese are smooth surfaces in a specified compatible triangulation. No identification of an arbitrary topological marking with a smooth or PL structure is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-rectangular-normal-cut-components-are-interval-bundles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth three-manifold with a supplied finite whole-star smooth manifold triangulation. Let $\\mathcal S$ be a finite disjoint two-sided closed smooth torus family in its interior, in normal disk position: each tetrahedron meets it in proper disks with the triangle or quadrilateral boundary patterns. Suppose all balls obtained by cutting individual tetrahedra along those disks have supplied ball parameters, with their disk faces and face-cell corners retained. Supply parameters of the rectangle face cells, taking their opposite edges on $\\mathcal S$ as horizontal and their other two edges on triangulation edges as vertical.\n\nLet $C$ be a connected component of $M$ cut open along $\\mathcal S$ which meets every triangular face only in rectangle cells. Then $C$ is disjoint from $\\partial M$ and is a topological interval bundle over a compact connected surface without boundary. Its bundle admits a locally flat embedded core surface $Q$, obtained as a section, and the base has a finite cellulation. If $C$ is not homeomorphic to $T^2\\times I$, the core is one-sided and $C\\setminus Q$ is connected. No choice axiom is assumed.\n\nThe conclusion is a topological interval-bundle parameterization from the specified finite data. It asserts neither smoothness nor compatibility with an unrelated marking.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-connected-complement-surfaces-have-independent-mod-two-homology-classes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $N$ be a connected Hausdorff topological three-manifold without boundary. Let $S_1,\\ldots,S_m$ be finitely many disjoint compact connected locally flat embedded surfaces without boundary. They may be one-sided and nonorientable. If $N\\setminus\\bigcup_j S_j$ is connected, their canonical mod-two fundamental classes have linearly independent images in $H_2(N;\\mathbb F_2)$. The manifold may be noncompact and nonorientable, and the family may be empty. No choice axiom or ambient triangulation is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-haken-torus-bound-for-a-supplied-compatible-smooth-triangulation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable irreducible smooth three-manifold with nonempty boundary, and supply a finite compatible whole-star smooth manifold triangulation with $t$ triangular faces. Let $\\mathcal S$ be a finite disjoint family of two-sided incompressible smooth interior tori. If no component of $M$ cut open along $\\mathcal S$ is homeomorphic to $T^2\\times I$, then\n$$\n|\\mathcal S|\\le 4t+\\dim_{\\mathbb F_2}H_2(M;\\mathbb F_2)-1.\n$$\nThus any process accumulating cuts whose every finite stage has this no-product property can make only finitely many cuts. The family may be empty.\n\nThe supplied triangulation is compatible with the smooth structure in the stated whole-star sense. This result asserts no compatibility of an arbitrary topological marking and does not alter the scope of any other finiteness item.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-simplicial-and-singular-homology-agree-for-simplicial-complexes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every simplicial complex $K$ and abelian group $G$, there is a canonical\nnatural isomorphism $H_n^{\\mathrm{simp}}(K;G)\\cong H_n(|K|;G)$ for all $n$.\nWhen the vertex set has a supplied total order, it is induced by the\nsimplicial-to-singular chain map of [[def-simplicial-to-singular-chain-map]],\nextended $G$-linearly. Different supplied orders induce the same homology map.\nFor an arbitrary vertex set, the isomorphism is defined compatibly on its\nfinite subcomplexes and requires no choice principle.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F2] to the given finite torus family for the very triangulation in the Statement. Its smooth ambient isotopy retains incompressibility, all cut-component homeomorphism types and the no-product condition. It puts the family into actual normal disks in each parametrized tetrahedron and disjoint arcs in each triangular face, joining distinct edges. This invokes the accepted marked normal-position construction rather than treating a surgery that changes a torus as an isotopy.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "In each triangular face group the arcs by the pair of distinct edges they join. Within each of the three types they occur in a parallel stack. Between consecutive arcs of a stack is a rectangle, and collapsing those strips leaves at most one arc of each type, hence at most four complementary regions. Every nonrectangular region survives distinctly in this collapsed picture, so there are at most $4t$ such regions over all faces. The successive finite face-disk cuts and the marked rectangle product maps are supplied by [F2] and [F5]; no unparametrized polygon or missing side matching is being assumed.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $m$ be the number of cut components meeting all faces only in rectangles. By [F3] each is an interval bundle. The torus-product possibility is excluded by hypothesis. Thus they have disjoint one-sided compact locally flat cores $Q_1,\\ldots,Q_m$, and removing each core leaves its component connected. No ambient triangulation or smoothness of these cores is required.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Form the connected finite dual graph of all torus cuts, allowing loops, and choose a spanning tree. Let $k$ be the number of edges outside that tree. The corresponding $k$ tori, together with all the $Q_j$, have connected complement: remove the cores in the fully cut pieces, which remain connected by step2.1, and glue back the tree faces. A tree joins all the pieces and cannot create a new disconnection. Pass to $\\operatorname{Int}M$ by the finite inward collar push in [F5], chosen disjoint from these compact surfaces; its homotopy fixes them and proves that the interior inclusion induces a homology isomorphism, while their complement remains connected. Now [F4] gives $k+m$ independent mod-two classes, so $k+m\\le\\beta$, where $\\beta=\\dim_{\\mathbb F_2}H_2(M;\\mathbb F_2)$. This number is finite because the supplied triangulation is finite and its simplicial homology computes singular homology.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "Write $n=|\\mathcal T|$ and let $v$ be the number of cut components. The dual graph has $n$ edges, $v$ vertices and $k=n-v+1$ non-tree edges; thus $v=n-k+1$. Each component other than the $m$ rectangular-only ones contains a nonrectangular face region. A physical region belongs to just one cut component: its two tetrahedral sides are glued through its interior, or it is a boundary face with only one incident side. Distinct components therefore use distinct such regions, and step1.2 gives $v\\le m+4t$.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Combining these inequalities yields $n+1\\le k+m+4t\\le\\beta+4t$, which is exactly the stated bound. It also holds for the empty family. If an accumulating cutting process continued past $4t+\\beta-1$ cuts while each finite accumulated family retained the stated hypotheses, that stage would contradict the same bound for the fixed original triangulation. Thus no such process continues indefinitely. The proof retains the original countable-choice and smooth-compatible triangulation scope; it does not postulate an arbitrary unrelated TOP marking.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.2",
