          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $X=e(X_P)$ and $Y=E_K$. They are compact orientable smooth three-manifolds with common boundary torus $T_c$. The exterior $Y$ is connected by [F2]. For completeness $X$ is connected as well: in the standard connected solid torus join two points of $X_P$ by a path consisting of finitely many smooth chart arcs. Remove repeated arcs and perturb finitely many joins in small coordinate balls to obtain an embedded arc, fixed near its endpoints; in dimension three finitely many arcs can be made disjoint except at consecutive endpoints by small finite chart perturbations. By [F5] perturb it to avoid the pattern core $P$. On the punctured supplied tube of $P$, radial projection to its boundary, extended by the identity outside that tube, is continuous and sends this path into $X_P$. Consequently all exterior points are connected by paths. The outer torus has no compressing disk by hypothesis, so [F3], with the entire outer boundary component as subsurface and normal subgroup $1$, gives $\\pi_1(T_c)\\hookrightarrow\\pi_1(X)$. The companion product tube is exactly the supplied input of [F2], so $\\pi_1(T_c)\\hookrightarrow\\pi_1(Y)$ too. Both applications are smooth; an abstract finite PL disk has not been treated as a smooth disk.",
