        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-incompressible-surface-in-solid-torus-is-meridian-or-boundary-parallel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $V=S^1\\times D^2$ be a solid torus. Every connected, two-sided, properly embedded incompressible and boundary-incompressible surface $F\\subset V$ with nonempty boundary is a disk, either a meridian disk or a boundary-parallel disk. In particular, if $F$ is not boundary-parallel, it is a meridian disk. Any two smooth meridian disks with the same boundary admit a smooth isotopy of embeddings relative to that boundary, with a boundary-fixing topological ambient extension. If both disks are neat, this can be a smooth ambient isotopy relative to the boundary. In addition, every two-sided embedded torus $T\\subset\\operatorname{int}V$ has a compressing disk in $V$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-smooth-loop-theorem-and-incompressibility-criterion-in-orientable-three-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable smooth three-manifold, let $F$ be a compact connected smooth subsurface of $\\partial M$, and let $N\\lhd\\pi_1(F)$ be normal. If a continuous disk map into $M$ has its boundary in $\\operatorname{Int}F$ with class outside $N$, there is a smoothly properly embedded disk in $M$ whose boundary lies in $\\operatorname{Int}F$ and has class outside $N$. No pointwise preservation of the original boundary loop is asserted.\n\nConsequently a compact neatly properly embedded two-sided smooth surface $S$ in such a compact orientable smooth three-manifold is incompressible if and only if its inclusion induces an injection on fundamental groups, component by component. Here incompressibility means absence of a smooth embedded compressing disk, with the conventions of the indicated definition.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-incompressible-surface-torus-boundary-essential-or-parallel-annulus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable irreducible $3$-manifold whose boundary components are tori. Let $S\\subset M$ be a connected, two-sided, properly embedded incompressible surface with nonempty boundary contained in $\\partial M$. Then either $S$ is boundary-incompressible, or $S$ is an annulus boundary-parallel into $\\partial M$.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-choice-free-smooth-torus-curves-and-annulus-strips",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "An essential smooth embedded circle in the standard torus $T=\\mathbb R^2/\\mathbb Z^2$ represents a nonzero primitive integral homology class. A finite disjoint family of essential smooth circles can be carried by a jointly smooth ambient isotopy of $T$ to parallel affine circles. Two essential circles with the same primitive unoriented class are smoothly ambient isotopic. The components between disjoint essential circles have smooth annulus parameters, with prescribed product collars on their boundary circles. No choice axiom is assumed.\n\nLikewise two smooth spanning arcs of a parametrized annulus with the same endpoints and relative winding number are smoothly ambient isotopic relative to any collars on which they already agree. When disjoint, they have a smooth disk-strip parameter; disjoint essential circles in a parametrized annulus have smooth annulus-strip parameters. All isotopies may be stationary near their time endpoints.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-smooth-two-sided-klein-bottle-curves-have-vertical-or-horizontal-normal-forms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be the smooth Klein bottle with coordinates $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2$ modulo $(x,y)\\sim(x,y+1)$ and $(x,y)\\sim(x+1,-y)$, with its circle-bundle fibers the images of $x=\\text{constant}$. Every finite disjoint family of essential two-sided smooth embedded circles can be carried by a jointly smooth ambient isotopy to a family either of vertical fiber circles or of horizontal circles transverse to those fibers. No choice axiom is assumed. The horizontal circles have representatives $y=c$ together with their deck partners $y=-c$, with $c\\not\\equiv0,1/2$ modulo one. The isotopy may be stationary near its time endpoints.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-simultaneous-smooth-disk-and-primitive-annulus-normalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. All isotopies below are jointly smooth ambient isotopies, stationary near their time endpoints. In this lemma a **neatly proper** surface meets the ambient boundary exactly in its own boundary and transversely there; this is the transverse proper-embedding convention of Hatcher, printed p.3. A boundary-preimage condition alone is insufficient. The explicit common-collar alternative in (a) also covers tangent boundary collars.\n\n(a) In a parametrized smooth ball or solid torus $U$, let two finite disjoint systems of smooth disks meet $\\partial U$ exactly in their boundaries, paired so corresponding disks have the same boundary. Suppose either both systems are neatly proper, or the paired systems already agree on supplied embedded boundary collars. Then they are ambient isotopic relative to $\\partial U$. In the common-collar alternative the collars may be tangent to $\\partial U$, and the isotopy fixes one smaller common collar. The families may be empty.\n\n(b) In the product solid torus $V=D^2\\times S^1$, a finite disjoint system of neatly proper incompressible smooth annuli whose boundary circles are vertical product fibers is ambient isotopic, relative to the boundary, to vertical annuli $a_j\\times S^1$, with disjoint neatly proper arcs $a_j\\subset D^2$. If the annuli are already vertical on a boundary collar, a smaller collar may be fixed.\n\n(c) A finite disjoint system of neatly proper smooth disks in $V$ whose boundary circles are horizontal and meridional is ambient isotopic, relative to the boundary, to horizontal disks (graphs of smooth maps $D^2\\to S^1$). The same common-collar assertion holds if the disks are already horizontal there.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-relative-transversality-for-embedded-surfaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $F$ be a compact smooth surface with boundary, let $M$ be a smooth three-manifold without boundary, and let $S\\subset M$ be a closed embedded smooth surface. If $i:F\\to M$ is a smooth embedding whose boundary restriction $i|_{\\partial F}$ is transverse to $S$, then there is a smooth isotopy $i_t:F\\to M$ through embeddings, fixed on a neighbourhood of $\\partial F$, such that $i_1(F)$ is transverse to $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-parametric-transversality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\mathcal F:M\\times S\\to N$ be a smooth family of maps and let $Z\\subseteq N$\nbe an embedded submanifold. If $\\mathcal F\\pitchfork Z$, then the set of\nparameters $s\\in S$ for which the slice $\\mathcal F_s:M\\to N$ fails to be\ntransverse to $Z$ is a null subset of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
