    "lem-essential-annulus-in-seifert-piece-isotopic-to-vertical": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-essential-surface-in-seifert-piece-is-vertical-or-horizontal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M$ be a compact connected irreducible Seifert-fibered $3$-manifold, and let $F\\subset M$ be a connected, two-sided essential annulus or torus, neatly embedded at its boundary (the condition is vacuous for a torus). Then $F$ is ambient isotopic to either a vertical surface, meaning a union of regular fibers, or a horizontal surface, meaning a surface transverse to every fiber. For a horizontal surface $F$ with $n$ sheets over the base orbifold $B$ and exceptional-fiber multiplicities $q_1,\\ldots,q_m$, the Euler characteristics satisfy\n$$\n\\frac{\\chi(F)}{n}=\\chi(B)-\\sum_{i=1}^m\\left(1-\\frac1{q_i}\\right).\n$$\nIf $F$ is two-sided and horizontal, cutting $M$ along $F$ gives an interval bundle over a surface.\n\nFor a smooth boundary-preimage embedding without the neatness condition, the same vertical-or-horizontal normal form is attained by a smooth isotopy of embeddings, fixed on the original boundary during its preliminary neatification, and by a topological ambient isotopy. The Euler count and interval-bundle conclusions apply to the resulting horizontal representative. A smooth ambient isotopy is asserted for the neat inputs above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-horizontal-annuli-in-orientable-seifert-pieces-admit-vertical-refiberings",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable smooth Seifert-fibered three-manifold and let $A$ be a properly embedded connected two-sided horizontal annulus. Then $M$ has another smooth Seifert fibration for which the given annulus $A$ is vertical. Its underlying manifold is $T^2\\times I$ or the orientable twisted interval bundle over the Klein bottle. No irreducibility assumption is needed, and no isotopy of the given horizontal annulus is needed for the refibering conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-incompressibility-seifert-fibration-and-jsj-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth $3$-manifold. A properly embedded surface is **two-sided** if it has a product neighbourhood $F\\times[-1,1]$ in $M$. A **compressing disk** for a two-sided surface $F$ is an embedded disk $D\\subset M$ with $\\operatorname{int}(D)\\cap F=\\varnothing$ and $\\partial D$ an essential simple closed curve in $F$; $F$ is **incompressible** if it has no compressing disk. A **boundary-compressing disk** is an embedded disk $D$ with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha=D\\cap F$ is an arc not cutting off a disk in $F$, $\\beta=D\\cap\\partial M$, and $\\operatorname{int}(D)$ misses $F\\cup\\partial M$; $F$ is **boundary-incompressible** if it has no such disk. A surface is **boundary-parallel** if it cobounds a product with a subsurface of $\\partial M$, and it is **essential** if it is incompressible, boundary-incompressible, and not boundary-parallel. $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. An incompressible torus is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product $T^2\\times[0,1]$ with a component of $\\partial M$; $M$ is **atoroidal** if every incompressible torus is boundary-parallel. A **Seifert fibration** is a decomposition into circles with local models given by fibered solid tori; in the model $(D^2\\times S^1)/\\langle(z,w)\\mapsto(e^{2\\pi i/q}z,e^{2\\pi ip/q}w)\\rangle$, where $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, the core fiber has multiplicity $q$. Fibers of multiplicity $1$ are **regular** and those of multiplicity greater than $1$ are **exceptional**. A finite family of pairwise disjoint incompressible tori is a **JSJ system** if every component obtained by cutting $M$ along the family is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. It is **inclusion-minimal** if no proper subfamily is a JSJ system. Atoroidal does not mean hyperbolic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-orientable-seifert-pieces-with-boundary-are-irreducible",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Every compact connected orientable smooth Seifert-fibered three-manifold with nonempty boundary is irreducible.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-compact-smooth-boundary-preimage-surfaces-admit-neat-embedding-isotopies",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth three-manifold, possibly nonorientable and with boundary, and let $f:F\\to M$ be a smooth embedding of a compact smooth surface with $f^{-1}(\\partial M)=\\partial F$. Transversality to $\\partial M$ is not assumed. Given an open neighborhood $U$ of $C=f(\\partial F)$, there is a jointly smooth map $(x,t)\\mapsto f_t(x)$ such that each $f_t$ is an embedding, $f_0=f$, every $f_t$ agrees with $f$ on $\\partial F$ and outside $f^{-1}(U)$, and $f_t$ is neatly proper for every $t>0$. Time may instead be reparametrized to be stationary near both endpoints; in that version neatness holds after the initial stationary interval.\n\nThere is also a jointly continuous ambient isotopy of homeomorphisms $H_t:M\\to M$, starting at the identity, supported in $U$, fixing $\\partial M$ pointwise, and satisfying $H_t f=f_t$. The construction makes $H_t$ jointly smooth on $(M\\setminus C)\\times[0,1]$. If $f$ is already neat, the same $f_t$ alternatively admits a jointly smooth ambient extension fixing $\\partial M$. No smooth ambient extension at an original tangency is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-angular-boundary-neatification-preserves-smooth-disk-obstructions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F_0=f(F)$ be a compact two-sided smoothly embedded surface in a compact smooth three-manifold, with $f^{-1}(\\partial M)=\\partial F$, and let $F_1,H_1$ be its angular neatification from [[lem-compact-smooth-boundary-preimage-surfaces-admit-neat-embedding-isotopies]]. The ambient manifold may be nonorientable. If $F_1$ has a smooth compressing disk or smooth boundary-compressing disk, then $F_0$ has a disk of the same type. Boundary-compressing disks can also be transported in the opposite direction: if $F_0$ has one, then $F_1$ has one. Consequently absence of either kind of smooth disk passes from $F_0$ to $F_1$.\n\nHere the boundary-compressing witness has one boundary arc in the surface and the other in $\\partial M$, its interior missing both. The construction allows the broader convention of ordinary smooth disk embeddings with tangential endpoint contact, and also gives smooth corner-disk witnesses with transverse surface arcs when that convention is used. No smooth ambient removal of an original surface tangency is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a broad input first use [F9] to pass to a neat representative, retaining both disk-obstruction hypotheses. At the end prepend that smooth embedding path and its explicit topological ambient extension. For a neat input use the argument directly. Properness of the annulus makes the boundary of $M$ nonempty, so $M$ is irreducible by [F4]. A boundary-parallel annulus has a boundary compression: in its product with a boundary annulus, a spanning arc crossed with the product interval is a boundary-compressing rectangle. Therefore the given boundary-incompressible annulus is not boundary-parallel and is essential. All hypotheses of [F1], including irreducibility, are now discharged. Apply it to isotope $A$ to a vertical or horizontal annulus.",
