          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] to the second fibration. Again the annulus becomes vertical by a smooth embedding isotopy without replacing that second fibration. Restricting this embedding isotopy to the boundary gives a smooth isotopy of boundary circles on $T$, so it preserves the free isotopy class of its boundary circle on $T$, so that circle also represents the regular-fiber slope for the second fibration. The two primitive slopes are therefore isotopic on $T$. Repeating this argument for each boundary torus, using the annulus family $\\mathcal A$, proves the claim for all boundary components.",
