            "1.3",
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-classification-of-compact-connected-surfaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Every nonempty compact\nconnected boundaryless topological $2$-manifold is homeomorphic to exactly one\nof the following types: the sphere $S^2$, a connected sum of $g\\geq1$ tori,\nor a connected sum of $k\\geq1$ real projective planes. The connected sums here\nare the explicit polygonal models with their chosen disk removals and boundary\ngluings ([[def-connected-sum-of-compact-surfaces]]). The integer is unique\nwithin its type. Equivalently, two such surfaces are homeomorphic if and only\nif they have the same integral orientability and Euler characteristic.\n\nThe canonical one-face words are respectively the sphere digon $aa^{-1}$\n(whose *reduced notation* is empty), the $g$-fold commutator word\n$\\prod_{i=1}^g a_i b_i a_i^{-1} b_i^{-1}$, and the $k$-fold square word\n$\\prod_{j=1}^k c_jc_j$. The empty notation is not an empty polygonal schema.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-essential-surface-in-seifert-piece-is-vertical-or-horizontal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M$ be a compact connected irreducible Seifert-fibered $3$-manifold, and let $F\\subset M$ be a connected, two-sided essential annulus or torus, neatly embedded at its boundary (the condition is vacuous for a torus). Then $F$ is ambient isotopic to either a vertical surface, meaning a union of regular fibers, or a horizontal surface, meaning a surface transverse to every fiber. For a horizontal surface $F$ with $n$ sheets over the base orbifold $B$ and exceptional-fiber multiplicities $q_1,\\ldots,q_m$, the Euler characteristics satisfy\n$$\n\\frac{\\chi(F)}{n}=\\chi(B)-\\sum_{i=1}^m\\left(1-\\frac1{q_i}\\right).\n$$\nIf $F$ is two-sided and horizontal, cutting $M$ along $F$ gives an interval bundle over a surface.\n\nFor a smooth boundary-preimage embedding without the neatness condition, the same vertical-or-horizontal normal form is attained by a smooth isotopy of embeddings, fixed on the original boundary during its preliminary neatification, and by a topological ambient isotopy. The Euler count and interval-bundle conclusions apply to the resulting horizontal representative. A smooth ambient isotopy is asserted for the neat inputs above.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-satellite-torus-is-essential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $K$ be a nontrivial smooth knot with a specified companion solid torus $V=e(S^1\\times D^2)$, let $P\\subset\\operatorname{int}(S^1\\times D^2)$ be a pattern, and assume the outer torus of its pattern exterior $X_P$ has no compressing disk and is not product-parallel to the inner pattern torus. Let $T_c=\\partial V=e(\\partial(S^1\\times D^2))\\subset E_{P(K)}$ be the companion torus. Then $T_c$ is incompressible and is not boundary-parallel in $E_{P(K)}$; hence it is essential.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-satellite-companion-is-detected-by-jsj",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M=E_{P(K)}$ be a satellite knot exterior with companion torus $T_c$. Suppose the pattern hypotheses of the essential-companion-torus lemma hold, both components of $M$ cut along $T_c$ are atoroidal or Seifert-fibered, and $M$ itself is neither atoroidal nor Seifert-fibered. Then the inclusion-minimal JSJ system of $M$ consists of a torus ambient isotopic to $T_c$. Cutting along that torus identifies the companion exterior $E_K$ as the component on the outside of the embedded companion solid torus. No conclusion is made for an essential companion torus that is absorbed into a Seifert-fibered union.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-jsj-decomposition-of-knot-exteriors",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $K:S^1\\to S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with a chosen compact exterior $E_K$. There exists a finite inclusion-minimal collection $\\mathcal T$ of pairwise disjoint incompressible tori in the interior of $E_K$ such that every component of $E_K$ cut along $\\mathcal T$ is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. Any two such inclusion-minimal collections are ambient isotopic in $E_K$. Atoroidal pieces in this statement are not asserted to be hyperbolic.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-seifert-boundary-fibers-are-rigid-under-nonexceptional-free-product-quotients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable smooth Seifert-fibered three-manifold and let $T$ be a boundary torus whose inclusion induces an injection on fundamental groups. Fix one Seifert fibration and a based regular fiber $h\\in\\pi_1(T)\\subset\\pi_1(M)$. Suppose the quotient $\\pi_1(M)/\\langle h\\rangle$ is isomorphic to a finite free product of at least two nontrivial groups, excluding the free product of two groups of order two. Then every Seifert fibration of $M$ has the same unoriented regular-fiber slope on $T$ as the fixed fibration. No choice hypothesis is introduced. The notation $\\langle h\\rangle$ denotes the cyclic subgroup, which is normal as proved below.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-compact-smooth-manifolds-with-corners-admit-relative-finite-triangulations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice as an ambient hypothesis; the finite construction and\nthe ODE interfaces below use no additional choice, so this hypothesis is\ndispensable. A smooth manifold with corners here is Hausdorff and second\ncountable, with charts in relatively open subsets of $[0,\\infty)^c\\times\\mathbb R^{n-c}$\nand transition maps smoothly extendible with invertible derivative at corner\npoints; transitions preserve the local depth and its face germs. Let $M$ be\ncompact. Then $M$ has a finite corner-adapted whole-star smooth triangulation.\nCorner faces are locally subpolyhedra, and any specified finite union of\nclosed faces is a subpolyhedron.\n\nIf the total boundary already has a finite piecewise smooth triangulation\n$f:L\\to\\partial M$ compatible on all local face intersections, with its\ndifferential fan giving a homeomorphism onto the union of boundary faces of the\ntangent orthant at each point, the triangulation extends to $M$, pointwise\npreserving $f$ with compatible subdivisions allowed. In the resulting finite\nunion construction all intersections may be made full subcomplexes. The same\nrelative conclusion applies to a fixed corner-adapted triangulated closed neighbourhood piece\nwhose smaller protected subcomplex lies in its relative interior: fitting is\nperformed away from that protected subcomplex. No extension of arbitrary\nincompatible boundary data is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-nonexceptional-finite-free-products-have-no-normal-cyclic-subgroup",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G=G_1*\\cdots*G_k$, where $2\\le k<\\infty$ and every factor is nontrivial. If $k=2$, assume the two factors are not both cyclic of order two. Then every cyclic normal subgroup of $G$ is trivial. No choice hypothesis is required.",
          "uses": [
