        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "The horizontal case is impossible. The interval-bundle bases inherit smooth transverse charts from the horizontal surface and the fiber charts. For any such compact connected surface with nonempty boundary, [F10] gives a finite triangulation. Root a spanning tree of its triangle-adjacency graph at a boundary edge. Delete each triangle across its exposed parent edge, starting at the root; this collapses the surface to a finite connected graph. Its group is therefore free, with rank equal to the rank of its abelianization, by collapsing a graph spanning tree. For a closed surface, the polygon models of [F5] give abelianization $\\mathbb Z^{2g}$ in the orientable genus $g$ case and $\\mathbb Z^{k-1}\\oplus\\mathbb Z/2$ in the nonorientable genus $k$ case, by adding the signed edge occurrences in its single face word. Hence a compact surface group with torsion-free rank-two abelianization is either the torus group $\\mathbb Z^2$ or the free group of rank two; with torsion-free rank-one abelianization it is infinite cyclic. The cable group has abelianization $\\mathbb Z^2$, is nonabelian because its quotient is $\\mathbb Z*C_2$, and has the nonidentity central element $h$ since $[h]=-2[c]$ in its abelianization. It is neither $\\mathbb Z^2$ nor the free group of rank two, whose center is trivial by [F11]. The trefoil group has abelianization $\\mathbb Z$ and is nonabelian by step 1.3, so it too is not a surface group with that abelianization. Thus neither cut piece can be an interval bundle over a surface.",
