        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F4] and [A1], $H_2(X;\\mathbb Z)$ is finitely generated. Its quotient $L=H_2(X;\\mathbb Z)/\\operatorname{Tor}$ is therefore free of finite rank, and every homomorphism from $H_2$ to $\\mathbb Z$ factors uniquely through $L$. Thus $\\operatorname{Hom}(H_2,\\mathbb Z)=\\operatorname{Hom}(L,\\mathbb Z)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Under the isomorphism [F2], the map $L\\to\\operatorname{Hom}(L,\\mathbb Z)$ sending $\\bar x$ to $(\\bar y\\mapsto Q_X(\\bar x,\\bar y))$ is the composite of the inverse Poincaré-duality map $H_2\\to H^2$ with the evaluation map of [F3]; [F5] identifies that composite with the intersection pairing. Both component maps are isomorphisms by steps 1.1–1.2 and [F2]. Hence the pairing map is an isomorphism.",
