    "thm-asd-deformation-complex-index-formula": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-graded-hermitian-clifford-module-and-geometric-dirac",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth Riemannian manifold of even dimension $2m$, and let $V=V^+\\oplus V^-$ be a finite-rank smooth complex vector bundle with a Hermitian metric linear in its first variable. Write $\\mathrm{Cl}_{\\mathbb C}(T^*M,-g)$ for the complex Clifford bundle generated by cotangent vectors with relation $\\xi\\eta+\\eta\\xi=-2g^{-1}(\\xi,\\eta)$. A **graded Hermitian Clifford action** is a smooth real-linear map $c:T^*M\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$ such that $c(\\xi)^*=-c(\\xi)$, $c(\\xi)V^\\pm\\subseteq V^\\mp$, and $c(\\xi)c(\\eta)+c(\\eta)c(\\xi)=-2g^{-1}(\\xi,\\eta)I$. It extends complex-linearly to the complex Clifford bundle.\n\nA **graded Hermitian Clifford connection** is a unitary connection $\\nabla^V$ preserving $V^\\pm$ and satisfying\n$$\\nabla^V_X(c(\\xi)v)=c(\\nabla^{LC}_X\\xi)v+c(\\xi)\\nabla^V_Xv$$\nfor smooth $X,\\xi,v$, where $\\nabla^{LC}$ is the Levi–Civita connection. In a local orthonormal coframe $(e^i)$ define\n$$D_Vv=\\sum_i c(e^i)\\nabla^V_{e_i}v.$$\nThe operator is odd, so its chiral part is $D_V^+:C^\\infty(V^+)\\to C^\\infty(V^-)$. Under Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), the formula's frame independence, formal symmetry on closed $M$, and global bundle well-definedness are proved in [[lem-geometric-dirac-operator-is-well-defined]]. A domain and index are assigned only under the compact hypotheses of later items.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-even-dimensional-spinor-bundle-chirality-and-spin-connection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M$ be an oriented Riemannian manifold of even dimension $n=2m\\ge2$ with a specified Spin lift $P\\to P_{SO}(TM)$. Let $S_0$ be the complex irreducible Clifford module of dimension $2^m$ supplied by [[thm-complex-spin-modules-and-compact-reducibility]], with positive-square complex Clifford action $c_+$ and full-even Spin representation $\\sigma$. Put $c_0(v)=i\\,c_+(v)$ for the negative-square cotangent action. This changes the Clifford square sign and preserves equivariance under $\\sigma$, since the scalar $i$ commutes with that action. Define the complex spinor bundle by the associated real bundle $P\\times_{\\operatorname{Spin}(n)}(S_0)_{\\mathbb R}$ using the realification of $\\sigma$, and descend multiplication by $i$ and the Clifford action through their commuting transition maps.\n\nFor an oriented orthonormal basis $(e_1,\\ldots,e_{2m})$, define the chirality operator on $S_0$ by $\\Gamma_{S_0}=i^m c_0(e^1)\\cdots c_0(e^{2m})$. The calculation below proves that this is basis-independent, squares to $1$, and anticommutes with each $c_0(\\xi)$. Its $\\pm1$ eigenspaces are $S_0^\\pm$ and their associated bundles are $S^\\pm$. The Levi–Civita connection on $P_{SO}(TM)$ lifts through the differential of $\\operatorname{Spin}(n)\\to SO(n)$ to the **spin connection** $\\widetilde\\nabla^S$ on $S$. This item defines these objects; [[lem-spinor-and-clifford-associated-bundles-are-well-defined]] supplies the global bundle, metric, connection, and transition verification.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-spinor-and-clifford-associated-bundles-are-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. On an even-dimensional oriented Riemannian manifold with a supplied Spin lift, the realified associated construction in [[def-even-dimensional-spinor-bundle-chirality-and-spin-connection]] is a smooth Hermitian graded Clifford bundle with a global compatible spin connection. For any graded Hermitian Clifford module there is locally a graded factorization $V\\cong S_0\\widehat\\otimes W$. Its local multiplicity spaces $W=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathrm{Cl}}(S_0,V)$ and the relative curvature $F^{V/S}:=F^V-c(R^{LC})$ have transition laws for which $\\operatorname{str}_W\\exp(-F^{V/S}/(2\\pi i))$ is a global even form. In the spin-twisted case $V=S\\widehat\\otimes E$, this relative curvature is the curvature $F^E$ of the supplied unitary connection. For the zero-dimensional relative statement, use the orientation-graded reference line of [[def-ahat-and-relative-chern-character-forms]]; this does not construct a Spin$(0)$ lift.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-geometric-clifford-module-dirac-index-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M$ be a compact, boundaryless, oriented Riemannian manifold of even dimension $2m$ with $m\\ge0$, and let $V=V^+\\oplus V^-$ be a finite-rank graded Hermitian Clifford module with compatible connection as in [[def-graded-hermitian-clifford-module-and-geometric-dirac]]. The chiral operator\n$$D_V^+:H^1(M;V^+)\\longrightarrow L^2(M;V^-)$$\nis Fredholm and its complex index is\n$$\\operatorname{ind}(D_V^+)=\\int_M\\left[\\widehat A(TM,\\nabla^{LC})\\operatorname{ch}(V/S)\\right]_{2m}.$$\nThe integral is with the stated orientation. For $m=0$, write $\\varepsilon_x\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ for the orientation sign at $x$. Use the reference line $S_{0,x}=\\mathbb C$ with grading $\\Gamma_{S_{0,x}}=\\varepsilon_xI$ and multiplicity grading $\\Gamma_{W_x}=\\varepsilon_x\\Gamma_{V_x}$ from [[def-ahat-and-relative-chern-character-forms]]. The relative curvature is zero and the degree-zero integrand is $\\varepsilon_x(\\dim V_x^+-\\dim V_x^-)$. The given grading of $V$ remains arbitrary, and no Spin lift is required. The result is a complex index; it makes no real KO-index assertion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-hodge-laplacian-kernel-represents-de-rham-and-dolbeault-cohomology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a compact smooth Riemannian manifold without boundary, of real dimension at least two. Its metric defines the $L^2$ pairing on forms by the Riemannian density; orientation is unnecessary. Let $d^*$ denote the formal adjoint of $d$, and $\\Delta=dd^*+d^*d$ on total real forms, or on their complexification. With domain $H^2$ the operator is nonnegative and selfadjoint. Its degreewise smooth kernels $\\mathcal H^k$ are finite dimensional, and there is an orthogonal smooth decomposition\n$$\\Omega^k(M)=\\mathcal H^k\\mathbin{\\oplus^\\perp}d\\Omega^{k-1}(M)\\mathbin{\\oplus^\\perp}d^*\\Omega^{k+1}(M).$$\nThe map $h\\mapsto[h]$ is an isomorphism $\\mathcal H^k\\to H^k_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)$, over $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$ as appropriate.\n\nMore generally let $X$ be a compact complex manifold of complex dimension $m\\ge1$ with a smooth Hermitian metric. Let $E$ be a holomorphic bundle of finite rank with a smooth Hermitian metric. The holomorphic bundle structure supplies frames with holomorphic invertible transition matrices; the Hermitian metric is separate smooth positive-definite pairing data. On $E$-valued $(p,q)$ forms, define $\\bar\\partial_E$ by the coefficientwise Dolbeault operator in holomorphic bundle charts and let $\\bar\\partial_E^*$ be its formal adjoint. For each fixed $p$, $\\Delta_{\\bar\\partial_E}=\\bar\\partial_E\\bar\\partial_E^*+\\bar\\partial_E^*\\bar\\partial_E$ on the sum over $q$ is nonnegative and selfadjoint on $H^2$; its smooth harmonic spaces $\\mathcal H_E^{p,q}$ are finite dimensional and\n$$\\Omega^{p,q}(X,E)=\\mathcal H_E^{p,q}\\mathbin{\\oplus^\\perp}\\bar\\partial_E\\Omega^{p,q-1}(X,E)\\mathbin{\\oplus^\\perp}\\bar\\partial_E^*\\Omega^{p,q+1}(X,E).$$\nIn particular harmonic forms represent the smooth Dolbeault quotient\n$$H_{\\bar\\partial}^{p,q}(X,E):=\\ker\\bar\\partial_E/\\operatorname{im}\\bar\\partial_E$$\nuniquely. Terms outside the degree range are zero. This asserts smooth Dolbeault cohomology; an identification with sheaf cohomology requires a separate Dolbeault-resolution comparison theorem.\n\nFor either complex write $D=d$ or $D=\\bar\\partial_E$, $P=DD^*+D^*D$, and let $\\Pi,G$ be the preceding Green projection and inverse. They preserve degree, $G$ preserves smooth forms, and on smooth forms\n$$DG=GD,\\qquad D^*G=GD^*,\\qquad D\\Pi=\\Pi D=D^*\\Pi=\\Pi D^*=0.$$\nEvery closed smooth $u$ satisfies $u=\\Pi u+D(D^*Gu)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-de-rham-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, integration on smooth simplices followed by the inverse of restriction from continuous to smooth singular cohomology gives a natural isomorphism of unital graded real algebras\n$$J_M:H^*_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\xrightarrow{\\cong}H^*_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb R).$$\nThe source product is wedge of form classes, the target product is the front/back singular cup product, and both stars denote direct sums of the homogeneous groups. Naturality is for smooth maps. No compactness assumption is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-chern-pontryagin-and-euler-characteristic-forms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold,\npossibly with boundary. If $E\\to M$ is a rank-$r$ complex vector bundle with\ncomplex connection $\\nabla$ and curvature $\\Omega$, define the total Chern form\n$$c(\\nabla)=\\det\\!\\left(I-\\frac{\\Omega}{2\\pi i}\\right)=\\sum_{j=0}^r c_j(\\nabla),$$\nwhere $c_j(\\nabla)$ has degree $2j$. For a Hermitian connection these forms\nare real-valued; a general complex connection need not give real-valued forms.\n\nFor a rank-$r$ real vector bundle with real connection $\\nabla$, let\n$E_{\\mathbb C}=E\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb C$ carry the complexified\nconnection $\\nabla_{\\mathbb C}$ and set\n$$p_j(\\nabla)=(-1)^j c_{2j}(\\nabla_{\\mathbb C})\\quad\\text{in degree }4j,\\qquad p(\\nabla)=\\sum_{j\\geq0}p_j(\\nabla).$$\nThen $p_0=1$, $p_j=0$ when $2j>r$, and every $p_j(\\nabla)$ is real-valued,\neven if $\\nabla$ is not metric-compatible. If $\\nabla$ is compatible with a\nEuclidean metric, each odd Chern form $c_{2j+1}(\\nabla_{\\mathbb C})$ vanishes\npointwise. Assume full Axiom of Choice (AC); for every real connection each\n$c_{2j+1}(\\nabla_{\\mathbb C})$ is then exact, with AC used through existence\nof a metric-compatible connection and the transgression lemma.\n\nIf $E$ is an oriented Euclidean bundle of even rank $2m$ and $\\nabla$ is\nmetric-compatible, define\n$$e(\\nabla)=\\operatorname{Pf}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Omega}{2\\pi}\\right),$$\nwhere the Pfaffian uses the ordered oriented orthonormal frame and\n$\\operatorname{Pf}\\!\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&a\\\\-a&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)=a$.\nThese curvature evaluations are closed forms. In rank zero,\n$c(\\nabla)=p(\\nabla)=e(\\nabla)=1$. The Euler form is defined here only for\noriented even-rank Euclidean bundles with a metric-compatible connection.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
