      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Construct the required Clifford module explicitly. Put $V_0^+=\\Lambda^1T^*X\\otimes\\mathbb C$ and $V_0^-=(\\Lambda^0\\oplus\\Lambda^-)T^*X\\otimes\\mathbb C$, with their metric Hermitian forms. For a real covector $\\xi$ define $B_\\xi a=(-\\iota_{\\xi^\\sharp}a,\\sqrt2\\,\\pi_-(\\xi\\wedge a))$ and $c(\\xi)(a,b)=(-B_\\xi^*b,B_\\xi a)$ on $V_0^+\\oplus V_0^-$. In an oriented orthonormal frame with $\\xi=te^1$, the scalar part of $B_\\xi a$ has squared norm $t^2|a_1|^2$, while the three forms $\\sqrt2\\pi_-(e^1\\wedge e^j)$, $j=2,3,4$, are orthonormal. Hence $B_\\xi^*B_\\xi=|\\xi|^2I$. Both spaces have dimension four, so for $\\xi\\ne0$ also $B_\\xi B_\\xi^*=|\\xi|^2I$; the zero case is immediate. Polarization gives the Clifford relation $c(\\xi)c(\\eta)+c(\\eta)c(\\xi)=-2\\langle\\xi,\\eta\\rangle I$, and the displayed block matrix is skew-Hermitian and odd. Wedge, contraction and $\\pi_-$ are parallel for the Levi–Civita connection, so the induced connection is Clifford-compatible. Its chiral Dirac operator is exactly $\\widetilde D_0=d^*\\oplus\\sqrt2d^-$. This is an actual geometric Clifford module, not a postulated Spin factorization.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $E=P\\times_{SU(2)}\\mathbb C^2$ and $G=\\operatorname{ad}P\\otimes\\mathbb C$, with their induced unitary connections. The bundle $G$ is the trace-zero endomorphism bundle of $E$. Set $Z=-F_E/(2\\pi i)$, a traceless two-by-two matrix of commuting two-forms. The determinant convention gives $c_2(E)=-\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}(Z^2)$ as a form. For a traceless diagonal matrix with entries $u,-u$, its commutator action on trace-zero endomorphisms has eigenvalues $0,2u,-2u$. Therefore $\\operatorname{tr}(\\operatorname{ad}Z)=0$ and $\\operatorname{tr}((\\operatorname{ad}Z)^2)=4\\operatorname{tr}(Z^2)$. These are polynomial identities for all traceless complex matrices: matrices with distinct eigenvalues are dense, and continuity gives the rest. Polarizing the degree-two identity gives the same identity for matrices of two-forms. It follows, through degree four, that $\\operatorname{ch}(G)=3-4c_2(E)$, with no degree-two term. This fixes the factor and sign directly from the stated Chern normalization.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix its chirality sign and multiplicity. Write $c_j=c(e^j)$. Applying $c_4c_3c_2c_1$ to $e^1\\in V_0^+$ gives successively $(-1,0)$, $-e^2$, $(0,(e^{23}-e^{14})/\\sqrt2)$, and $-e^1$. Reversal of four distinct generators has positive sign, so $c_1c_2c_3c_4e^1=-e^1$. The construction is $SO(4)$-equivariant, and oriented rotations carry $e^1$ to every unit one-form; therefore $-c_1c_2c_3c_4=I$ on $V_0^+$. This volume operator anticommutes with every $c_j$; since $c_1$ identifies the two summands, it is $-I$ on $V_0^-$. Thus the given grading IS the reference chirality $i^2c_1c_2c_3c_4$. In the local factorization in [F1], $\\Gamma_{V_0}=\\Gamma_S\\otimes\\Gamma_W$ consequently forces $\\Gamma_W=I$. The local multiplicity has rank $8/4=2$ and is entirely even. If $\\Theta=\\operatorname{ch}(V_0/S)$, then $\\Theta_0=2$. This conclusion patches without choosing a global spinor bundle.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
