          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Calibrate the untwisted index by harmonic forms, rather than import an Euler/signature index formula. On a closed manifold, $\\int_Xda\\wedge da=\\int_Xd(a\\wedge da)=0$. Since $\\alpha\\wedge\\alpha=(|\\alpha^+|^2-|\\alpha^-|^2)d\\mathrm{vol}$ for real two-forms, polarization and complexification give $2\\|d^-a\\|_2^2=\\|da\\|_2^2$. Hence the smooth kernel of $\\widetilde D_0$ is exactly the harmonic one-forms. Its formal adjoint is $(f,\\omega)\\mapsto df+\\sqrt2d^*\\omega$, with $\\omega$ anti-self-dual. Pairing this equation with $df$ gives $\\|df\\|_2^2=0$, because $\\langle d^*\\omega,df\\rangle=\\langle\\omega,d(df)\\rangle=0$. Thus $f$ is constant and $d^*\\omega=0$. For an anti-self-dual two-form the latter is equivalent to $d\\omega=0$, so $\\omega$ is harmonic. The Dirac regularity in [F1] identifies the Hilbert kernel and cokernel with these smooth spaces. On harmonic two-forms the Hodge star preserves the space and splits the intersection form into positive and negative definite summands: its value on $\\omega^++\\omega^-$ is $\\|\\omega^+\\|_2^2-\\|\\omega^-\\|_2^2$. By [F2] their negative dimension is precisely $b_-(X)$. Consequently $\\operatorname{ind}_{\\mathbb C}\\widetilde D_0=b_1(X)-1-b_-(X)$.",
