        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Multiplication of the target summand $\\Lambda^-\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P$ by $\\sqrt2$ is a bounded invertible map on $L^2$, so $\\widetilde D_A$ and $D_A$ have the same kernel and isomorphic cokernels on their exact first-Sobolev domains. The complex operator just computed is the complexification of the real one. If a complexified equation holds, its real and imaginary parts satisfy the real equation; hence its kernel is the complexification of the real kernel, and the same holds for the orthogonal cokernel using the adjoint. Finite complex dimensions of these spaces equal the corresponding real dimensions. Closed range transfers to the real operator by restricting the complex estimate, or by taking real parts of convergent-image preimages in the kernel complement. Thus $D_A:H^1\\to L^2$ is real Fredholm and has index $8k-3(1-b_1+b_-)$, with $k=-\\langle c_2(P),[X]\\rangle$. This is not a claim about a KO-index theorem.",
