          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. There are constants $\\eta,\\delta>0$ with the following property. Let $g$ be a smooth Riemannian metric on the Euclidean unit four-ball with $\\|g-I\\|_{C^0}<\\eta$. Let $a\\in W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(B_1;\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\otimes\\mathbb R^4)$ satisfy the EUCLIDEAN Coulomb condition $d_e^*a=0$ and either $F_a^{+,g}=0$ or $F_a^{-,g}=0$ distributionally. Then $a$ has a smooth representative in $B_1$.\n\nThere is also a uniform interior conclusion. If a family of such metrics is bounded in every fixed $C^j$ norm on compact subballs and the forms satisfy $\\|a\\|_{L^4(B_1)}\\le\\delta$, the forms are bounded in every fixed $C^j$ norm on each compact subball, by constants depending only on that subball and the corresponding finite metric bounds. No initial bound on higher derivatives of $a$ is required.\n\nThere are also $\\epsilon>0$ and constants $C_M$ such that every smooth connection $A$ near $\\overline B_1$ with $F_A^{\\pm,g}=0$, $\\|F_A\\|_{L^2(B_1)}\\le\\epsilon$, and $\\|g\\|_{C^1(B_1)}\\le M$ satisfies $\\|F_A\\|_{L^\\infty(B_{1/2})}\\le C_M\\|F_A\\|_{L^2(B_1)}$. Curvature norms in this assertion are Euclidean coefficient norms; $\\epsilon$ is independent of $A$. On a ball of radius $r$ the corresponding bound is $r^2\\|F_A\\|_\\infty\\le C_M\\|F_A\\|_2$, with $M$ a bound for the rescaled metric.\n\nConsequently the assertion applies in sufficiently small normal-coordinate balls for ANY smooth Riemannian four-manifold, after dilation, whenever its small-curvature construction supplies the Euclidean Coulomb gauge in those coordinates. It supplies local regularity and interior estimates in that given gauge, not point removal, charge quantization or global compactness.",
