          "claim": "Fix finitely many positive coordinate-ball charts with compactly contained working subcharts covering $X$. A positive linear change makes the metric Euclidean at each chart center; shrink the charts so its coefficient matrix is within [F2]'s fixed small $C^0$ neighborhood throughout. For each sequence member, a frame at a chart center extends over that ball by radial parallel transport. This exists intrinsically: the ordered matrix integral series constructs transport in each bundle chart; finite subdivision of each compact path and uniqueness patch those solutions, and smooth parameter dependence makes the endpoint frame smooth. It is smooth at the center by the same local radial equation there. Thus the coordinate-ball trivializations can be chosen even for a sequence of different bundles; no common topological trivialization is being assumed. Metric energy and Euclidean coefficient energy are uniformly comparable on this finite list. List the coordinate balls with rational centers and radii whose quadrupled closures are in a chart. By successive bounded real subsequence extraction and one diagonal, arrange that the energy integral on every quadrupled listed ball has a limit. Choose $\\theta>0$ so energy less than $2\\theta$ on such a ball implies the Euclidean curvature smallness in [F1] and the resulting potential smallness in [F2], after dilation. Let $S$ be the points not lying in any listed ball whose quadrupled-ball limiting energy is less than $\\theta$. Its complement is open. If $N$ distinct points lie in $S$, choose $N$ listed balls containing them with pairwise disjoint quadrupled closures; this is possible by the coordinate basis and finitely many positive distances. Each limiting energy is at least $\\theta$, so $N\\theta\\le M$. Thus $S$ is finite. For every compact set in its complement finitely many good balls suffice, and all their required smallness bounds hold eventually.",
