        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-intersection-lattice-of-a-four-manifold-with-boundary",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $W$ be a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold whose boundary $M$ is a rational homology three-sphere, meaning $H_*(M;\\mathbb Q)\\cong H_*(S^3;\\mathbb Q)$. The boundary intersection lattice has underlying group $L_W=H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)/\\operatorname{Tor}H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$. Poincaré–Lefschetz duality identifies each $x\\in H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$ with a class $\\alpha_x\\in H^2(W,M;\\mathbb Z)$. Define $Q_W(\\bar x,\\bar y)=\\langle\\alpha_x\\smile\\alpha_y,[W,M]\\rangle$, where $\\smile$ is the relative cup product and $[W,M]\\in H_4(W,M;\\mathbb Z)$ is the relative orientation class; equivalently, this is the signed intersection number of interior two-cycle representatives. Its rank is $b_2(W)=\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}H_2(W;\\mathbb Q)$. A nondegenerate positive-definite $Q_W$ is called a positive-definite filling lattice. It is not required to be unimodular; the boundary linking form can make its determinant have absolute value greater than one.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The collar of $M$ lets every absolute two-cycle be moved into $\\operatorname{int}W$. The signed transverse intersection number is integer-valued and bilinear. Since $H_2(M;\\mathbb Q)=0$, [F1] shows that $H_2(W;\\mathbb Q)\\to H_2(W,M;\\mathbb Q)$ is an isomorphism.",
          "inputs": [
