        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-donaldson-reducible-ends-and-one-instanton-compactification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $X$ be a closed simply connected oriented smooth Riemannian four-manifold with positive-definite intersection form, and let $P\\to X$ be an $SU(2)$ bundle with $c_2(P)[X]=-1$. Use the unbased $H^3/H^4$ quotient and the determinant-line orientation. There is a compatible perturbation procedure as follows: prepare the finitely many reducible circle-slice obstruction maps by arbitrarily small equivariant finite-rank smoothing perturbations, and make a further generic bounded smoothing perturbation on finitely many bounded irreducible slice collars in a middle region. The procedure is fixed near the prepared reducibles and vanishes on the entire small-instanton end. The further choices may be made arbitrarily small; they form a relatively open dense transverse class on the prescribed smaller middle collars.\n\nThe resulting regular irreducible zero set is an oriented five-manifold. Its ideal completion is compact, has one small-instanton boundary stratum identified with $X$, and has exactly the finitely many prepared reducible cone points, with no other strata. A neighborhood of each cone point is a cone on $\\mathbb{CP}^2$. Delete smaller open cone neighborhoods and add the small-instanton boundary. The result is a smooth compact oriented five-manifold with boundary $X$ and one projective-plane link for each actual reducible orbit, with their induced orientations. Each projective link has one of the two complex-orientation signs; no common sign or numerical spectral-flow computation is asserted. The orientation on the component with the small-instanton end can be chosen so that this boundary is $X$ with its given orientation. The number of projective links is at most $b_2(X)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-reducible-charge-one-su-two-connections-admit-controlled-projective-links",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $X$ be a closed simply connected oriented smooth Riemannian four-manifold with positive-definite intersection form, and $E$ a Hermitian rank-two bundle with fixed unit determinant and $c_2(E)[X]=-1$. Use the equation $F_A^-=0$ and the unbased $H^3/H^4$ configuration quotient. The actual smooth reducible solution orbits are finite. Sending such a solution to its pair of parallel eigenlines gives an injection into the unordered pairs $\\{x,-x\\}$ in $H^2(X;\\mathbb Z)$ with $x^2[X]=1$. Distinct pairs arising in this way are orthogonal, so there are at most $b_2(X)$ reducible solution orbits.\n\nAt each such orbit there are invariant Coulomb-slice coordinates with effective circle stabilizer. The finite obstruction map is a smooth circle-equivariant map $\\phi:\\mathbb C^{3+p}\\to\\mathbb C^p$, with $\\phi(0)=D\\phi(0)=0$ and scalar circle action of the same nonzero weight on both spaces. Arbitrarily small $C^1(H^3,H^2)$, bounded $H^3$-valued, equivariant perturbations supported near that orbit can be chosen so that their zero set near it, before taking the circle quotient, is equivariantly diffeomorphic to a neighborhood of $0$ in $\\mathbb C^3$. After quotient it is a cone on $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, with smooth irreducible punctured part. The finitely many constructions may have disjoint supports.\n\nDeleting a smaller cone neighborhood leaves a smooth boundary link diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb{CP}^2$. An orientation of the surrounding regular moduli manifold induces one of its two orientations; the result does not assert a common sign for distinct links. This is a local preparation operation and does not assert a small-instanton end or a global compactification.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-topological-five-bordisms-preserve-ordinary-and-l2-signatures",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $Y$ be a compact oriented Hausdorff topological five-manifold with supplied boundary collar, and let $X=\\partial Y$ have its boundary orientation. Supply a based homomorphism $\\psi:\\pi_1Y\\to G$ on each component, where $G$ is an arbitrary discrete group, with the standard unitary regular coefficient systems. Then\n$$\\operatorname{sign}^{(2)}_G(X,\\psi|_X)=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{sign}(X)=0.$$\nIn particular, when $X=X_1\\sqcup(-X_0)$, the ordinary and $L^2$ signatures of these closed oriented four-manifolds agree across this actual coefficient-extending TOP bordism. The equality is unchanged under embedding the target group in a larger group. This theorem asserts bordism invariance and does not assert existence of a bordism between arbitrary closed coefficient manifolds.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-integral-cohomology-ring-of-complex-projective-space-by-splitting",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For every $N\\geq0$ identify\n$\\mathbb{CP}^N=\\operatorname{Gr}_1(\\mathbb C^{N+1})$, the space of complex\nlines in $\\mathbb C^{N+1}$, and let $\\gamma$ be the tautological complex line\nwith $x=e(\\gamma_{\\mathbb R})\\in H^2(\\mathbb{CP}^N;\\mathbb Z)$ its Euler class\nin the complex orientation. Then, as a graded ring,\n$$H^*(\\mathbb{CP}^N;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z[x]/(x^{N+1}),$$\nthe class $x$ generating each even degree and the odd groups vanishing; for\n$0\\leq m\\leq N$ the standard inclusion\n$\\mathbb{CP}^m\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb{CP}^N$, induced by\n$\\mathbb C^{m+1}\\subseteq\\mathbb C^{N+1}$, pulls $x$ back to $x$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
