    "lem-slice-branched-cover-produces-definite-four-manifold": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-double-cover-branched-over-a-slice-disk-is-a-rational-homology-ball",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. If $D\\subset B^4$ is a smooth proper embedded disk, the connected double cover $W\\to B^4$ branched along $D$ is a compact connected oriented smooth $4$-manifold with $H_i(W;\\mathbb F_2)=0$ for $i>0$, hence $H_i(W;\\mathbb Q)=0$ for $i>0$. Its boundary is the double cover of $S^3$ branched over $\\partial D$.\n\nHere a proper embedded disk means a smooth embedding of the closed disk $D^2$ whose interior lies in the interior of $B^4$ and whose boundary circle lies in $S^3=\\partial B^4$; the embedding is taken neat, so it meets $S^3$ transversely along $\\partial D$ and carries a boundary collar. All homology below is singular homology.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cyclic-branched-cover-of-a-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented $3$-sphere, choose its compact exterior $E_K$ with boundary torus $T_K$, and let $\\mu$ and $\\lambda$ be its positive meridian and preferred longitude. The exterior homology theorem gives $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z[\\mu]$. Let $\\phi:\\pi_1(E_K)\\to\\mathbb Z$ be the composite of abelianization with the homomorphism $[\\mu]\\mapsto1$. For an integer $q\\ge2$, let $\\phi_q$ be the composite $\\pi_1(E_K)\\xrightarrow{\\phi}\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb Z/q\\mathbb Z$, and let $p_q:E_q\\to E_K$ be the connected cover classified by $\\ker\\phi_q$. On $T_K$, the homomorphism sends $[\\mu]\\mapsto1$ and $[\\lambda]\\mapsto0$, so the preimage of $T_K$ is a connected torus cover whose fundamental group corresponds to $\\langle\\mu^q,\\lambda\\rangle$. Let $\\widetilde\\mu$ be the closed lift of $\\mu^q$. Attach a solid torus to $E_q$ with its meridian identified with $\\widetilde\\mu$; the resulting oriented closed $3$-manifold is the $q$-fold cyclic branched cover $\\Sigma_q(K)$. In tubular coordinates near $K$, the extended map to $S^3$ has local form $(s,w)\\mapsto(s,w^q)$, and the deck group $\\mathbb Z/q\\mathbb Z$ acts by $w\\mapsto e^{2\\pi i/q}w$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-smooth-and-topological-slice-genus",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere and let $B^4$ be the standard four-ball with boundary $S^3$. Let $\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{sm}}(K)$ be the set of genera of connected compact orientable smooth properly embedded surfaces $F\\subset B^4$ with oriented boundary $K$. Let $\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{top}}(K)$ be the set of genera of connected compact orientable locally flat topologically properly embedded surfaces $F\\subset B^4$ with oriented boundary $K$. For each such surface, its genus $g(F)$ is the nonnegative integer determined by $\\chi(F)=1-2g(F)$. Define the smooth slice genus and the locally flat topological slice genus by\n\n$$\ng_4^{\\mathrm{sm}}(K):=\\min\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{sm}}(K),\\qquad g_4^{\\mathrm{top}}(K):=\\min\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{top}}(K).\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-intersection-lattice-of-a-four-manifold-with-boundary",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $W$ be a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold whose boundary $M$ is a rational homology three-sphere, meaning $H_*(M;\\mathbb Q)\\cong H_*(S^3;\\mathbb Q)$. The boundary intersection lattice has underlying group $L_W=H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)/\\operatorname{Tor}H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$. Poincaré–Lefschetz duality identifies each $x\\in H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$ with a class $\\alpha_x\\in H^2(W,M;\\mathbb Z)$. Define $Q_W(\\bar x,\\bar y)=\\langle\\alpha_x\\smile\\alpha_y,[W,M]\\rangle$, where $\\smile$ is the relative cup product and $[W,M]\\in H_4(W,M;\\mathbb Z)$ is the relative orientation class; equivalently, this is the signed intersection number of interior two-cycle representatives. Its rank is $b_2(W)=\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}H_2(W;\\mathbb Q)$. A nondegenerate positive-definite $Q_W$ is called a positive-definite filling lattice. It is not required to be unimodular; the boundary linking form can make its determinant have absolute value greater than one.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-boundary-intersection-pairing-well-defined-and-nondegenerate",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $W$ be a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold with rational-homology-sphere boundary $M$. The signed intersection pairing on interior two-cycles descends to a symmetric integer-valued bilinear form on $H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)/\\operatorname{Tor}$, this group is free of finite rank, and the form is nondegenerate after extending scalars to $\\mathbb Q$. The form need not be unimodular.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=U\\cup V$, where $U$ and $V$ are open path-connected subsets of $X$, let $U\\cap V$ be path-connected, and fix $x_0\\in U\\cap V$. For the inclusion-induced maps in the diagram\n\n$$\\pi_1(U,x_0)\\longleftarrow\\pi_1(U\\cap V,x_0)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(V,x_0),$$\n\nthe group $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$, together with the two inclusion-induced homomorphisms, is a pushout ([[def-pushout-of-group-homomorphisms]]). Equivalently, the canonical homomorphism from any pushout $P$ of the displayed diagram to $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ is an isomorphism. No injectivity of either map from the overlap group is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-mayer-vietoris-sequence-in-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an open cover $X=U\\cup V$, the sequence\n$$\\cdots\\to H_n(U\\cap V;G)\\to H_n(U;G)\\oplus H_n(V;G)\\to H_n(X;G)\\xrightarrow{\\delta}H_{n-1}(U\\cap V;G)\\to\\cdots$$\nis exact, with middle map $(u,v)\\mapsto u+v$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-collar-neighborhood-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Every smooth manifold with boundary has a smooth collar.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-rationalization-is-exact-and-commutes-with-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For every abelian group A, A⊗_Z Q is its positive-integer localization: every element is a/s and a/s=0 iff a is killed by some positive integer. Thus A⊗Q=0 iff A is torsion. Rationalization is exact and commutes with direct sums. Naturally for every space Y, H_j(Y;Z)⊗Q≅H_j(Y;Q); for every rational vector space V, H_j(Y;V)≅H_j(Y;Q)⊗_Q V.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
