        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every natural number $n\\ge2$, the unit sphere $S^n\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ is simply connected ([[def-simply-connected]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-universal-covering-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **universal covering space** of $B$ is a covering map $p:\\widetilde B\\to B$ whose total space $\\widetilde B$ is simply connected ([[def-covering-map-and-evenly-covered-neighbourhoods]], [[def-simply-connected]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-deck-group-of-a-universal-cover-is-the-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a path-connected, locally path-connected, semilocally simply connected base $B$, the deck group of a universal cover is isomorphic to $\\pi_1(B,b_0)$. With the library's traversal-order path product the monodromy action is a right action, and the assignment carrying a loop class to the deck transformation that moves the chosen point of the fibre to the corresponding lifted endpoint is itself an isomorphism; no path reversal is inserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "prop-the-first-hurewicz-map-in-degree-one-is-abelianization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every path-connected topological space $X$ and $x_0\\in X$, the Hurewicz map\n$$h:\\pi_1(X,x_0)\\longrightarrow H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$$\nis surjective and has kernel $[\\pi_1(X,x_0),\\pi_1(X,x_0)]$. Hence it induces a natural isomorphism\n$$\\pi_1(X,x_0)^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\cong H_1(X;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe proof is choice-free and requires no CW, separation, local path-connectivity or local simple-connectivity assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
