          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-top-alexander-grading-of-knot-floer-homology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $Y$ be a closed connected oriented $3$-manifold, let $K\\subset Y$ be a smooth oriented null-homologous knot, and let $F\\subset Y$ be a compact connected oriented embedded surface with oriented boundary $K$. Choose a closed tubular neighborhood $N(K)$, put $M=Y\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$, and let $\\gamma_\\mu$ be the union of two disjoint annuli on $\\partial M$ whose core sutures are parallel meridians of $K$ with opposite orientations. Delete a collar of $\\partial F$ to regard $F$ as a properly embedded surface in $(M,\\gamma_\\mu)$, with relative class $[F]\\in H_2(M,\\partial M;\\mathbb Z)$. Let $t_\\mu$ be the meridional boundary trivialization of the oriented plane field used in the relative Spin-c definition. For $\\mathfrak{s}\\in\\operatorname{Spin}^c(M,\\gamma_\\mu)$, its Alexander grading is\n\n$$A_F(\\mathfrak{s})=\\frac12\\left\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak{s},t_\\mu),[F]\\right\\rangle,$$\n\nwith the standard sign convention that gives the usual Alexander grading for knots in $S^3$. For a balanced admissible sutured Heegaard diagram of $(M,\\gamma_\\mu)$, the Spin-c summand $SFH(M,\\gamma_\\mu,\\mathfrak{s};\\mathbb Z)$ is the homology of the integral sutured Floer disk-count complex generated by the Heegaard generators assigned to $\\mathfrak{s}$. Define the integral knot Floer group in Alexander grading $i$ by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathrm{HFK}}(Y,K,[F],i;\\mathbb Z)=\\bigoplus_{\\substack{\\mathfrak{s}\\in\\operatorname{Spin}^c(M,\\gamma_\\mu)\\\\ A_F(\\mathfrak{s})=i}}SFH(M,\\gamma_\\mu,\\mathfrak{s};\\mathbb Z).$$\n\nThis fixes the integral coefficient convention. The equivalent associated-graded closed Heegaard-complex presentation, invariance under changing diagrams, and comparison with the mod-two grid complex are separate theorems; this definition supplies none of their proofs or a coefficient-change map.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-fiber-outer-grading-and-integral-cut-euler-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the actual marked Seifert surface of genus g, oriented as minus the fiber for the cut, an adapted generator x has Alexander degree g minus the number of its interior polygon coordinates. Thus the top degree-g generators are exactly the outer generators and correspond to the cut diagram generators with their ordered intersection signs. If the integral top homology is one copy of the integers, the actual cut incidence matrix is unimodular, the cut is an integral homology product, and its periodic-domain group vanishes.",
          "uses": [
