    "thm-fibered-knot-has-rank-one-top-knot-floer-homology": {
      "authoring_status": "complete",
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-marked-fiber-admits-a-sutured-surface-diagram",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let a null-homologous knot in a closed connected oriented three-manifold be fibered with a specified connected oriented Seifert fiber. Its compact exterior with two meridional sutures admits a good surface-adapted balanced diagram carrying that actual Seifert surface, its boundary longitude, and the meridional boundary trivialization. For a fiber of an actual open book the cut/double diagram represents the marked product obtained by cutting along the fiber, with the original monodromy retained in regluing.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-permissible-surface-niceification-preserves-admissibility",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "A good admissible surface-adapted balanced diagram can be made nice by a finite sequence of permissible fingers and own-family disk slides preserving its surface marking. Weak admissibility is preserved. Every complementary region of the underlying diagram disjoint from its true boundary is a bigon or square.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-relative-sutured-diagrams-have-signed-move-equivalences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Two balanced Heegaard diagrams of the same marked sutured manifold are connected by relative attaching-circle isotopies, own-family disk slides, and ordinary stabilizations and destabilizations. An ordinary stabilization can be moved by such slides to the true boundary region. These moves induce integral chain homotopy equivalences for the transported coherent orientation class. The product sutured manifold has integral homology equal to one copy of the integers; on a homology-product branch the periodic-domain character ambiguity vanishes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-fiber-outer-grading-and-integral-cut-euler-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the actual marked Seifert surface of genus g, oriented as minus the fiber for the cut, an adapted generator x has Alexander degree g minus the number of its interior polygon coordinates. Thus the top degree-g generators are exactly the outer generators and correspond to the cut diagram generators with their ordered intersection signs. If the integral top homology is one copy of the integers, the actual cut incidence matrix is unimodular, the cut is an integral homology product, and its periodic-domain group vanishes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-signed-outer-summand-cut-comparison-with-zero-periodic-cut",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an actual nice admissible good surface diagram whose cut has zero periodic-domain group, the outer differential and the cut differential are identified over the integers after changing generator signs. This identifies the top Alexander homology with the integral homology of the cut diagram when the surface is the marked Seifert surface and the outer degree is its genus. The conclusion applies in particular to a fiber cut and to the rank-one branch of the cut Euler-matrix reduction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the actual binding bridge and drilling construction to represent the exterior with two meridional sutures, carrying the actual page longitude and the class of F. The adapted surface cut is literally the result of cutting this mapping torus along its fiber, hence is F times an interval with its product sutures. Apply the finite permissible niceification. Signed relative diagram moves compare the resulting complex to the chosen defining complex, with its specified coherent orientation class transported. No restriction on Y or change of coefficient ring is made.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "In this marked adapted diagram the local Euler obstruction is minus 2g plus twice the number of interior polygon coordinates when evaluated on minus F. Therefore the Alexander degree is g minus that number: degree g is precisely the outer generator set, and no higher degree occurs. The product cut has zero periodic-domain group, either directly from its empty product diagram and the relative handle description, or from its vanishing relative integral homology. The signed cut comparison consequently identifies the whole degree-g differential with the cut differential, after harmless changes of generator signs.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "The empty diagram of the actual product has one empty-tuple generator and no nonconstant disk, so its integral chain complex is Z with zero differential. The signed relative move equivalences identify its homology with that of the actual cut diagram. Combining with the full top-summand chain identification gives the asserted integral group isomorphism. The exterior orientation character restricts trivially to the zero-periodic cut, so this conclusion holds for the specified defining class without asserting equivalence of arbitrary orientation systems on the uncut exterior.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
