    "lem-essential-curves-on-torus-with-same-primitive-class-are-isotopic": {
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since $v$ is primitive, Bezout's identity gives $w\\in H_1(T;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\det(v,w)=1$. The linear torus diffeomorphism taking the basis $(v,w)$ to the standard basis takes both curves to curves of class $(1,0)$. It is enough to prove the claim for that class.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Put the two curves in transverse position. If they intersect, lift them to the annular cover $A=\\mathbb R^2/\\langle(1,0)\\rangle$. Each lift is an essential embedded circle, and the deck translates of each one are disjoint. In the universal cover of $A$, a lift of either circle is a proper line invariant under horizontal unit translation and contained in a bounded horizontal strip; all such lines have the same two ends. The signed intersection sum in a fundamental period is zero because both curves have class $(1,0)$. Thus intersections have both signs. Two successive intersections of opposite sign cut off a bounded region between subarcs of the two lines. Choose among such regions one with the least number of interior intersections and, among those, least horizontal width modulo deck translation. If a lifted arc entered the region, its first entry and exit would cut off a region with fewer interior intersections; if a nontrivial deck translate met it, the overlap would yield a region of smaller horizontal width. Hence its interior is disjoint from all lifted arcs and translates, and its projection is an embedded bigon in $T$. Isotoping one arc across the bigon removes two intersections and creates none. Compactness and transversality give finitely many intersections, so finitely many such isotopies make the curves disjoint.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
