          "source": "def-mirror-of-a-classical-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the orientation-reversing involution $r:S^3\\to S^3$ given by reflection across a fixed equatorial two-sphere containing a chosen standard unknot $U$. For a smooth knot $K\\subset S^3$, its mirror is $\\overline K:=r(K)$, with the orientation transported by $r$. A diagram of the mirror can be obtained by keeping the projected shadow fixed and interchanging the over- and under-crossing data at every crossing; equivalently, one may reflect the planar shadow while retaining the crossing data. These are alternative mirror conventions, not operations to perform together. Since $r^2=\\mathrm{id}$ and $r(U)=U$, mirroring twice returns $K$, and $K$ is the unknot exactly when $\\overline K$ is.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-reduced-khovanov-basepoint-and-reidemeister-invariance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $K$ be an oriented knot and let $(D,p)$ and $(D',p')$ be finite regular oriented diagrams of $K$ with basepoints away from all crossings. Then the reduced integral groups $\\widetilde{Kh}^{i,\\widetilde j}(D,p)$ and $\\widetilde{Kh}^{i,\\widetilde j}(D',p')$ are isomorphic for every $(i,\\widetilde j)$. In particular, they are invariant under Reidemeister moves supported away from the basepoint and under transport of the basepoint along the knot.",
