          "claim": "**Construction for all mean-zero Lp data.** Extend $f$ by zero to $\\mathbb R^4$, approximate in $L^p$ by smooth functions using [F4], and restrict to the closed ball. Each approximant is continuous there and hence uniformly approximable by polynomials by [F4]. A choice of uniform error tending to zero gives polynomial approximants in $L^p$; subtract their means to obtain mean-zero $f_j\\to f$ in $L^p$, since $\\|(f_j-f)_{B_1}\\|\\,|B_1|^{1/p}\\le\\|f_j-f\\|_p$ before normalization. Let $u_j$ be the polynomial solutions from [F1]. Applying step 4.1 to their differences makes all derivative components through order two Cauchy in $L^p$. Completeness [F4] and passage in the weak-derivative test identities give a limit $u\\in W^{2,p}$ with $u_j\\to u$ strongly and the same bound. Finite volume and $p\\ge2$ imply $u_j\\to u$ in $W^{1,2}$ and $f_j\\to f$ in $L^2$; their weak Neumann identities pass to the limit for every $W^{1,2}$ test. Thus the solution is the unique $L^2$ Neumann solution of [F1], now with the stronger regularity and norm estimate.",
