        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-riemannian-metric-and-riemannian-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **Riemannian metric** on a Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold $M$ is a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor $g$ such that $g_p(v,v)>0$ for every point $p$ and every nonzero $v\\in T_pM$. A **Riemannian manifold** is the pair $(M,g)$.\n\nThis is a [[def-smooth-tensor-field]] giving a [[def-smooth-bundle-metric]] on $TM$. Dimension zero is allowed: its zero bilinear form is positive definite because there are no nonzero vectors. The empty manifold has its unique empty metric. Boundaries are allowed where stated, with smoothness understood up to the boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.2",
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-local-existence-uniqueness-and-smooth-dependence-for-manifold-integral-curves",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a smooth vector field on $M$ and let $p\\in M$. Then there exist\n$h>0$, an open neighbourhood $U$ of $p$, and a smooth map\n\n$$ \\Phi:(-h,h)\\times U\\to M $$\n\nsuch that for every $q\\in U$, the curve $t\\mapsto \\Phi(t,q)$ is the unique\nintegral curve of $X$ on $(-h,h)$ with initial value $\\Phi(0,q)=q$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Embed the segment in a collar extension across $\\partial M_0$ and cover it by finitely many coordinate neighborhoods. On an embedded subsegment, parallel transport an orthonormal normal frame and set $F(t,y)=\\exp_{\\gamma(t)}(\\sum_\\alpha y_\\alpha E_\\alpha(t))$. The inverse function theorem gives local Fermi coordinates with $g_{jk}(t,0)=\\delta_{jk}$ and $\\partial_i g_{jk}(t,0)=0$. This construction is along an interval and uses no orientation of $M_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "In one such chart, seek $v_s=e^{is\\Theta}a$, $s=\\tau+i\\lambda$, with $\\Theta(t,0)=t$ and $\\operatorname{Im}\\Theta(t,y)\\geq c|y|^2$. Expand $\\Theta$ into homogeneous transverse Taylor polynomials. The quadratic polynomial is $\\frac12H(t)y\\cdot y$; substituting into $g(d\\Theta,d\\Theta)=1$ gives the matrix Riccati equation $H'+H^2=F(t)$, where $F$ is determined by the second transverse Taylor coefficient of the inverse metric. An initial complex symmetric $H(t_0)$ with positive-definite imaginary part yields a smooth solution with positive imaginary part on a short subsegment. Each higher homogeneous phase term then solves a linear first-order ODE along $t$, because its newest coefficient occurs linearly in the eikonal equation.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Substituting $a=\\tau^{(m-1)/4}\\sum_{j=0}^N s^{-j}a_{-j}$ into $(-\\Delta_{g_0}-s^2)e^{is\\Theta}a$ cancels successive powers of $s$ by linear transport ODEs for the transverse Taylor coefficients of each $a_{-j}$. The leading coefficient solves $a_0(t,0)=c_0\\exp(-\\frac12\\int_{t_0}^t\\operatorname{tr}H(r)\\,dr)$. A finite Taylor realization to the order required for the fixed $K$ and a transverse cutoff produce a smooth local beam; Gaussian integration gives bounded $L^2$ norm and a residual that tends to zero faster than any prescribed power after increasing the Taylor order.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On an oriented transversal of dimension at least two, apply [F3] to complete the finite overlap matching and eliminate all distinct-branch cross terms. This gives the stated global family and limit; steps 1.1--2.1 describe its local ingredients, without claiming a new proof of the imported global estimates.",
