            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "For a nonorientable transversal let $\\pi:\\widetilde M_0\\to M_0$ be its orientation double cover, with deck involution $\\sigma$, and lift $\\gamma$ to $\\widetilde\\gamma$. Let $w_s$ be the oriented beam from [F3] along that lift. Define the descended function by $\\pi^*v_s=w_s+\\sigma^*w_s$. The residual and norm bounds follow by a finite sum and the covering integral formula. The two lifted segments have no common tangent branch: equality of position and velocity at different times would make the geodesic periodic, contrary to its nontangential boundary exit; opposite velocities at a repeated point would force a zero midpoint velocity by geodesic uniqueness. There are only finitely many crossing branches, by compactness and local uniqueness. The distinct-branch estimate in [F3], applied to these two lifts, therefore gives a zero mixed-term limit. For every continuous $\\psi$ the covering formula is\n$$\\int_{M_0}|v_s|^2\\psi\\,dV=\\int_{\\widetilde M_0}|w_s|^2(\\psi\\circ\\pi)\\,dV+\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\widetilde M_0}w_s\\overline{\\sigma^*w_s}(\\psi\\circ\\pi)\\,dV.$$\nThe first term has precisely the required ray limit and the second tends to zero. In particular there is no extra factor two in the normalization. The same argument works if the cover is disconnected.",
