          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-sobolev-spaces-on-compact-riemannian-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary, with no orientability assumption. Fix a finite smooth atlas of interior and boundary charts and a smooth partition of unity subordinate to it. For an integer $k\\geq0$, define $H^k(M)$ as the completion of $C^\\infty(M)$ in the sum of the Euclidean $W^{k,2}$ norms of the localized coordinate representatives; derivatives in a boundary chart are taken on the corresponding half-space. Define $H_0^1(M)$ as the closure of $C_c^\\infty(M^\\circ)$ in $H^1(M)$. Define $H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ by the fixed finite boundary atlas and partition of unity, using the Euclidean fractional Slobodeckij space $W^{1/2,2}(\\mathbb R^{n-1})$ for each localized representative. Define $H^{-1/2}(\\partial M)$ as the continuous dual of $H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ with the Riemannian boundary density pairing. These are the fixed-atlas norms used on this page; no atlas-independence assertion is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-conductivity-map-wellposedness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $(M,g)$ be a compact connected smooth Riemannian domain with nonempty smooth boundary and no orientability assumption, and let $\\gamma\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$ satisfy $\\gamma>0$. There is a constant $C_P>0$ such that $\\|w\\|_{L^2(M)}\\leq C_P\\|\\nabla_gw\\|_{L^2(M)}$ for every $w\\in H_0^1(M)$. For every $h\\in L^2(M)$ and $f\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ there is a unique $v\\in H^1(M)$ with trace $f$ satisfying $\\int_M\\gamma\\,g(\\nabla_gv,\\nabla_g\\overline z)\\,dV_g=-\\int_Mh\\overline z\\,dV_g$ for every $z\\in H_0^1(M)$. If $h$ and $f$ are smooth, this weak solution is smooth up to the boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-smooth-manifold-elliptic-nonresonant-regularity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $(M,g)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian domain with smooth boundary and no orientability assumption, let $q\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$, and suppose the only $w\\in H_0^1(M)$ satisfying $(-\\Delta_g+q)w=0$ weakly is $w=0$. For every $F\\in L^2(M)$ and $f\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ there is a unique weak solution $u\\in H^1(M)$ of $(-\\Delta_g+q)u=F$ with trace $f$. If $f=0$, $F\\in H^m(M)$ and $m\\geq0$ is an integer, then $u\\in H^{m+2}(M)$ and $\\|u\\|_{H^{m+2}}\\leq C_m\\|F\\|_{H^m}$. If $F$ and $f$ are smooth, this solution belongs to $H^m(M)$ for every integer $m\\geq0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-sobolev-algebra-above-the-critical-index",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega$ be a bounded $W^{k,p}$-extension domain, $k\\ge1$ and $1\\le p<\\infty$ with $kp>n$. Then there is $C(n,p,k,\\Omega)$ with\n$$\\|uv\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}\\|v\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}\\qquad(u,v\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega)),$$\nso $W^{k,p}(\\Omega)$ is a Banach algebra; the constants and the conclusion may depend on the choice of equivalent Sobolev norm only through $C$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega$ be a bounded $W^{k,p}$-extension domain, $k\\ge1$ and $1\\le p<\\infty$. Let $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and $u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. Then:\n\n1. if $kp<n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every $q$ with $\\frac1q\\ge\\frac1p-\\frac kn$ (equivalently $1\\le q\\le\\frac{np}{n-kp}$);\n2. if $kp=n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C(q)\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every finite $q$;\n3. if $kp>n$, then for every integer $m\\ge0$ and every $0<\\alpha<1$ with $m+\\alpha<k-\\frac np$ there is a representative in $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ with $\\|u\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$; when $k-\\frac np\\notin\\mathbb Z$ one may take $m=\\lfloor k-\\frac np\\rfloor$ and $\\alpha=k-\\frac np-m$.\n\nHere $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ uses the continuous derivatives of the constructed representative on an ambient neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$, with norm\n$$\\|g\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}:=\\sum_{|\\beta|\\le m}\\sup_{\\overline\\Omega}|D^\\beta g|+\\sum_{|\\beta|=m}\\sup_{\\substack{x,y\\in\\overline\\Omega\\\\x\\ne y}}\\frac{|D^\\beta g(x)-D^\\beta g(y)|}{|x-y|^\\alpha}.$$\nFor $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ set these norm values to $0$. The proof supplies such an ambient representative, so no boundary differentiability or regularity of $\\partial\\Omega$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-implicit-function-theorem-for-banach-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $X$, $Y$, $Z$ be real\nBanach spaces, let $U \\subseteq X \\times Y$ be open for the product metric, let\n$F : U \\to Z$ be of class $C^k$ with $k \\ge 1$\n([[def-c-k-map-between-banach-spaces]]), and let $(a,b) \\in U$ with\n$F(a,b) = 0$. Let $D_XF(a,b) := DF(a,b)\\circ i_X$ and\n$D_YF(a,b) := DF(a,b)\\circ i_Y$ be the partial derivatives, where\n$i_X(x) := (x,0)$ and $i_Y(y) := (0,y)$. If $D_YF(a,b) : Y \\to Z$ is a bounded\nlinear isomorphism, then there are open neighbourhoods $A \\subseteq X$ of $a$\nand $B \\subseteq Y$ of $b$ and a unique map $g : A \\to B$ of class $C^k$ such\nthat\n\n$$\\{\\,(x,y) \\in A \\times B : F(x,y) = 0\\,\\} = \\{\\,(x,g(x)) : x \\in A\\,\\},$$\n\nand along the graph $Dg$ satisfies\n$$Dg(x) = -\\,D_YF\\bigl(x,g(x)\\bigr)^{-1}\\,D_XF\\bigl(x,g(x)\\bigr) \\qquad (x \\in A).$$\nIn particular $g(a) = b$.",
