          "claim": "Let $v\\in H^1(M)$ be a weak conductivity solution and put $u=\\rho v$. Since $\\rho$ is smooth, the distributional Leibniz rule in charts gives\n$$\\operatorname{div}_g(\\rho^2\\nabla_gv)=\\rho^2\\Delta_gv+2\\rho\\,g(\\nabla_g\\rho,\\nabla_gv),\\qquad \\Delta_g(\\rho v)=\\rho\\Delta_gv+2g(\\nabla_g\\rho,\\nabla_gv)+v\\Delta_g\\rho.$$\nThe first expression is zero distributionally by the weak equation. Dividing by the smooth positive $\\rho$ in that identity and substituting into the second gives $\\Delta_g(\\rho v)=v\\Delta_g\\rho=q(\\rho v)$, hence $u\\in H^1(M)$ solves $(-\\Delta_g+q)u=0$ weakly. Equivalently, the same identity reads $\\operatorname{div}_g(\\rho^2\\nabla_g(u/\\rho))=\\rho(\\Delta_gu-qu)$, so the transformation also sends every weak Schrödinger solution in $H^1$ back to a weak conductivity solution. These distributional identities use only the divergence convention in [F4], and are independent of orientability.",
