          "claim": "Let $f,h\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$, let $v_f$ be the conductivity solution, and put $u=\\rho v_f$. Let $U_h$ be the Schrödinger solution with trace $\\rho_bh$; it exists by step 1.2 and the nonresonant solvability supplier. Then $U_h/\\rho$ is the conductivity solution with trace $h$ by step 1.1. Use these solutions as extensions in the weak DN pairings from [F1]. The weak product calculation, valid almost everywhere for $H^1$ functions and smooth $\\rho$, is\n$$\\rho^2g\\!\\left(\\nabla_g(u/\\rho),\\nabla_g(\\overline{U_h}/\\rho)\\right)=g(\\nabla_gu,\\nabla_g\\overline{U_h})+q\\,u\\overline{U_h}-\\operatorname{div}_g\\!\\left(u\\overline{U_h}\\nabla_g\\log\\rho\\right).$$\nHere $q=\\Delta_g\\log\\rho+|\\nabla_g\\log\\rho|_g^2$. The product $u\\overline{U_h}$ lies in $W^{1,1}(M)$. Approximate $u,U_h$ in $H^1$ by smooth functions using the completion defining $H^1$; their products converge in $W^{1,1}$, and their boundary products converge in $L^1$ by the trace bound in [F1]. Thus the smooth Gauss--Green formula passes to this product, whose trace is $(\\rho_bf)\\overline{(\\rho_bh)}$. Integrating therefore gives $\\langle\\Lambda_\\gamma f,h\\rangle=\\langle\\Lambda_q(\\rho_bf),\\rho_bh\\rangle-\\int_{\\partial M}\\rho_b(\\partial_{\\nu_{\\rm out}}\\rho)f\\overline h\\,dS_g$. By duality, multiplication of an $H^{-1/2}$ distribution by $\\rho_b$ is characterized by pairing against $\\rho_bh$, so this is exactly the asserted operator identity for arbitrary $H^{1/2}$ data.",
