          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-simple-surface-and-geodesic-xray-transform",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g)$ be a compact, connected, nonempty smooth two-dimensional Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary. For $x\\in\\partial M$, let $\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}(x)$ be the outward unit normal. The boundary is **strictly convex** if\n$$\ng(\\nabla_w\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}},w)>0\n$$\nfor every $x\\in\\partial M$ and every nonzero $w\\in T_x\\partial M$. A **Jacobi field** along a unit-speed geodesic $\\gamma$ is a smooth vector field $J$ along $\\gamma$ satisfying\n$$\nD_t^2J+R(J,\\dot\\gamma)\\dot\\gamma=0,\n$$\nwhere $R(X,Y)Z=\\nabla_X\\nabla_YZ-\\nabla_Y\\nabla_XZ-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z$. The manifold has **no conjugate points** if, on every unit-speed geodesic segment $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to M$, a Jacobi field with $J(a)=J(b)=0$ must be identically zero. It is **nontrapping** if every maximal unit-speed geodesic starting in $M^\\circ$ reaches $\\partial M$ in finite forward and backward time. The pair $(M,g)$ is a **simple surface** if its boundary is strictly convex, it has no conjugate points, and it is nontrapping.\n\nThe unit tangent bundle is $SM=\\{(x,v):x\\in M,\\ v\\in T_xM,\\ g_x(v,v)=1\\}$. Write $\\nu_{\\mathrm{in}}=-\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}$ and\n$$\n\\partial_+SM=\\{(x,v)\\in SM:x\\in\\partial M,\\ g_x(v,\\nu_{\\mathrm{in}})>0\\}\n$$\nfor inward-pointing unit boundary vectors. For $(x,v)\\in\\partial_+SM$, let $\\gamma_{x,v}$ be the unit-speed geodesic with $\\gamma_{x,v}(0)=x$ and $\\dot\\gamma_{x,v}(0)=v$, and let $\\tau_+(x,v)$ be its first positive exit time from $M$. For $f\\in C^\\infty(M)$, its scalar **geodesic X-ray transform** is the function on $\\partial_+SM$ defined by\n$$\nI_0f(x,v)=\\int_0^{\\tau_+(x,v)} f(\\gamma_{x,v}(t))\\,dt.\n$$\nThe geodesic equation is interpreted up to first boundary exit; local smooth extensions of the metric are used only to solve the coordinate ODE at boundary initial data.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-ip-cgo-solvability-and-remainder-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a transversally anisotropic product domain, let $q\\in C^\\infty(\\overline M,\\mathbb R)$, and let $\\psi\\in C^\\infty$ be real on a neighborhood of the transversal projection of $M$, with $|d\\psi|_{g_0}=1$. Suppose $a\\in C^\\infty(\\overline M,\\mathbb C)$ satisfies\n$$2\\partial_{x_1}a+2i\\,g_0(\\nabla_{g_0}\\psi,\\nabla_{g_0}a)+i(\\Delta_{g_0}\\psi)a=0.$$\nThen, for all sufficiently large $\\tau$, there is an exact solution\n$$u_\\tau=e^{-\\tau(x_1+i\\psi)}a+e^{-\\tau x_1}r_\\tau,\\qquad (-\\Delta_g+q)u_\\tau=0\\ \\text{in }M^\\circ,$$\nwith $r_\\tau\\in H^1(M)$ and $\\|r_\\tau\\|_{L^2(M)}\\leq C\\tau^{-1}\\|(-\\Delta_g+q)a\\|_{L^2(M)}$. No boundary value is prescribed for $r_\\tau$.",
          "uses": []
        }
