          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\geq3$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded smooth domain contained in a ball $B(0,R)$, and let $q\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega,\\mathbb C)$. There are constants $C>0$ and $\\tau_0\\geq1$, depending only on $n$, $R$, and $\\|q\\|_{L^\\infty}$, such that for every $\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C^n$ satisfying $\\zeta\\cdot\\zeta=0$ and $|\\zeta|\\geq\\tau_0$, there is $r_\\zeta\\in H^1(\\Omega,\\mathbb C)$ for which\n$$u_\\zeta(x)=e^{i\\zeta\\cdot x}(1+r_\\zeta(x))$$\nsolves $(-\\Delta+q)u_\\zeta=0$ distributionally in $\\Omega$, and\n$$\\|r_\\zeta\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\leq C|\\zeta|^{-1}\\|q\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)},\\qquad \\|\\nabla r_\\zeta\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\leq C\\|q\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}.$$\nThe dot product in the null-phase condition is complex bilinear, and $|\\zeta|=(\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\zeta_j|^2)^{1/2}$.",
