        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-cubic-dn-small-data-differentiability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a compact transversally anisotropic product domain of dimension $n\\geq3$ and let $q\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$. Fix an integer $s>n/2+2$ and a finite family $h_1,\\ldots,h_m\\in C^\\infty(\\partial M,\\mathbb C)$. For $\\epsilon=(\\epsilon_1,\\ldots,\\epsilon_m)\\in\\mathbb R^m$ sufficiently near zero, the equation\n$$-\\Delta_g u+q u^3=0\\quad\\text{in }M^\\circ,\\qquad u|_{\\partial M}=\\sum_{j=1}^m\\epsilon_jh_j$$\nhas a unique solution in a fixed small $H^s(M)$ ball. The parameter map $\\epsilon\\mapsto u_\\epsilon$ is $C^3$ into $H^s(M)$, and the corresponding nonlinear Dirichlet-to-Neumann data $\\partial_{\\nu_{\\rm out}}u_\\epsilon|_{\\partial M}$ is $C^3$ into $C^0(\\partial M)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-cubic-dn-third-linearization-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian domain with smooth boundary, let $q\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$, and let $\\Lambda_q^{\\rm NL}$ be the small-data nonlinear Dirichlet-to-Neumann map for $-\\Delta_g u+qu^3=0$. For smooth boundary directions $h_1,h_2,h_3,h_4$, let $v_j$ be the harmonic solution with boundary value $h_j$. If\n$$w_{123}=\\left.\\partial_{\\epsilon_1}\\partial_{\\epsilon_2}\\partial_{\\epsilon_3}u_\\epsilon\\right|_{\\epsilon=0},$$\nwhere $u_\\epsilon$ has boundary value $\\epsilon_1h_1+\\epsilon_2h_2+\\epsilon_3h_3$, then\n$$-\\Delta_gw_{123}+6qv_1v_2v_3=0,\\qquad w_{123}|_{\\partial M}=0,$$\nand\n$$\\left\\langle (D^3\\Lambda_q^{\\rm NL})_0[h_1,h_2,h_3],h_4\\right\\rangle =6\\int_Mq\\,v_1v_2v_3\\overline{v_4}\\,dV_g.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-intersecting-gaussian-beam-four-factor-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $M\\Subset\\mathbb R\\times M_0^\\circ$ be a three-dimensional transversally anisotropic product domain with compact connected simple surface transversal $(M_0,g_0)$. For every $p\\in M_0^\\circ$ choose two nontangential unit-speed geodesic segments $\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2$ meeting transversely at $p$ and nowhere else. For every $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R$ and every $Q\\in C^\\infty(\\overline M)$, there are four exact harmonic solutions $u_{\\tau,1}^+,u_{\\tau,1}^-,u_{\\tau,2}^+,u_{\\tau,2}^-\\in H^1(M)$, with no prescribed boundary values, such that\n$$\\lim_{\\tau\\to\\infty}\\int_MQ(x_1,x')u_{\\tau,1}^+u_{\\tau,1}^-u_{\\tau,2}^+u_{\\tau,2}^-\\,dV_g =c(p,\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)\\int_{I_p}e^{-i\\xi x_1}Q(x_1,p)\\,dx_1,$$\nwhere $I_p=\\{x_1\\in\\mathbb R:(x_1,p)\\in M^\\circ\\}$ and $c(p,\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2)>0$ is the nonzero Gaussian normalization divided by $|\\sin\\angle(\\dot\\gamma_1(p),\\dot\\gamma_2(p))|$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-the-l-one-fourier-transform",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. If $f,g\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$ and $\\widehat f=\\widehat g$, then $f=g$ almost everywhere. Equality of the transforms almost everywhere already suffices.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
