        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ip-injective-geodesic-ray-transform",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M_0,g_0)$ be a compact connected smooth Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary. A nontangential maximal geodesic segment is a unit-speed geodesic $\\gamma:[0,L]\\to M_0$, with $L>0$, whose initial velocity points strictly inward at $\\partial M_0$, whose terminal velocity points strictly outward at $\\partial M_0$, and whose points for $0<t<L$ lie in $M_0^\\circ$. For $f\\in C_c^\\infty(M_0^\\circ,\\mathbb R)$ define its geodesic ray transform by $I_{g_0}f(\\gamma)=\\int_0^L f(\\gamma(t))\\,dt$. The unweighted scalar ray transform is called injective on compactly supported smooth functions if $I_{g_0}f(\\gamma)=0$ for every such segment implies $f=0$. For complex-valued $f$, injectivity means injectivity of its real and imaginary parts separately.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-the-l-one-fourier-transform",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. If $f,g\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$ and $\\widehat f=\\widehat g$, then $f=g$ almost everywhere. Equality of the transforms almost everywhere already suffices.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-ip-cgo-solvability-and-remainder-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a transversally anisotropic product domain, let $q\\in C^\\infty(\\overline M,\\mathbb R)$, and let $\\psi\\in C^\\infty$ be real on a neighborhood of the transversal projection of $M$, with $|d\\psi|_{g_0}=1$. Suppose $a\\in C^\\infty(\\overline M,\\mathbb C)$ satisfies\n$$2\\partial_{x_1}a+2i\\,g_0(\\nabla_{g_0}\\psi,\\nabla_{g_0}a)+i(\\Delta_{g_0}\\psi)a=0.$$\nThen, for all sufficiently large $\\tau$, there is an exact solution\n$$u_\\tau=e^{-\\tau(x_1+i\\psi)}a+e^{-\\tau x_1}r_\\tau,\\qquad (-\\Delta_g+q)u_\\tau=0\\ \\text{in }M^\\circ,$$\nwith $r_\\tau\\in H^1(M)$ and $\\|r_\\tau\\|_{L^2(M)}\\leq C\\tau^{-1}\\|(-\\Delta_g+q)a\\|_{L^2(M)}$. No boundary value is prescribed for $r_\\tau$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-ip-cta-gaussian-beam-ray-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M_0,g_0)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary and let $\\gamma:[0,L]\\to M_0$ be a unit-speed nontangential geodesic segment. For every real $\\lambda$ and every $K>0$, there is a family $v_{\\tau+i\\lambda}\\in C^\\infty(M_0)$, $\\tau\\geq1$, such that\n$$\\|(-\\Delta_{g_0}-(\\tau+i\\lambda)^2)v_{\\tau+i\\lambda}\\|_{L^2(M_0)}=O(\\tau^{-K}),\\qquad \\|v_{\\tau+i\\lambda}\\|_{L^2(M_0)}=O(1),$$\nand for every $\\psi\\in C(M_0)$,\n$$\\lim_{\\tau\\to\\infty}\\int_{M_0}|v_{\\tau+i\\lambda}|^2\\psi\\,dV_{g_0} =\\int_0^L e^{-2\\lambda t}\\psi(\\gamma(t))\\,dt.$$\nThe statement permits transversal manifolds without an orientability assumption and makes no boundary condition assertion for the quasimodes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For exact $H^1$ solutions $u_j$ let $f_j=\\operatorname{tr}u_j\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ and define $a_j(v,w)=\\int_M(g(\\nabla_gv,\\nabla_g\\overline w)+q_jv\\overline w)\\,dV_g$. By the weak DN definition, $\\langle\\Lambda_{q_1}f_1,f_2\\rangle=a_1(u_1,u_2)$. Let $\\widetilde u_1\\in H^1(M)$ be the $q_2$ solution with trace $f_1$, which exists by nonresonant weak solvability. Then $\\langle\\Lambda_{q_2}f_1,f_2\\rangle=a_2(\\widetilde u_1,u_2)$. Since $u_1-\\widetilde u_1\\in H_0^1(M)$ and $u_2$ solves the $q_2$ weak equation, $a_2(u_1-\\widetilde u_1,u_2)=\\overline{a_2(u_2,u_1-\\widetilde u_1)}=0$, where the conjugation uses real-valuedness of $q_2$. Subtracting the weak pairings and using equality of the DN maps on all $H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ gives $\\int_M Q(x_1,x')u_1(x_1,x')\\overline{u_2(x_1,x')}\\,dV_g=0$, where $Q=q_1-q_2$. This derives the needed weak-trace extension of [F2]. Because $q_j\\in C_c^\\infty(M^\\circ)$ and $M\\Subset\\mathbb R\\times M_0^\\circ$, extension of $Q$ by zero gives a smooth compactly supported function on $\\mathbb R\\times M_0$.",
