          "claim": "Fix $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, put $s=\\tau+i\\lambda$, and choose the beam $v_s$ of [F6] with residual $O(\\tau^{-K})$. Use the signed real-weight solver [F5] to obtain exact solutions\n$$u_1=e^{-s x_1}(v_s+r_1),\\qquad u_2=e^{s x_1}(v_s+r_2)$$\nfor the respective potentials, with $r_j\\in H^1(M)$ and $\\|r_j\\|_2=O(\\tau^{-1})$. Indeed the conjugated residual is $(-\\Delta_{g_0}-s^2)v_s+q_jv_s$, of bounded $L^2$ norm on a fixed product cylinder; multiplication by $e^{\\pm i\\lambda x_1}$ is bounded there. Correcting with the real-weight solver gives precisely this bound. Their product in step 1.1 is $e^{-2i\\lambda x_1}(v_s+r_1)(\\overline{v_s}+\\overline{r_2})$. Every error integral tends to zero by Cauchy--Schwarz, the boundedness of $Q$, $\\|v_s\\|_2=O(1)$, and $\\|r_j\\|_2=o(1)$. Integrating first in $x_1$ gives the smooth compactly supported transversal test function $\\widehat Q_\\lambda$ below. Applying [F6] proves the attenuated ray identity for every $\\lambda$ and every nontangential $\\gamma$. This argument works on the base manifold; it does not assume injectivity of a ray transform on its orientation cover.",
