          "claim": "Step 2.1 supplies\n$$\\int_{\\gamma}\\widehat Q_\\lambda(\\gamma(t))e^{-2\\lambda t}\\,dt=0 \\quad\\text{for every real }\\lambda\\text{ and every nontangential geodesic }\\gamma,$$\nwhere $\\widehat Q_\\lambda(x')=\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-2i\\lambda x_1}Q(x_1,x')\\,dx_1$. At $\\lambda=0$, [F3] gives $\\widehat Q_0=0$. Assume inductively that $\\partial_\\lambda^j\\widehat Q_0=0$ for every $j<k$. Differentiate the ray identity $k$ times at $\\lambda=0$. Differentiation under the finite geodesic integral is valid because $Q$ is smooth with compact $x_1$ support. Leibniz' rule leaves the unweighted ray integral of $\\partial_\\lambda^k\\widehat Q_0$; all other terms contain lower derivatives and vanish by the induction hypothesis. Thus [F3], applied to real and imaginary parts, gives $\\partial_\\lambda^k\\widehat Q_0=0$. Induction proves that every Taylor coefficient of $\\lambda\\mapsto\\widehat Q_\\lambda(x')$ vanishes for each fixed $x'$.",
