          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\geq2$, be a bounded smooth domain, and let $\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega,\\mathbb R)$ be strictly positive. If their full physical conductivity Dirichlet-to-Neumann maps agree on all boundary data, then every inward boundary-normal derivative agrees:\n$$\\partial_{x_n}^k\\gamma_1|_{\\partial\\Omega} =\\partial_{x_n}^k\\gamma_2|_{\\partial\\Omega} \\qquad(k=0,1,2,\\ldots),$$\nwhere $x_n=\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Omega)$ in a boundary normal collar.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-conductivity-liouville-transform-and-dn-conjugacy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian domain with smooth boundary, let $\\gamma\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$ be strictly positive, put $\\rho=\\sqrt{\\gamma}$, and define $q=(\\Delta_g\\rho)/\\rho$. If $v$ solves $\\operatorname{div}_g(\\gamma\\nabla_gv)=0$ and $u=\\rho v$, then $u$ solves $(-\\Delta_g+q)u=0$. The potential $q$ has trivial Dirichlet kernel. For boundary data $f=v|_{\\partial M}$, with $\\rho_b=\\rho|_{\\partial M}$ and the physical flux convention $\\Lambda_\\gamma f=\\gamma\\partial_{\\nu_{\\rm out}}v|_{\\partial M}$, the exact boundary relation is\n$$\\Lambda_\\gamma f=\\rho_b\\Lambda_q(\\rho_b f)-\\rho_b(\\partial_{\\nu_{\\rm out}}\\rho)|_{\\partial M}f.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-ip-euclidean-schrodinger-calderon-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $n\\geq3$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded, nonempty, connected smooth domain, and let $q_1,q_2\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega,\\mathbb R)$. Suppose zero is not a Dirichlet eigenvalue of either $-\\Delta+q_j$, and the full Schrödinger Dirichlet-to-Neumann maps satisfy $\\Lambda_{q_1}=\\Lambda_{q_2}$ on all of $H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$. Then $q_1=q_2$ on $\\Omega$.",
