          "claim": "For any skein triple, the constant coefficients of $\\nabla_{L_+}$ and $\\nabla_{L_-}$ agree because their difference is divisible by $z$. Hence $c_0(L):=[z^0]\\nabla_L$ is unchanged by crossing switches. Choose a basepoint on each component of a link diagram and a finite order of the components; switch each crossing so its first encounter in that traversal is the overpass. The resulting diagram is descending: starting at each basepoint, every arc can be pulled successively over the portions it meets, in the chosen component order, until each component is a round circle in a separate ball. Thus every link can be changed by crossing switches to an unlink. For the $r$-component unlink $U_r$, the normalization gives $c_0(U_1)=1$. Add a local curl to one component of $U_r$; its positive and negative versions are both $U_r$, while its oriented smoothing is the split unlink $U_{r+1}$. The skein relation gives $z\\nabla_{U_{r+1}}=0$. Multiplication by the indeterminate $z$ is injective in $\\mathbb Z[z]$: if $f=\\sum_{j=0}^{N}a_jz^j$ and $zf=0$, then each coefficient $a_j$ is zero. Hence $\\nabla_{U_{r+1}}=0$. Therefore $c_0(L)=1$ for knots and $c_0(L)=0$ for links with at least two components.",
