        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-kontsevich-configuration-integral-convergence-needle-estimates",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix either a strict Morse knot presentation $K:S^1\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb C\\times\\mathbb R$ or a compact strict Morse tangle $T\\subset\\mathbb C\\times[a,b]$ with distinct regular boundary points, and a degree $m\\ge0$. Each degree-$m$ coefficient of the unframed Kontsevich series is an absolutely convergent improper integral after summing its finitely many pairing terms in the 4T+1T quotient. A critical needle of width $\\varepsilon$ in a horizontal slab is a local arc $N_\\varepsilon$ whose intersection with each horizontal disk in the slab has at most two points and whose horizontal diameter is at most $\\varepsilon$, with the knot or tangle fixed outside the slab and with the nondegenerate critical-point charts uniformly bounded in a compact smooth family. For every fixed $m$, the sum of degree-$m$ pairing integrals that have at least one endpoint on $N_\\varepsilon$ has norm at most $C_m\\varepsilon$, where $C_m$ is independent of $\\varepsilon$. Thus these contributions tend to zero as the needle narrows.\n\nThe following figure illustrates a local maximum. The two branches meet at the critical point; the short chord is isolated if no higher endpoint interrupts the small oriented component arc, while a long chord joins a needle branch to a point outside the needle.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9,thick]\n  \\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (1.2,0) node[right] {$z$};\n  \\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (-1.2,2.5) node[above] {$t$};\n  \\draw (-0.7,0.25) .. controls (-0.42,1.35) and (-0.18,1.95) .. (0,2.2);\n  \\draw (0.7,0.25) .. controls (0.42,1.35) and (0.18,1.95) .. (0,2.2);\n  \\draw[dashed] (-0.4,1.2) -- (0.4,1.2);\n  \\draw[dashed] (-0.52,0.75) -- (0.52,0.75);\n  \\draw[dashed] (-0.95,0.75) -- (-0.52,0.75);\n  \\fill (0,2.2) circle (1.2pt);\n  \\node at (0.25,2.28) {$t_c$};\n  \\node at (0,1.42) {short};\n  \\node at (-0.72,0.9) {long};\n  \\node at (0.83,1.2) {$\\varepsilon$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
