          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-barnatan-tangle-cobordism-category",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let an object be a finite disjoint union of smooth embedded circles in the oriented plane $\\mathbb R^2$, including the empty union. A cobordism from $C_0$ to $C_1$ is a compact smooth orientable surface properly embedded in $\\mathbb R^2\\times[0,1]$, with boundary $C_0\\times\\{0\\}\\sqcup C_1\\times\\{1\\}$, product collars at those boundaries, and no other boundary. Surfaces are identified up to ambient isotopy fixed on the boundary collars. Compose cobordisms by gluing along their common boundary circles; the identity of $C$ is $C\\times[0,1]$, and disjoint union gives the monoidal product. Let $\\mathsf{Cob}_3$ be this additive monoidal category before imposing local relations.\n\nTake the free abelian group on these isotopy classes of cobordisms and quotient by the following local relations, which are required to hold inside every surrounding cobordism. A sphere component is zero (the S relation). Adding a disjoint torus component multiplies a cobordism by $2$ (the T relation). For four disjoint disk patches numbered $1,2,3,4$, write $S_{ij}$ for the surface obtained by deleting the interiors of patches $i,j$ and joining their boundary circles by an orientable tube; the four-tube relation is $S_{12}+S_{34}=S_{13}+S_{24}$. The resulting additive monoidal category is denoted $\\mathsf{Cob}_{BN}$.",
