          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and the actual finite balanced polygon data, a Spin-c structure $\\mathfrak s$ on $W$, and one realized reference polygon $u_0$. Let $L$ be the lattice of formal integral periodic region chains $Q$ with $j\\operatorname{PD}H(Q)=0$, and let $G\\subset L$ impose zero multiplicity at every $w_l$. On $G$ put $\\ell(P)=\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak s),H(P)\\rangle/2$. The values of $\\ell$ are integers. Assume the strict strong-admissibility condition\n$$0\\ne P\\in G,\\quad\\ell(P)=N\\ge0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\max_R P(R)>N.$$\nThen the relative-index functional\n$$\\nu(Q)=\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak s),H(Q)\\rangle+2\\sum_l n_{w_l}(Q)$$\nis strictly positive on every nonzero nonnegative direction in $L$. The nonnegative domains of actual same-corner, same-Spin-c polygons have a finite lower relative-index bound. For every integer $K$, only finitely many such nonnegative integral domains have relative index at most $K$. These statements concern domains, not the number of literal homotopy classes or holomorphic maps with one domain. They apply to actual Fredholm indices when the fixed-cap relative-index identification has been supplied.\n\nFor a boundary-pair sector the same conclusions hold with $L$ the FULL pair-periodic lattice, $G$ its all-mark-zero sublattice, and the Chern evaluation taken on its capped class in $Y$. Thus every nonempty fixed-end strip sector has a lower actual index bound $L_0$ and finitely many nonnegative domains of indices in $[L_0,K]$. For fixed attaching curves, marks and labels these bounds and finite sets are independent of the Cauchy–Riemann coefficients, auxiliary family parameters and source conformal parameters satisfying the same index conventions; they depend only on the finite domain lattice and Chern functional. No uniformity under an unspecified change of attaching curves is asserted.\n\nThere is also a weaker fixed-index statement. If every nonzero $P\\in G$ with $\\ell(P)=0$ has both signs, then at a fixed entire mark vector and any fixed finite set of relative indices there are only finitely many nonnegative domains in the fixed Spin-c sector. For supplied actual strip classes the pair version also holds under the separate weaker condition testing mixed signs only on REALIZED normalized index-zero periodic classes. Ordinary weakness for a specified Spin-c sector instead tests ALL formal normalized periodic domains with Chern evaluation zero, even when that sector has no generators; whenever an actual generator is supplied, the actual periodic index formula shows that ordinary specified-sector weakness implies this weaker realized-class condition. This proves a finite-domain statement only for supplied actual classes; it neither establishes invariance under moves nor supplies a generator in an intended sector whose generator set is empty. It does not give an unrestricted lower index bound. Any separately supplied uniform positive area bound also bounds every nonnegative component domain and hence its index, by its finite region coefficients.\n\nThere is a separate all-Spin-c zero-mark conclusion. If every nonzero formal periodic domain with all $w$ multiplicities zero has both positive and negative coefficients, then for fixed corners and fixed zero $w$ multiplicities there are only finitely many nonnegative domains, without fixing a Spin-c structure or an index. This global multi-periodic hypothesis is not inferred from strong admissibility for one Spin-c structure or from pairwise weak admissibility. No existence or winding construction of either type of diagram is asserted.",
