        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-khovanov-cobordism-map-up-to-sign",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and fix a generic movie $M$ of a smooth oriented surface cobordism $\\Sigma$ from an oriented link $L_0$ to an oriented link $L_1$. For each regular frame $D$, let $Kh(D)$ be the normalized integral Khovanov homology group. The empty frame has the one-term complex $\\mathbb Z$ in both gradings zero, using the empty resolution and the zero-fold tensor product. Associate to a birth the unit map $\\eta:\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathcal A$, $\\eta(1)=1$; associate to a death the counit $\\epsilon:\\mathcal A\\to\\mathbb Z$, $\\epsilon(1)=0,\\epsilon(X)=1$; and associate to a saddle the multiplication $m:\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal A\\to\\mathcal A$ when two resolution circles merge, or the comultiplication $\\Delta:\\mathcal A\\to\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal A$ when one splits. Each local map is tensored with the identity on unaffected circles. Associate to planar isotopy the induced identification of the cube complexes. For Reidemeister moves, use the following fixed Bar-Natan constructions: the kink retractions $F_+=v-\\Delta$, $G_+=e$, $F_-=u$, $G_-=w-m$; the two inverse-crossing strong retractions with degree-zero inclusion $(1,ut)^T$ and projection $(1,-se)$, with their specified reverse-order cube signs; and the third-move equivalences obtained by the explicit source- or target-cone retractions onto the common six-point residual complex. Here $u,e$ are circle birth/death, $v,w$ are punctured-torus birth/death, and the saddle letters denote the actual local bands in these constructions. Use their specified inverses for the reversed moves. Insert these formal surface matrices into the fixed planar outside of the diagram, then apply the integral Frobenius cobordism functor. Normalize a state $v$ to cochain degree $|v|-n_-$ and quantum shift $|v|+n_+-2n_-$. Fix the local coordinate identifications and orientation cases once, with the Koszul crossing-order correction $(-1)^{v(a)v(b)}$ when crossings $a,b$ are interchanged. These local maps are chosen once; composing them with an arbitrary automorphism of a complex is not an allowed change of representative. Compose the induced maps on homology in movie order and denote the result by $Kh(M):Kh(L_0)\\to Kh(L_1)$, considered modulo multiplication by $-1$. This definition fixes a movie; independence from changing the movie is not included in the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-barnatan-formal-kink-retractions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $P$ be a single arc in a disk and let $Z=P\\sqcup O$ add a small disjoint circle next to it. Let $m:Z\\to P$ merge the circle into the arc by an elementary saddle and let $\\Delta:P\\to Z$ be the reversed saddle. In the open Bar-Natan category the two normalized kink complexes $C_+=(Z\\{1\\}\\xrightarrow{m}P\\{2\\})$ in cochain degrees $0,1$, and $C_-=(P\\{-2\\}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta}Z\\{-1\\})$ in degrees $-1,0$, both have explicit internal degree zero strong deformation retractions onto the one-term complex $P$ in degree zero. These retractions are preserved by insertion into an arbitrary planar surrounding complex.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-barnatan-formal-inverse-boundary-crossings",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. In the open Bar-Natan category on four boundary points of a rectangle, let $I$ be the vertical two-strand identity pairing and let $E$ be the pairing consisting of a top cap and bottom cup. Let $t:I\\to E$ and $s:E\\to I$ be the elementary saddle surfaces. Define the formal crossing complexes $K_+=(I\\{1\\}\\xrightarrow{t}E\\{2\\})$, in cochain degrees $0,1$, and $K_-=(E\\{-2\\}\\xrightarrow{s}I\\{-1\\})$, in degrees $-1,0$. Then both planar stackings $K_+K_-$ and $K_-K_+$ have explicit internal degree zero strong deformation retractions onto the one-term complex $I$ in degree zero, with no grading shift. All maps and homotopies can be inserted into an arbitrary planar surrounding complex. Consequently adjoining either of these crossing complexes preserves simplicity in both directions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-barnatan-formal-third-reidemeister-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Put six points on a disk boundary in clockwise order $1,2,3,4,5,6$, at northwest, northeast, east, southeast, southwest and west. Consider diagonal arcs $a$ from $1$ to $4$ and $b$ from $2$ to $5$, and an arc $c$ from $6$ to $3$. In the left diagram $c$ bows above the intersection of $a,b$; in the right it bows below. In both diagrams $c$ passes over both diagonals. Either $a$ passes over $b$ in both diagrams, or $b$ passes over $a$ in both. These are the two local third Reidemeister variants considered here. More precisely one can use the diameters $y=-x,y=x$ for $a,b$ and the curves $y=\\pm h(1-x^2)$, $0<h<1/2$, for $c$, with fixed radial collars near the six boundary points.\n\nUse the local smoothing chart in which the over-strand joins southwest to northeast and the under-strand joins northwest to southeast. The A-smoothing joins the left pair and right pair, and the B-smoothing joins the top pair and bottom pair. An A-first cube uses $0=A,1=B$; a B-first cube uses $0=B,1=A$. For either convention, the raw formal cube of an ordered diagram has term $\\bigoplus_{|v|=s}S_v\\{s\\}$ in cochain degree $s$ and edge saddle coefficient $(-1)^{\\sum_{k<j}v_k}$ in coordinate $j$. For any choice of strand orientations, put this term in degree $s-n_-$ and shift it internally by the additional $n_+-2n_-$.\n\nFor each of these two smoothing conventions, the formal cubes of the two diagrams admit internal degree zero chain homotopy equivalences, with explicit maps given below. The displayed smoothing table and cone calculation use A-first cubes: turning right from an entering underpassing half-line gives the A-smoothing. The B-first maps are obtained from the raw A-first maps by complementing every bit and reversing surface time, without spatial reflection, with the explicit vertex sign correction below; only after this operation are the same given $n_+,n_-$ normalizations applied to both sides. All these maps and homotopies can be inserted into any planar surrounding complex.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-barnatan-open-pairings-have-scalar-degree-zero-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. In the open Bar-Natan category, let $P$ be a smoothing consisting of $k$ arcs and no circles, including $k=0$. The map $\\mathbb Z\\to\\operatorname{End}^0(P)$ sending $n$ to $n\\operatorname{id}_P$ is an isomorphism of rings. Consequently the one-term complex $P$, with any common grading shift, is simple.",
          "uses": [
