          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-barnatan-planar-homotopies-and-conditional-boundary-crossing-simplicity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the open Bar-Natan categories, planar totalization defines operations on bounded complexes and on their homotopy categories, preserving internal degrees and homotopy equivalences. Simplicity is invariant under an internal degree zero chain homotopy equivalence. In particular let $A$ and $B$ be the operations of adjoining a fixed boundary crossing complex and its proposed inverse, each using precisely two adjacent boundary legs. Suppose local internal degree zero chain homotopy equivalences of their two possible composites with the straight two-strand identity complex have been supplied. Then a complex $C$ is simple if and only if $A(C)$ is simple. This is a conditional assertion about these local inverse equivalences; it does not assert Reidemeister invariance or provide crossing complexes or their inverse maps.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-barnatan-morse-movie-matrices",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Assign to births, deaths and saddles their elementary formal surfaces, and to the Reidemeister kink, inverse-crossing and third-move constructions the explicit formal retractions specified below. Then the two sides of each Euclidean Morse movie relation MM11, MM12, MM13, MM14 and MM15 induce chain maps equal up to an overall sign. This includes both directions, reflected models, all compatible orientations, and insertion into a fixed planar surrounding complex. It asserts the five Morse movie identities for those choices; it asserts no spherical sweep-around identity.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-integral-khovanov-frobenius-algebra-defines-a-cobordism-functor",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$, with $\\deg1=1$, $\\deg X=-1$, and structure maps $m,\\eta,\\Delta,\\epsilon$ given by $\\eta(1)=1$, $\\epsilon(1)=0$, $\\epsilon(X)=1$, $\\Delta(1)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1$, and $\\Delta(X)=X\\otimes X$. There is an additive symmetric monoidal functor $F$ from the category of closed Bar-Natan smoothing cobordisms before local relations to finite free graded abelian groups. Here closed means that objects consist of circles, including the empty collection, and there are no vertical side intervals. The target has all integral linear maps, graded by their homogeneous degrees, and its symmetry is the ordinary interchange of tensor factors, without a Koszul sign from quantum degrees.\n\nExplicitly, $F(C)$ is the free group on the labelings of the finite circle set of $C$ by $1,X$, graded by the sum of the label degrees. For a connected surface $\\Sigma$ of genus $g$, with $p$ incoming and $q$ outgoing circles, its map in any temporary ordering of those circles is $F(\\Sigma)=\\Delta_q H^g m_p$, where $H=m\\Delta$, $m_0=\\eta$, $m_1=1_{\\mathcal A}$, $m_p$ is iterated multiplication for $p\\ge2$, $\\Delta_0=\\epsilon$, $\\Delta_1=1_{\\mathcal A}$ and $\\Delta_q$ is iterated comultiplication for $q\\ge2$. For a disconnected surface, take the tensor product over its connected components and route each factor to its actual boundary circle. The empty surface is sent to $1_{\\mathbb Z}$. This construction is independent of all temporary orderings, respects embedded isotopy, stacking and disjoint union, sends the elementary birth, death, merge and split to $\\eta,\\epsilon,m,\\Delta$, and sends a surface to a homogeneous map of degree $\\chi(\\Sigma)$. It respects S, T and four-tube and therefore descends to the closed Bar-Natan quotient category.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-khovanov-frobenius-tqft-respects-barnatan-relations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf{Cob}_3$ be the additive monoidal category of oriented cobordisms before the S, T, and four-tube relations are imposed, and let $\\mathsf{Cob}_{BN}$ be its quotient by those relations. Suppose $F:\\mathsf{Cob}_3\\to\\mathsf{GrAb}$ is a symmetric monoidal functor to graded abelian groups and homogeneous maps, with a circle sent to $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$, a cup to $\\eta(1)=1$, a cap to $\\epsilon(1)=0,\\epsilon(X)=1$, a merge pair of pants to $m$, and a split pair of pants to $\\Delta(1)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1,\\ \\Delta(X)=X\\otimes X$. Then every sphere component is sent to zero, every disjoint torus component acts by multiplication by $2$, and the two sides of the four-tube relation induce the same map. Therefore $F$ factors through an additive monoidal functor on $\\mathsf{Cob}_{BN}$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
