          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "**All actual rank-two foams are covered.** In the positive-label raw foam category a facet of label at least three has no two-pigment coloring. Every closure of a foam containing it therefore evaluates to zero, so that foam is in the radical. Each allowed tetrahedral vertex has such a facet: its first positive-label model contains $a+b+c\\ge3$; its second has $a>c\\ge1$, $b\\ge1$, and contains $a+b\\ge3$. Thus every nonzero rank-two foam is vertex-free, with precisely the facets of step 1.1; no zero-facet removal assertion is required. To compare raw left-hand Robert–Wagner signs with the right-hand evaluation above, let $B_i$ be the blue pigment-$i$ surfaces, $R$ the red surface, $M$ the seam count, and $t_i$ the number of seams positive on blue pigment $i$ in right-hand order. Their two sign exponents are $\\chi(B_1\\cup R)/2+t_2$ and $\\chi(B_2\\cup R)/2+t_1$. Their difference modulo two is $\\chi(B)/2+\\chi(R)+M=\\chi(B)/2$, since $\\chi(R)\\equiv M\\pmod2$. Their common denominator is $d^{\\chi(B)/2}$. Consequently raw evaluation differs by $(-1)^{\\chi(B)/2}$. At $h=t=0$ a nonzero scalar evaluation with $D$ blue dots has $2D=\\chi(B)$; replacing each raw blue dot by its negative cancels this sign. Positive-degree red decorations specialize to zero. Thus every closed evaluation, and hence every bent radical, agrees after this explicit dot normalization. In particular the normalized dotted sphere is $+1$; it is not identified with the unrenormalized raw dotted sphere, which is $-1$.",
