          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "**Pairing bases, without red compression.** A closed web with $k$ underlying blue circles has exactly $2^k$ boundary pigment states: a pigment changes across every red vertex on its blue circle, and there are an even number of these vertices because blue orientation changes there. Localized closed evaluation factors through these states, so its pairing rank is at most $2^k$. For a pure-blue web, dotted disk cups and complementary dotted caps have a unimodular tensor-product pairing: the one-circle Gram matrix is $\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\\\1&h\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, of determinant $-1$. Hence its universal module is exactly the free module with those $2^k$ cups. For a general web $W$, there is a foam $I_W$ with underlying blue cylinders from standardly oriented blue circles to $W$. To construct it, checkerboard-color the complement of the underlying blue cylinders; complete every wrongly oriented region boundary by a red copy, push its interior into that region and assign the opposite color in the new thin region. The boundary red arcs are completed in the same way. This finite construction uses only parallel copies in collars, so produces the prescribed boundary and no singular vertices. The reflected composite $I_W^*I_W$ commutes with dots: crossing a seam changes $X$ to $h-X$, and each blue cylinder crosses an even number of orientation changes. It is degree zero, so on the pure-blue module it is multiplication by an integer. For each fixed boundary pigment, its color contraction is a single nonzero term of absolute value one; hence that integer is $\\pm1$. Thus $I_W$ has a left inverse. Its cokernel is a direct summand of the universal module, is torsion-free because all its pairing evaluations lie in the domain $\\mathbb Z[h,t]$, and has rank zero by the localized $2^k$ bound. It is zero, and $I_W$ is invertible. Bending now identifies every local morphism space with the corresponding blue cup module. In particular a $k$-arc pairing has basis degrees $2|L|$ in its degree-zero endomorphism calculation, so $\\operatorname{End}^0=\\mathbb Z\\,1$. This argument does not compress a possibly knotted red surface in a ball.",
