        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "**The actual crossing and tensor conventions.** Let $I$ be the two-strand pairing and $A_i$ the thick web at adjacent positions $i,i+1$. In the over-southwest-to-northeast source chart, $I$ is the A-smoothing and the underlying blue pairing of $A_i$ is the B-smoothing. Use auxiliary crossing factors $(A_i\\{-1\\}\\to I)$ in degrees $(-1,0)$ and $(I\\to A_i\\{1\\})$ in degrees $(0,1)$ for the two inverse physical braid generators. These are the two rank-two foam factors with all-parallel term in degree zero; the braces here are the formal shift convention, opposite to their printed $q$ exponent. Surface-time cochain duality, with intrinsic grading changed from $-\\chi$ to $\\chi$, gives $(I\\to E_i\\{1\\})$ and $(E_i\\{-1\\}\\to I)$, respectively. Adding the common writhe shifts $+1$ and $-1$ yields exactly $(I\\{1\\}\\to E_i\\{2\\})$ and $(E_i\\{-2\\}\\to I\\{-1\\})$, the normalized ordinary factors. For an arbitrary balanced boundary orientation, cut an annulus at its basepoint and apply the same region completion as in step 3.1 to blue radial curtains. Its red facets convert the standard alternating boundary orientation to the prescribed one, while its blue curtains and cochain degree remain unchanged. Reversing an arrow is handled by bending this same boundary curtain; the underlying smoothing and the differential are unchanged, and the normalization is still $|v|-n_-$ and $|v|+n_+-2n_-$. At a curl this is also checked directly by the two kink retractions in [F4]. A zip or unzip transports to a primitive signed saddle, since its bent minimal-degree morphism space has one cup basis vector and its two-pigment contraction has coefficient a unit. Square commutation makes these edge signs a cube cocycle, removed by vertex signs from a fixed vertex; all two-saddle composites here are nonzero, as their blue Frobenius values show. Two blue-curtain completion choices differ on each state by a dot-linear degree-zero unit of the pure-blue module, hence by $\\pm1$. After correcting the cube edges, connectedness of the cube forces these state signs to be one common sign. Therefore local insertion and the comparison of whole complexes are coherent up to one overall sign, which is the precise coherence required here. Finally prefix and suffix totalization conventions differ by the vertex sign $(-1)^{\\sum_{i<j}r_i r_j}$ for actual factor degrees $r_i$. Across the $i$th differential its ratio is $(-1)^{\\sum_{j\\ne i}r_j}$, exactly the difference of the two tensor signs. Thus this is a local comparison of actual complexes with their orders and duals, not only an equality of closed graded ranks.",
