        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "**Check of the matrix equations and the two required features.** Here is the full finite checking rule. Enumerate the eight input and eight output colorings, then the paths of each listed word by the two choices at each permitted step in step 4.2. Apply a matrix entry by successively replacing the local subpath by the stated operator's outputs, multiply coefficients, and add coincident paths. A differential at coordinate $r$ has coefficient $(-1)^{\\sum_{l<r}(b_l-\\mathbf1_{\\text{factor }l\\text{ positive}})}$ and applies $\\varepsilon$ or $\\eta$ according as that factor is positive or negative. Direct substitution gives $d'F=Fd$ on all $72$ state-path columns in each case. For each nonzero component, the internal degree $(b_1-\\mathbf1_{\\text{internal positive}})$ in the target is at least $(b_3-\\mathbf1_{\\text{internal positive}})$ in the source; there are $80$ nonzero color coefficients in each case, all satisfying this inequality. Finally compare I with II and III with IV by complementing the first two source bits and last two target bits. Their path words are then identical. On pairs of states of equal internal degree their coefficients agree: $144$ coefficient tests in each comparison. This enumeration is also a short exact verification algorithm for all the asserted equations: every basic operator coefficient is $0,1$ or $-1$, sums use exact integers, and the only division is by a color difference $\\pm1$. For general difference $d$, every entry of $F$ has common power $d^{(\\ell_{\\rm target}-\\ell_{\\rm source})/2}$, where $\\ell$ counts thick letters; every coefficient in $d'F-Fd$ has common power $d^{(\\ell_{\\rm target}-\\ell_{\\rm source}+1)/2}$. Thus the checks at a unit difference prove the rational identities for arbitrary distinct pigments, and step 4.2 proves the integral foam identities. The complete executable rule, including all four matrices, is retained in the calculation record below. Our representatives are internally nondecreasing, the dual filtration direction to the convention in the cited sweep calculation; this is sufficient because both sweep endpoints have external degree zero.",
