          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-ip-eig-is-joint-relative-entropy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Fix a design $d$ in a design-indexed dominated inverse experiment, with prior $\\mu_0$, likelihood density $p_d(y\\mid\\theta)$ relative to the common sigma-finite measure $\\nu$, predictive density $p_d(y)$, and posterior $\\mu_d^y$ on $G_d=\\{y:0<p_d(y)<\\infty\\}$. Let $P_d$ be the joint law of $(\\theta,Y)$, so on measurable rectangles\n$$P_d(A\\times B)=\\int_B\\int_Ap_d(y\\mid\\theta)\\,\\mu_0(d\\theta)\\,\\nu(dy),$$\nlet $P_{Y\\mid d}(B)=\\int_Bp_d(y)\\,\\nu(dy)$, and let $Q_d=\\mu_0\\otimes P_{Y\\mid d}$. Then\n$$\\operatorname{EIG}(d)=D(P_d\\|Q_d)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nOn $G_d$, the Radon–Nikodym density $dP_d/dQ_d$ is $p_d(y\\mid\\theta)/p_d(y)$ almost everywhere. The identity is valid as an extended integral: its negative part is finite, its positive part may be infinite, and no subtraction of two infinite entropies occurs.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-ip-finite-linear-gaussian-posterior",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $n,m\\ge1$, $m_0\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and let\n$C_0\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be symmetric with $v^TC_0v\\ge0$ for every\n$v\\in\\mathbb R^n$. Let\n$A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ and let\n$\\Gamma\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times m}$ be symmetric positive definite, meaning\n$u^T\\Gamma u>0$ for every nonzero $u\\in\\mathbb R^m$. Suppose\n$X$ and $\\eta$ are independent random vectors with laws\n$X\\sim N_n(m_0,C_0)$ and $\\eta\\sim N_m(0,\\Gamma)$, and set\n$Y=AX+\\eta$. Define $\\mu_0(E)=\\mathbb P(X\\in E)$ for Borel\n$E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, and set\n$S=AC_0A^T+\\Gamma$. Then $S$ is positive definite. For each\n$y\\in\\mathbb R^m$, define\n$$ \\Phi_y(x)=\\tfrac12(y-Ax)^T\\Gamma^{-1}(y-Ax),\\qquad Z(y)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-\\Phi_y(x)}\\,\\mu_0(dx). $$\nFor every $y$, $0<Z(y)<\\infty$, so the normalized potential update\n$$ \\Pi^y(E)=\\frac{\\int_E e^{-\\Phi_y(x)}\\,\\mu_0(dx)}{Z(y)} \\quad(E\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n)) $$\nis a probability measure and equals the conditional-law version obtained by\nconditioning the observation model at $y$. Its Gaussian formula, defined for\nevery $y$, is\n$$ N_n\\!\\left( m_0+C_0A^TS^{-1}(y-Am_0),\\ C_0-C_0A^TS^{-1}AC_0 \\right). $$\nLet $P_Y(B)=\\mathbb P(Y\\in B)$ for Borel $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$.\nConditional-law versions are unique only $P_Y$-almost surely. The displayed\nmean and covariance formulas invert only $S$, not $C_0$; $C_0$ and the\nposterior covariance may be singular. The prior support is\n$m_0+\\operatorname{Ran}(C_0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-finite-independent-gaussian-vectors-form-joint-multivariate-normal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $m\\ge1$ be finite, let $d_j\\ge1$, and let\n$Z_j$ for $1\\le j\\le m$ be mutually independent random elements of\n$\\mathbb R^{d_j}$. Suppose $Z_j\\sim N_{d_j}(a_j,\\Sigma_j)$, where each\n$\\Sigma_j$ is symmetric positive semidefinite. Put\n$D=\\sum_{j=1}^m d_j$, $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$,\n$a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_m)$, and\n$\\Sigma=\\operatorname{diag}(\\Sigma_1,\\ldots,\\Sigma_m)$. Then\n$$Z\\sim N_D(a,\\Sigma).$$\nNo covariance block needs to be invertible.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-nondegenerate-multivariate-normal-law-has-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $d\\ge1$, $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $\\Sigma$ be a real symmetric positive-definite $d\\times d$ matrix. If $X\\sim N_d(m,\\Sigma)$, then its law has Lebesgue density\n$$f_{m,\\Sigma}(x)=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}\\det(\\Sigma)^{-1/2} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-m)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(x-m)\\right), \\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$",
          "uses": [
