ed, return `numerically-unresolved`. The returned candidate's true utility gap is at most $\\tau+2\\max_d\\varepsilon_d$. If its lower interval endpoint exceeds every earlier candidate's upper endpoint and is at least every later candidate's upper endpoint, certify it as the first exact maximizer. The algorithm makes no bit-complexity claim for exact-real comparisons.",
