          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-hahn-banach-extension-principle-relative",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Work over ZF. The **real dominated-extension principle**, denoted **HB**, is the following assertion:\n\nFor every real vector space $X$, every sublinear functional $p:X\\to\\mathbb R$ in the sense of [[def-sublinear-functional]], every real linear subspace $M\\le X$ in the sense of [[def-linear-subspace]], and every real linear functional $g:M\\to\\mathbb R$ in the sense of [[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]],\n$$\\bigl(\\forall m\\in M,\\ g(m)\\le p(m)\\bigr)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\exists F:X\\to\\mathbb R\\ \\bigl(F\\text{ is real linear},\\ F|_M=g,\\ \\forall x\\in X,\\ F(x)\\le p(x)\\bigr).$$\n\nThis names an additional principle; it does not assert a proof of HB in ZF. Subsequent results explicitly state when they assume HB. Neither topology nor completeness is part of this assertion. The subspace may be $\\{0\\}$ or all of $X$. Sublinearity at scalar zero gives $p(0)=0$, and a linear functional has value zero at zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-borel-gaussian-random-element-on-a-separable-banach-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space and let $B$ be a real\nseparable Banach space with its norm Borel sigma-algebra. A map\n$W:\\Omega\\to B$ is a **centered Borel Gaussian random element** if it is\nBorel-measurable and, for every $\\ell\\in B^*$, the real random variable\n$\\ell(W)$ has a centered (possibly zero-variance) normal law. Its law is the\nBorel probability $\\mu=P\\circ W^{-1}$. When the word *tight* is used, it means\nthat for every $\\delta>0$ there is a norm-compact $K\\subseteq B$ with\n$\\mu(K)>1-\\delta$.\n\nThis definition concerns a random element in $B$. A Gaussian process on an\narbitrary index set with only a cylinder-space law does not satisfy it unless\na Borel $B$-valued realization is separately constructed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-banach-gaussian-covariance-operator-and-rkhs",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $W$ be a centered Borel Gaussian random element in a real separable\nBanach space $B$, with law $\\mu$. For $\\ell\\in B^*$ define\n$$C\\ell=E[\\ell(W)W]\\in B,\\qquad Q(\\ell,m)=E[\\ell(W)m(W)]\\quad(\\ell,m\\in B^*).$$\nThe **Cameron–Martin reproducing-kernel Hilbert space** $H$ is the completion\nof $B^*/N$ in the inner product induced by $Q$, where\n$N=\\{\\ell\\in B^*:Q(\\ell,\\ell)=0\\}$. The canonical map sends the class of\n$\\ell$ to $C\\ell$ and extends continuously to an injective map $i:H\\to B$;\nwe identify $H$ with its image in $B$ and use the completed Hilbert norm\n$\\|\\cdot\\|_H$. The preceding construction is well-defined by\n[[thm-banach-gaussian-covariance-rkhs-is-well-defined]].\n\nThe term *RKHS* here denotes this covariance-induced Hilbert space of shifts\ninside $B$; it is not the space of all continuous functions on an arbitrary\nindex set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-banach-gaussian-covariance-rkhs-is-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Under the hypotheses and notation of\n[[def-banach-gaussian-covariance-operator-and-rkhs]], each $C\\ell$ exists as a\nBochner integral; $Q$ is a finite positive-semidefinite bilinear form;\n$N=\\{\\ell:Q(\\ell,\\ell)=0\\}$ is its nullspace; and the induced map from the\ncompletion of $B^*/N$ to $B$ is continuous and injective. Thus the definition\nidentifies a Hilbert space of shifts in $B$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-separable-banach-has-countable-norming-family",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume HB and countable choice. Let $B$ be a real separable Banach space. There is a sequence\n$(\\ell_j)_{j\\ge1}\\subseteq B^*$ with $\\|\\ell_j\\|=1$ such that\n$$\\|x\\|=\\sup_{j\\ge1}|\\ell_j(x)|\\qquad(x\\in B).$$\nIf $B=\\{0\\}$, the conclusion is interpreted using the zero functional and the\nsupremum is $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-gaussian-centering-for-bounded-symmetric-quasiconvex-loss",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $X\\sim N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ with positive-definite covariance\n$\\Sigma$. Let $L:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,M]$ be Borel measurable and symmetric,\n$L(-x)=L(x)$, and quasi-convex, meaning each sublevel set\n$\\{x:L(x)\\le t\\}$ is convex. Then for every $a\\in\\mathbb R^d$,\n$$E L(X-a)\\ge E L(X-m).$$\nThus the Gaussian center $m$ minimizes expected loss. The result includes\nquasi-convex losses whose sublevel sets are not closed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-continuity-from-above-for-measures",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(E_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be a decreasing sequence of measurable sets for a measure $\\mu$. If $\\mu(E_{n_0})<+\\infty$ for some $n_0$, then\n\n$$\\mu\\left(\\bigcap_{n\\in\\mathbb N}E_n\\right)=\\inf_{n\\in\\mathbb N}\\mu(E_n).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Choose the norming family from [F3]. Fix $r>0$, $h\\in E$, and $n\\ge1$. Write $Y_n=(\\ell_1(W),\\ldots,\\ell_n(W))$, let $\\Sigma_n$ be its covariance matrix, and put $a_n=(\\ell_1(ih),\\ldots,\\ell_n(ih))$. The vector $Y_n$ is centered Gaussian by [F1]. If $c\\in\\ker\\Sigma_n$, then $\\sum_{j\\le n}c_j\\ell_j(W)$ has variance zero and hence is zero almost surely. For $\\ell_c=\\sum_{j\\le n}c_j\\ell_j$, this gives $C\\ell_c=0$ and $[\\ell_c]=0$ in $E$; therefore [F2] gives $c\\cdot a_n=\\ell_c(ih)=\\langle h,[\\ell_c]\\rangle_E=0$. Thus $a_n\\perp\\ker\\Sigma_n$, so $a_n\\in S_n:=\\operatorname{ran}(\\Sigma_n)$, the support subspace of $Y_n$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
