            "F3",
            "given",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In the finite-dimensional space $S_n$, the restriction of $Y_n$ is a nondegenerate Gaussian vector. Let $Q_n=[-r,r]^n$ and $D_n=Q_n\\cap S_n$, a closed symmetric convex set. Since $a_n\\in S_n$, the event $Y_n-a_n\\in Q_n$ is the translate by $a_n$ of $D_n$ inside $S_n$. An invertible linear change of coordinates turns the centered law on $S_n$ into a nondegenerate Gaussian law and preserves symmetry and convexity of $D_n$. Apply [F4] to the bounded loss $1_{D_n^c}$ in those coordinates: its sublevel sets are $D_n$ and the whole space, so it is symmetric and quasi-convex. This yields\n$$ \\mathbb P(Y_n-a_n\\in Q_n)\\le\\mathbb P(Y_n\\in Q_n). $$\nIf $S_n=\\{0\\}$, then $Y_n=a_n=0$ almost surely and the same inequality is equality.",
