        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\delta>0$ and put $w_n^* =\\max_jw_{n,j}$. If $w_{n,j}=0$, its Lindeberg summand is zero. Otherwise $w_{n,j}|V_{n,j}|>\\delta$ implies $|V_{n,j}|>\\delta/w_n^*$. Therefore $$\\sum_j\\mathbb E\\!\\left[w_{n,j}^2V_{n,j}^2\\mathbf1_{\\{w_{n,j}|V_{n,j}|>\\delta\\}}\\right]\\le \\frac12\\mathbb E\\!\\left[V^2\\mathbf1_{\\{|V|>\\delta/w_n^*\\}}\\right].$$ Because $w_n^*\\to0$ and $V^2$ is integrable by [F1], the right side tends to zero by [F3]. This is the Lindeberg condition for the array $w_{n,j}V_{n,j}$.",
