          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "The ratio is a weighted average of $1/b_j$: with $p_j(A)=c_jb_j(1-Ab_j)_+/D(A)$, the numbers $p_j(A)$ are nonnegative, sum to one, and $R(A)=\\sum_jp_j(A)/b_j$. Fix $K>0$ and choose $j_0$ so $b_j\\ge K$ for $j\\ge j_0$. The head weight $\\sum_{j<j_0}p_j(A)$ tends to zero as $A\\downarrow0$: its numerator is bounded by the fixed finite sum $\\sum_{j<j_0}c_jb_j$, while for any $M\\ge j_0$ the denominator is at least $\\frac12\\sum_{j=j_0}^M c_jb_j$ once $A b_M\\le1/2$. Since $c_jb_j\\to\\infty$, these finite partial sums can be made arbitrarily large. Also $R(A)\\le b_1^{-1}\\sum_{j<j_0}p_j(A)+1/K$, so $\\limsup_{A\\downarrow0}R(A)\\le1/K$. As $K$ is arbitrary, $R(A)\\to0$.",
