          "claim": "Fix any $\\eta\\in\\Theta_2(r)$ and put $x_j=\\eta_j^2$ and $\\widetilde x_j=\\widetilde\\eta_j^2$. Both series $\\sum_j\\sigma_j^2x_j$ and $\\sum_ja_j^2\\sigma_j^2x_j$ are finite by [F1], so their displayed difference is absolutely convergent. For $j\\notin S$, $1-Aa_j^2\\le0$; since $x_j\\ge0$, $$\\sum_{j\\in S}\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)x_j=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)x_j-\\sum_{j\\notin S}\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)x_j\\ge r^2-A,$$ where the first sum is at least $r^2-A$ by [F1]. For the candidate, the same quantity equals $\\sum_{j\\in S}\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)\\widetilde x_j=r^2-A$ by its two active constraints. Multiplying the inequality by $z_0^2$ therefore gives $\\sum_j\\widetilde x_jx_j\\ge\\sum_j\\widetilde x_j^2$.",
