          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The finite coordinate density of $X_i$ with mean $\\mu_i$ divided by its null density is $\\exp(\\mu_iX_i-\\mu_i^2/2)$. Thus a finite uniform mixture of the two signs at a coordinate of absolute mean $z$ has likelihood $e^{-z^2/2}\\cosh(zX_i)$, and the prior with probabilities $1-g,g/2,g/2$ at $0,z,-z$ has likelihood $1+g\\xi_z(X_i)$, where $\\xi_z(t)=e^{-z^2/2}\\cosh(zt)-1$. Multiplication over finitely many coordinates is justified by [F7]. Off these coordinates the law is unchanged; equality first on finite cylinders and then [F9] proves the likelihood identity on the whole sequence sample space. Every prior below has finite coordinate support; no new countable product probability law is constructed.",
