          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "First suppose $\\lambda>0$ and set $L=qs-2\\beta>0$, $\\kappa=L/(2-q)$. Use [F2] to define the unique positive $n,h_0$ by $$c_1h_0n^{2\\beta+1}=r^2\\varepsilon^{-2},\\qquad c_2h_0n^{qs+1}=\\varepsilon^{-q},\\qquad U^2=c_0h_0^2n,$$ and put $z_i=z(i/n)$, $h_i=h_0d(i/n)$. Then $n^L=(c_1/c_2)\\varepsilon^{2-q}/r^2$. The scalar certificate in [F2] gives $$\\sum_{i\\le m}h_i(\\cosh(z_iv_i)-1)\\ge\\frac{h_0}2\\left(n^{-2\\beta}\\sum_{i\\le m}i^{2\\beta}v_i^2-n^{-qs}\\sum_{i\\le m}i^{qs}|v_i|^q\\right).$$ Also $\\sum h_i<\\infty$ and $V:=\\sum h_i^2(\\cosh(z_i^2)-1)\\sim U^2$ as $n\\to\\infty$. These are score coefficients, which need not be at most one. On compact subsets of $0<r/r_\\varepsilon^*<\\infty$, $U$ stays bounded and bounded away from zero, $n$ grows as a positive power of $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $h_0\\asymp n^{-1/2}$.",
