          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "In the Gaussian branch, with the profile notation of step 1.3, for the Gaussian lower bound, fix a compact transition range. The feasible construction in [F2] can be made with expected energy $(1+\\tau)r^2\\varepsilon^{-2}$ and expected budget $(1-\\tau)\\varepsilon^{-q}$, on a finite window $a\\le i/n'\\le b$, with probabilities $g_i=h'_0d(i/n')\\le1$ and amplitudes $z(i/n')$. Let the margins tend slowly to zero and windows tend slowly to $(0,\\infty)$. This can be specified without a rate assumption: use windows $[1/k,k]$, margin $1/k$, and pass to each next integer $k$ only after its finite Riemann errors, feasibility bounds and the concentration errors below are smaller than the prescribed margins; the preceding compact-window estimates hold uniformly on the chosen compact transition range. The profile moments $M_1,M_2$ give relative variances $O((nh_0)^{-1})=O(n^{-1/2})$ for both random constraint sums before the slowly varying window constants. Choose the numerical diagonal slowly enough that those relative variances divided by $\\tau^2$ tend to zero. Chebyshev then puts prior probability $1-o(1)$ on the actual alternative. Each draw has finite coordinate support and hence is in $\\ell^2$. The prior cost tends to $U^2$, its largest coordinate cost tends to zero, and its cost on $z_i>B$ tends uniformly to zero as $B\\to\\infty$, by the bounded small-end and exponential large-end envelopes in [F2]. Let $\\Pi$ denote this finite-support prior on parameter sequences, let $\\mathcal A$ be the event that a draw satisfies both alternative constraints, and put $p=\\Pi(\\mathcal A)=1-o(1)$. If $p=1$, conditioning changes no mixture law. Otherwise, eventually $0<p<1$ and the unconditioned observation law decomposes as $P_\\Pi=pP_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A}+(1-p)P_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A^c}$. For every measurable event $E$, this gives $|P_\\Pi(E)-P_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A}(E)|=(1-p)|P_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A^c}(E)-P_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A}(E)|\\le1-p$, so [F11] yields $\\|P_\\Pi-P_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A}\\|_{\\rm TV}\\le1-p$. For every randomized test $\\psi$ with values in $[0,1]$, the functions $N^{-1}\\sum_{k=1}^N\\mathbf1_{\\{\\psi\\ge k/N\\}}$ approximate $\\psi$ uniformly within $1/N$; applying the event bound to each summand and letting $N\\to\\infty$ gives $|\\mathbb E_{P_\\Pi}\\psi-\\mathbb E_{P_{\\Pi\\mid\\mathcal A}}\\psi|\\le1-p=o(1)$ uniformly over tests.",
