          "claim": "In the Gaussian branch, with the profile notation of step 1.3, fix $d\\in(0,1)$. Alternatives with $\\mathbb P_\\mu(\\mathcal Y^c)<d$ already have type-II error below $d$. For every other alternative, writing $p_i=\\mathbb P_\\mu(|X_i|>Q_i)$, finite independence gives $\\prod_{i\\le m}(1-p_i)\\ge d$ for each $m$. Hence $\\sum_i p_i\\le-\\log d$ and $|\\mu_i|\\le Q_i+C_d$, the latter because the probability of the inside interval is at most $\\Phi(Q_i-|\\mu_i|)$. Choose $K>(2-q)/(2L)$ and $N=\\lceil n(\\log n)^K\\rceil$. The energy of indices $i>N$ is at most $$\\varepsilon^{-q}\\sup_{i>N}i^{-L}(Q_i+C_d)^{2-q}\\le C_d\\varepsilon^{-q}N^{-L}(\\log N)^{(2-q)/2}=o(r^2\\varepsilon^{-2}).$$ The envelope is eventually decreasing and $\\varepsilon^{-q}n^{-L}$ is a fixed multiple of $r^2\\varepsilon^{-2}$. Among $i\\le N$ delete those with $|\\mu_i|>c\\sqrt{\\log n}$, where $c=0.45\\in(\\sqrt2-1,1/2)$. The lower Mills bound in step 1.2 implies $p_i\\ge n^{-a+o(1)}$ there, with $a=(\\sqrt2-c)^2/2<1/2$, uniformly over these indices. Thus at most $n^{a+o(1)}$ are deleted. Their energy is at most $n^{2\\beta+a+o(1)}=o(n^{2\\beta+1/2})=o(r^2\\varepsilon^{-2})$, including the polynomial logarithmic factors from $N$ and the means. Define $\\mu_i^*=\\mu_i$ for $i\\le N$ not deleted and $\\mu_i^*=0$ for every deleted index and every $i>N$. This vector has finite support, satisfies $|\\mu_i^*|\\le c\\sqrt{\\log n}$, and has no increased $q$-budget. Its energy loss is at most the sum of the already bounded tail energy and deleted-coordinate energy, so its retained energy is $(1-o(1))r^2\\varepsilon^{-2}$. Its score mean $M=\\mathbb E_{\\mu^*}F$ is therefore at least $U^2-o(1)$ by the certificate in step 1.3 and $c_0=(c_1-c_2)/2$. Coordinatewise $|\\mu_i^*|\\le|\\mu_i|$, and $|G+\\mu|$ is stochastically increasing in $|\\mu|$, since $\\Phi(t-|\\mu|)-\\Phi(-t-|\\mu|)$ decreases with $|\\mu|$. Independence and the positive coefficients imply the score comparison for every finite partial sum. The full score event is the decreasing intersection of these partial-sum events after adding the fixed $\\sum h_i$; [F12] passes the comparison to the full nonnegative series, giving $\\mathbb P_\\mu(F\\le t)\\le\\mathbb P_{\\mu^*}(F\\le t)$.",
