          "claim": "In the degenerate branch, with the notation of step 2.4, if $D_\\varepsilon\\to+\\infty$, step 2.4 settles both errors by nonnegativity. If $D_\\varepsilon\\to-\\infty$, fix $K>0$. The continuous strictly increasing function $r\\mapsto\\varepsilon^{-1}r^{s/\\alpha}-\\sqrt{(2/\\alpha)\\log(1/r)}$ ranges from $-\\infty$ to a positive value on $(0,1)$, so it has a unique radius $r_{\\varepsilon,K}$ at value $-K$, and this radius tends to zero. Eventually $r<r_{\\varepsilon,K}$; inclusion of alternative sets and step 3.3 give lower limits at least $\\Phi(K)$ for total error and $(1-\\ell)\\Phi(K)$ for type-II error. Let $K\\to\\infty$. Subsequences in the extended real line now establish the two degenerate formulas for arbitrary $D_\\varepsilon$. Finally put $p=s/\\alpha$ and $\\Lambda=(2/s)^{\\alpha/(2s)}$, so $\\Lambda^p=\\sqrt{2/s}$. For each fixed $c>0$, direct substitution into the displayed $r_\\varepsilon^*$ gives $$D_\\varepsilon(cr_\\varepsilon^*)=(c^p-1)\\sqrt{2\\log(\\varepsilon^{-1})/s}+o(1).$$ The logarithmic correction is $O(\\log\\log(\\varepsilon^{-1})/\\sqrt{\\log(\\varepsilon^{-1})})$, which tends to zero. Thus the stated sharp constant, zero/infinite-ratio separation limits and the assignment of $\\lambda=0$ all follow, with no strict inequality imposed on $e$.",
