    "ex-ip-mildly-illposed-analytic-detection-rate": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "example",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-ip-ellipsoid-detection-boundary",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the minimax detection-risk definition with $q=2$. For each $\\varepsilon$, suppose $\\Theta_2(r_\\varepsilon)$ is nonempty and has a finite-support fourth-power minimizer $\\widetilde\\eta_\\varepsilon$ with profile $\\widetilde\\eta_{\\varepsilon,j}^2=z_0^2\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)_+$ as in the extremal-profile lemma. Put\n$$u_\\varepsilon^2=\\frac{1}{2\\varepsilon^4}\\sum_j\\widetilde\\eta_{\\varepsilon,j}^4,\\qquad w_{\\varepsilon,j}=\\frac{\\widetilde\\eta_{\\varepsilon,j}^2}{\\sqrt{2\\sum_j\\widetilde\\eta_{\\varepsilon,j}^4}},\\qquad w_{\\varepsilon,0}=\\max_jw_{\\varepsilon,j},$$\nand define\n$$T_\\varepsilon=\\sum_jw_{\\varepsilon,j}\\bigl((Y_j/\\varepsilon)^2-1\\bigr),\\qquad \\psi_{\\varepsilon,H}=\\mathbf1_{\\{T_\\varepsilon>H\\}}.$$\nIf $u_\\varepsilon\\to0$, then $\\gamma_\\varepsilon(\\Theta_2(r_\\varepsilon))\\to1$ and $\\beta_\\varepsilon(\\Theta_2(r_\\varepsilon),\\alpha)\\to1-\\alpha$ for every $\\alpha\\in(0,1)$. If $u_\\varepsilon\\to\\infty$, then for every fixed $c\\in(0,1)$ the tests $\\psi_{\\varepsilon,cu_\\varepsilon}$ have total error tending to zero.\n\nFor the extremal profile, define the finite sums\n$$J_1=\\sum_j\\sigma_j^4(1-Aa_j^2)_+,\\quad J_2=A\\sum_ja_j^2\\sigma_j^4(1-Aa_j^2)_+,\\quad J_0=\\sum_j\\sigma_j^4(1-Aa_j^2)_+^2.$$\nThe active constraints imply\n$$r_\\varepsilon^2=A\\frac{J_1}{J_2},\\qquad u_\\varepsilon^2=\\left(\\frac{r_\\varepsilon}{\\varepsilon}\\right)^4\\frac{J_0}{2J_1^2}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-q2-ellipsoid-extremal-profile-exists-for-small-r",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the minimax detection-risk definition with $q=2$, and let $a_j$ and $\\sigma_j$ be positive nondecreasing sequences tending to infinity. There is a constant $r_0>0$ such that for every $r\\in(0,r_0)$ there exist $A,z_0>0$ with finite nonempty $S=\\{j:Aa_j^2<1\\}$ for which\n$$\\widetilde\\eta_j^2=z_0^2\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)_+$$\nsatisfies\n$$\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\sigma_j^2\\widetilde\\eta_j^2=r^2,\\qquad \\sum_{j\\ge1}a_j^2\\sigma_j^2\\widetilde\\eta_j^2=1.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-q2-ellipsoid-extremal-profile-is-minimizer",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the minimax detection-risk definition, $q=2$, and positive nondecreasing sequences $a_j$ and $\\sigma_j$ with $a_j\\to\\infty$. Let $A,z_0>0$ and suppose $S=\\{j:Aa_j^2<1\\}$ is finite and nonempty. Define $\\widetilde\\eta_j^2=z_0^2\\sigma_j^2(1-Aa_j^2)_+$ and assume\n$$\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\sigma_j^2\\widetilde\\eta_j^2=r^2,\\qquad \\sum_{j\\ge1}a_j^2\\sigma_j^2\\widetilde\\eta_j^2=1.$$\nThen $\\widetilde\\eta\\in\\Theta_2(r)$ and minimizes $\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\eta_j^4$ over $\\Theta_2(r)$; the nonnegative minimizer is unique.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1.1",
          "claim": "Let $m$ be the largest active index. Then $A e^{2\\alpha m}<1\\le A e^{2\\alpha(m+1)}$, so $t=Ae^{2\\alpha m}\\in[e^{-2\\alpha},1)$. For $j=m-\\ell$ with $0\\le\\ell<m$, the profile factor is $\\rho_\\ell=1-te^{-2\\alpha\\ell}$. For $\\ell\\ge1$, $1-e^{-2\\alpha}\\le\\rho_\\ell\\le1$. Hence the terms with $1\\le\\ell\\le m/2$ give a lower bound for $J_1$ and $J_0$, while the full sums are bounded above by $\\sum_{j\\le m}j^{4\\beta}\\le m^{4\\beta+1}$. Thus $J_1\\asymp J_0\\asymp m^{4\\beta+1}$. In $J_2$, the summand at $\\ell$ is $t e^{-2\\alpha\\ell}(m-\\ell)^{4\\beta}\\rho_\\ell$. Its sum is at most $m^{4\\beta}\\sum_{\\ell\\ge0}e^{-2\\alpha\\ell}\\le C_\\alpha m^{4\\beta}$, and the $\\ell=1$ term is at least $c_{\\alpha,\\beta}m^{4\\beta}$ for $m\\ge2$. Therefore $J_2\\asymp m^{4\\beta}$. Formula [F1] gives $$r^2\\asymp e^{-2\\alpha m}m,\\qquad u_\\varepsilon^2\\asymp\\varepsilon^{-4}r^4m^{-(4\\beta+1)}.$$ Taking logarithms of the first relation shows $m\\asymp\\log(1/r)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "Set $L=\\log\\varepsilon^{-1}$ and $r_\\varepsilon^*=\\varepsilon L^{\\beta+1/4}$. At this radius, $\\log(1/r_\\varepsilon^*)\\sim L$, so [F1] and step 1.1 give $u_\\varepsilon^2\\asymp1$. If $r_\\varepsilon/r_\\varepsilon^*\\to0$, then $\\log(1/r_\\varepsilon)\\ge\\log(1/r_\\varepsilon^*)\\sim L$ and $$u_\\varepsilon^2\\asymp(r_\\varepsilon/r_\\varepsilon^*)^4\\left(\\frac{\\log(1/r_\\varepsilon^*)}{\\log(1/r_\\varepsilon)}\\right)^{4\\beta+1}\\to0.$$ If $r_\\varepsilon/r_\\varepsilon^*\\to\\infty$ and $r_\\varepsilon\\to0$, the logarithm ratio is at least one, so $u_\\varepsilon\\to\\infty$. If the radii do not tend to zero, use $\\bar r_\\varepsilon=\\sqrt{r_\\varepsilon r_\\varepsilon^*}$. Nonempty alternatives satisfy $r_\\varepsilon^2\\le a_1^{-2}$ because $\\sum_j\\sigma_j^2\\eta_j^2\\le a_1^{-2}\\sum_ja_j^2\\sigma_j^2\\eta_j^2\\le a_1^{-2}$. Hence $\\bar r_\\varepsilon\\to0$, $\\bar r_\\varepsilon/r_\\varepsilon^*\\to\\infty$, and $\\Theta_2(r_\\varepsilon)\\subseteq\\Theta_2(\\bar r_\\varepsilon)$; consistent tests for the larger class work on the original class. The two risk implications in [F1] prove the claimed rate.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
