          "claim": "For each row put $a_{n,j}=\\mathbb E X_{n,j}^2$ and $v_n=\\sum_ja_{n,j}$. Finite products in [F1] allow us to integrate replacement coordinates with independent centered Gaussian laws of variances $a_{n,j}$; a zero variance means the point mass at zero. These products need not be coupled across rows. The supplied scalar density already has integral one, so [F3] gives its scaled probability laws without a separate Gaussian-normalization theorem. For $\\phi(t)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-t^2/2}$, direct differentiation gives $\\phi'(t)=-t\\phi(t)$. On $[-R,R]$, apply [F5] with $(u,v)=(t,\\phi(t))$ and then $(u,v)=(t^3,\\phi(t))$ to obtain\n$$\\int_{-R}^R t^2\\phi(t)\\,dt=[-t\\phi(t)]_{-R}^R+\\int_{-R}^R\\phi(t)\\,dt,$$\n$$\\int_{-R}^R t^4\\phi(t)\\,dt=[-t^3\\phi(t)]_{-R}^R+3\\int_{-R}^R t^2\\phi(t)\\,dt.$$\nThe boundary terms tend to zero by [F6]; [F7] and the supplied normalization $\\int\\phi=1$ then give $\\mathbb E G^2=1$ and $\\mathbb E G^4=3$. Odd moments vanish by symmetry. Consequently a replacement coordinate $G_{n,j}$ has mean zero, variance $a_{n,j}$ and fourth moment $3a_{n,j}^2$.",
