    "lem-ip-planar-isoperimetric-inequality-for-finite-perimeter-sets": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-strict-approximation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For every open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ there is a sequence $u_k\\in C^\\infty(\\Omega)$ such that $u_k\\to u$ in $L^1(\\Omega)$ and $\\int_\\Omega |\\nabla u_k|\\,dx\\to TV(u;\\Omega)$. When $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^n$, the approximating functions may be chosen compactly supported.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-smooth-coarea",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $u\\in C^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)\\cap L^1(\\Omega)$ satisfy $\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla u|\\,dx<\\infty$. For $t>0$ set $E_t^+:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:u(x)>t\\}$ and for $t<0$ set $E_t^-:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:u(x)<t\\}$. Then the level sets have finite perimeter relative to $\\Omega$ for almost every applicable $t$, and\n$$\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla u|\\,dx=\\int_0^\\infty P(E_t^+;\\Omega)\\,dt+\\int_{-\\infty}^0P(E_t^-;\\Omega)\\,dt.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-morse-sard-for-euclidean-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge0$, $n\\ge1$, and $r\\ge1$ be integers, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$\nbe open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be a $C^r$ map with\n\n$$ r>\\max\\{m-n,0\\}. $$\n\nThen the critical value set of $f$ is a null subset of $\\mathbb R^n$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-test-field-characterization-and-lsc",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and real-valued $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, $$TV(u;\\Omega)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi\\,dx:\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n),\\ |\\varphi(x)|\\le1\\right\\}.$$ If $u_k,u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ are real-valued and $u_k\\to u$ in $L^1(\\Omega)$, then $TV(u;\\Omega)\\le\\liminf_kTV(u_k;\\Omega)$. In addition, assuming Countable Choice, for any real-valued $u\\in L^1(\\Omega)$ the displayed supremum is finite if and only if $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, with equality to $TV(u;\\Omega)$; under the same assumption, an $L^1$ limit of real-valued BV functions with bounded liminf total variation belongs to $BV(\\Omega)$ and satisfies the same lower-semicontinuity inequality.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-greens-theorem-from-general-stokes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For a compact smooth planar region $D$ oriented by $dx\\wedge dy$ and smooth $P,Q$ on a neighborhood, general Stokes gives\n$$\\int_{\\partial D}(P\\,dx+Q\\,dy)=\\int_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dx\\,dy,$$\n$$\\int_{\\partial D}(P\\,dy-Q\\,dx)=\\int_D(P_x+Q_y)\\,dx\\,dy.$$\nWhen $D$ also has the supplied finite elementary Green decomposition required by the classical results, these are exactly their circulation and outward-flux formulas. Outer boundary curves run counterclockwise and holes clockwise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-ip-vector-radon-variation-is-radon",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_m)$ have finite signed Radon coordinate measures. For Borel $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ define $$V(A):=\\sup\\left\\{\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty |\\mu(A_j)|:(A_j)_{j\\ge1}\\text{ is a Borel partition of }A\\right\\},$$ where the norm is Euclidean. Then $V$ is a finite positive Radon measure and $$V(\\Omega)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega f\\cdot d\\mu:f\\in C_c(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^m),\\ |f(x)|\\le1\\right\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-a-compact-connected-one-dimensional-manifold-without-boundary-is-a-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $X$ be a nonempty\ncompact connected one-dimensional topological manifold without boundary\n([[def-compact-space]], [[def-connected-space]],\n[[def-hausdorff-space]]). Then $X$ is homeomorphic to the circle\n$S^1=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$\n([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]). If in addition $X$ carries a\nsmooth structure making it a smooth one-manifold without boundary, then $X$ is\ndiffeomorphic to this circle\n([[def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds]]).\n\nThe empty manifold is excluded by the hypothesis: it is compact and connected\nunder the conventions of this library, but it is not homeomorphic to a circle.\nThe hypothesis \"without boundary\" is likewise essential: the closed interval\n$[0,1]$ is compact and connected but has boundary points and is not a circle.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-parseval-identity-for-fourier-series",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). For all\n$f,g\\in L^2(\\mathbb T;\\mathbb C)$,\n\n$$\\|f\\|_2^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|\\widehat f(k)|^2,\\qquad \\langle f,g\\rangle=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\\widehat f(k)\\overline{\\widehat g(k)} ,$$where both sums are taken in the finite-subset-supremum/finite-subset-net sense of [[def-square-summable-family-on-an-arbitrary-index-set]]. The bilinear series is absolutely convergent,$$\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}\\bigl|\\widehat f(k)\\overline{\\widehat g(k)}\\bigr|\\le \\Bigl(\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|\\widehat f(k)|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2} \\Bigl(\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|\\widehat g(k)|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2},$$\n\nand the value is also the limit of the canonical symmetric partial sums\n$\\sum_{|k|\\le N}\\widehat f(k)\\overline{\\widehat g(k)}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "First suppose $G\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ is a bounded open set with compact smooth boundary $\\Gamma$. The boundary is a compact smooth one-dimensional manifold without boundary. Connected coordinate arcs cover $\\Gamma$; compactness gives a finite subcover, so $\\Gamma$ has finitely many connected components. By [F5] each component is a smooth embedded circle. Parametrize component $i$ by an orientation-compatible unit-speed smooth periodic curve $\\gamma_i:[0,L_i]\\to\\mathbb R^2$, where $L_i$ is its length. The divergence formula in [F3] gives $D\\chi_G=-\\nu\\,\\mathcal H^1|_\\Gamma$ with $\\nu$ the outward normal, and [F4] therefore gives $P(G;\\mathbb R^2)=\\mathcal H^1(\\Gamma)=\\sum_iL_i$. Applying Stokes in [F3] to $\\omega=(x\\,dy-y\\,dx)/2$, whose exterior derivative is $dx\\wedge dy$, gives\n$$|G|=\\frac12\\sum_i\\int_0^{L_i}(\\gamma_{i,1}\\gamma'_{i,2}-\\gamma_{i,2}\\gamma'_{i,1})\\,ds.$$\nFor each $i$, translating $\\gamma_i$ by its arclength mean $\\bar\\gamma_i=L_i^{-1}\\int_0^{L_i}\\gamma_i(s)\\,ds$ does not change this integral, since each coordinate derivative integrates to zero over a period. Write $z_i=\\gamma_i-\\bar\\gamma_i$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "claim": "For a smooth $L$-periodic scalar function $z$ of mean zero, set $v(\\theta)=z(L\\theta/(2\\pi))$. Its zeroth Fourier coefficient vanishes. Integration by parts gives $\\widehat{v'}(k)=ik\\widehat v(k)$ for every integer $k$. Parseval [F6] and $k^2\\ge1$ for $k\\ne0$ imply $\\int_0^{2\\pi}|v|^2\\,d\\theta\\le\\int_0^{2\\pi}|v'|^2\\,d\\theta$. Changing variables yields\n$$\\int_0^L|z(s)|^2\\,ds\\le\\left(\\frac L{2\\pi}\\right)^2\\int_0^L|z'(s)|^2\\,ds.$$\nApply this to both coordinates of $z_i$ and add. Since $|z'_i|=|\\gamma'_i|=1$, the Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality gives\n$$\\left|\\frac12\\int_0^{L_i}(z_{i,1}z'_{i,2}-z_{i,2}z'_{i,1})\\,ds\\right|\\le\\frac12\\|z_i\\|_{L^2}\\|z'_i\\|_{L^2}\\le\\frac{L_i^2}{4\\pi}.$$\nSumming over the finitely many boundary components and using $\\sum_iL_i^2\\le(\\sum_iL_i)^2$ proves $4\\pi|G|\\le P(G;\\mathbb R^2)^2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
