    "lem-ip-tv-l2-cutoff-approximation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-tv-functional-on-l2-distributional-variation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be open and let $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. Every $u\\in X$ is integrable on compact subsets by Cauchy\u2013Schwarz. Define its extended distributional total variation by\n$$TV_2(u;\\Omega):=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi\\,dx:\\ \\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2),\\ \\sup_{x\\in\\Omega}|\\varphi(x)|\\le1\\right\\}\\in[0,+\\infty],$$\nwhere $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2)$ denotes smooth compactly supported vector fields, $|\\cdot|$ is the Euclidean norm, and $\\operatorname{div}\\varphi=\\partial_1\\varphi_1+\\partial_2\\varphi_2$. This definition treats $L^2$ functions as almost-everywhere classes and does not require $u\\in L^1(\\Omega)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-tv-l2-finite-variation-has-radon-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. If $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ is open, $u\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$, and $TV_2(u;\\Omega)<\\infty$, then each distributional derivative of $u$ is represented by a finite signed Radon measure. Their vector measure $Du$ has finite Radon variation and $|Du|(\\Omega)=TV_2(u;\\Omega)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], choose $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$, $\\chi=1$ on $B(0,1)$, $\\operatorname{supp}\\chi\\subset B(0,2)$, and let $\\chi_R(x)=\\chi(x/R)$ for integers $R\\ge1$. This proves existence of a family with $\\sup_R R\\|\\nabla\\chi_R\\|_\\infty<\\infty$. Now fix any family satisfying all four conditions in the statement, write $C=\\sup_R R\\|\\nabla\\chi_R\\|_\\infty$, and put $v_R=\\chi_Ru$. Then $v_R\\in L^2$ has compact support, hence is in $L^1$, and $v_R\\to u$ in $L^2$ because $\\chi_R=1$ on $B(0,R)$ and $u\\in L^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "choose",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "For every scalar $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$, local integrability of $u$ and the definition of distributional derivative give $$-\\int \\chi_Ru\\,\\partial_i\\psi=-\\int u\\,\\partial_i(\\chi_R\\psi)+\\int u\\psi\\,\\partial_i\\chi_R=\\int\\chi_R\\psi\\,dD_i u+\\int u\\psi\\,\\partial_i\\chi_R.$$ Thus $D v_R=\\chi_RDu+u\\nabla\\chi_R\\,dx$. The variation of the first term is at most $|Du|(\\mathbb R^2)$, and the second has variation at most $$\\frac CR\\int_{B(0,2R)\\setminus B(0,R)}|u|\\,dx\\le C'\\|u\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb R^2\\setminus B(0,R))},$$ by Cauchy\u2013Schwarz and the annulus area bound $|B(0,2R)\\setminus B(0,R)|^{1/2}\\le C''R$. This error tends to zero. Since $v_R\\in L^1$ and its derivative is a finite vector Radon measure, $v_R\\in BV(\\mathbb R^2)$, and [F1] yields $\\limsup_R TV(v_R;\\mathbb R^2)\\le |Du|(\\mathbb R^2)=TV_2(u;\\mathbb R^2)$. The estimate applies to the arbitrary controlled family fixed in step 1.1 and proves the statement.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
