          "claim": "Write $p_n=p_{\\alpha_n,0}$. Maximality against $p_0\\in P_0$ and the support bound $\\langle f,p_n\\rangle_Y\\le R(u^\\dagger)$ give $\\|p_n\\|_Y\\le\\|p_0\\|_Y$ and $\\langle f,p_n\\rangle_Y\\to R(u^\\dagger)$. The closed span of any subsequence is separable; Gram\u2013Schmidt on rational finite linear combinations of its terms gives a finite or countable orthonormal family with dense span. Repeated use of [F8] and [A1] selects a further subsequence whose coordinates all converge. For each finite initial segment, expanding the nonnegative squared norm after orthogonal projection shows that the sum of squared limiting coordinates is at most $\\|p_0\\|_Y^2$; hence the coordinates define a vector $p$ in that closed span. Approximation by finite linear combinations proves weak convergence there, and [F9] represents each functional $x\\mapsto\\langle x,y\\rangle_Y$ restricted to the span, so convergence is weak in $Y$. The limit lies in $K$: by [F2], $K=\\bigcap_{z\\in X}\\{q:\\langle q,Az\\rangle_Y\\le R(z)\\}$, an intersection of weakly closed half-spaces. It also attains $\\langle f,p\\rangle_Y=R(u^\\dagger)$, so $p\\in P_0$. Weak lower semicontinuity of the norm and the bound force $\\|p\\|_Y=\\|p_0\\|_Y$, hence uniqueness gives $p=p_0$. Every subsequence therefore has a further subsequence weakly converging to $p_0$; testing against any fixed $y\\in Y$ shows that every scalar subsequence has a further subsequence converging to $\\langle p_0,y\\rangle_Y$, so $\\langle p_n,y\\rangle_Y\\to\\langle p_0,y\\rangle_Y$. Thus $p_n\\rightharpoonup p_0$. Weak lower semicontinuity and the uniform upper bound then give $\\|p_n\\|_Y\\to\\|p_0\\|_Y$. The norm identity [F10] yields strong convergence, and boundedness of $A^*$ yields strong convergence of $A^*p_n$.",
