    "lem-ip-tv-l2-coarea-formula": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-tv-l2-cutoff-approximation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. If $u\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb R)$ and $TV_2(u;\\mathbb R^2)<\\infty$, there exist smooth cutoffs $\\chi_k$ with $0\\le\\chi_k\\le1$, $\\chi_k=1$ on $B(0,k)$, support in $B(0,2k)$, and $\\sup_{k\\ge1}k\\|\\nabla\\chi_k\\|_\\infty<\\infty$. For every family satisfying these four conditions, $v_k:=\\chi_ku$ belongs to $BV(\\mathbb R^2)\\cap L^1(\\mathbb R^2)$, has support in $B(0,2k)$, converges to $u$ in $L^2(\\mathbb R^2)$, and\n$$\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}TV(v_k;\\mathbb R^2)\\le TV_2(u;\\mathbb R^2).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-coarea-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, and for $t>0$ set $E_t^+:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:u(x)>t\\}$ while for $t<0$ set $E_t^-:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:u(x)<t\\}$. Then for almost every applicable $t$ these sets have finite perimeter relative to $\\Omega$, and $$TV(u;\\Omega)=\\int_0^\\infty P(E_t^+;\\Omega)\\,dt+\\int_{-\\infty}^0P(E_t^-;\\Omega)\\,dt.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-test-field-characterization-and-lsc",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and real-valued $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, $$TV(u;\\Omega)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi\\,dx:\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n),\\ |\\varphi(x)|\\le1\\right\\}.$$ If $u_k,u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ are real-valued and $u_k\\to u$ in $L^1(\\Omega)$, then $TV(u;\\Omega)\\le\\liminf_kTV(u_k;\\Omega)$. In addition, assuming Countable Choice, for any real-valued $u\\in L^1(\\Omega)$ the displayed supremum is finite if and only if $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, with equality to $TV(u;\\Omega)$; under the same assumption, an $L^1$ limit of real-valued BV functions with bounded liminf total variation belongs to $BV(\\Omega)$ and satisfies the same lower-semicontinuity inequality.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-and-fubini-for-completed-product-measures",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), and let\n$\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu}$ be the completed product of two sigma-finite\nmeasure spaces.\n\n1. If $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ is $\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu}$-measurable, there are measurable null sets $A\\subseteq X$ and $B\\subseteq Y$ such that $f_x$ is $\\overline{\\mathcal B}$-measurable for $x\\notin A$ and $f^y$ is $\\overline{\\mathcal A}$-measurable for $y\\notin B$. Define $I_f(x)=\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\overline\\nu$ for $x\\notin A$ and $I_f(x)=0$ on $A$; define $J_f(y)=\\int_X f^y\\,d\\overline\\mu$ for $y\\notin B$ and $J_f(y)=0$ on $B$. Then $I_f,J_f$ are measurable and\n$$\\int f\\,d\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu}=\\int_X I_f\\,d\\mu=\\int_Y J_f\\,d\\nu.$$\n2. If $f \\in L^1(\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu})$, there are measurable null sets $A\\subseteq X$ and $B\\subseteq Y$ such that the sections are measurable and integrable outside them. Define $I_f,J_f$ by the same section integrals outside $A,B$ and by zero on $A,B$. They are measurable and integrable, and the same equality holds.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fatou-lemma",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(f_n)$ be nonnegative measurable functions. Then\n$$\\int \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}f_n\\,d\\mu\\le\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\int f_n\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. Let $f_n:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be measurable for every $n\\ge1$, with $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$, and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], choose compactly supported $v_R\\in BV(\\mathbb R^2)\\cap L^1$ with $v_R\\to u$ in $L^2$ and $\\limsup_RTV(v_R)\\le TV_2(u;\\mathbb R^2)$. For each fixed $t\\ne0$, $\\chi_{E_{R,t}}\\to\\chi_{E_t}$ in $L^1$: the symmetric difference is outside $B(0,R)$ for the cutoffs constructed in [F1] and contained in $\\{|u|>|t|\\}$, whose tail measure is at most $|t|^{-2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^2\\setminus B(0,R)}|u|^2$. Here $E_{R,t}$ and $E_t$ are the corresponding signed superlevel or sublevel sets.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
