          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain, meaning a bounded open set whose boundary has a finite cover by coordinate neighborhoods in which it is the graph of a Lipschitz function and $\\Omega$ lies on one side of that graph. For $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ define the Neumann total variation by $TV_N(u;\\Omega):=|Du|(\\Omega)$. Define its zero extension $\\widetilde u\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n)$ by $\\widetilde u=u$ almost everywhere on $\\Omega$ and $\\widetilde u=0$ almost everywhere on $\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\Omega$. Define the Dirichlet total variation by $TV_D(u;\\Omega):=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^n)$ when $\\widetilde u\\in BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ and by $TV_D(u;\\Omega):=+\\infty$ otherwise. For $v\\in BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ define the full-space total variation by $TV_F(v):=TV(v;\\mathbb R^n)$. Thus Neumann variation counts only the distributional derivative inside $\\Omega$, while Dirichlet variation also counts any jump created by zero extension across $\\partial\\Omega$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-test-field-characterization-and-lsc",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and real-valued $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, $$TV(u;\\Omega)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi\\,dx:\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n),\\ |\\varphi(x)|\\le1\\right\\}.$$ If $u_k,u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ are real-valued and $u_k\\to u$ in $L^1(\\Omega)$, then $TV(u;\\Omega)\\le\\liminf_kTV(u_k;\\Omega)$. In addition, assuming Countable Choice, for any real-valued $u\\in L^1(\\Omega)$ the displayed supremum is finite if and only if $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$, with equality to $TV(u;\\Omega)$; under the same assumption, an $L^1$ limit of real-valued BV functions with bounded liminf total variation belongs to $BV(\\Omega)$ and satisfies the same lower-semicontinuity inequality.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-ip-tv-l2-finite-variation-has-radon-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. If $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ is open, $u\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$, and $TV_2(u;\\Omega)<\\infty$, then each distributional derivative of $u$ is represented by a finite signed Radon measure. Their vector measure $Du$ has finite Radon variation and $|Du|(\\Omega)=TV_2(u;\\Omega)$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-bolzano-weierstrass",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every bounded sequence of reals has a convergent subsequence: if $(x_k)$ is a\nsequence of reals and there is $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \\le M$ for every\n$k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ ([[def-sequence]]), then there is a strictly increasing\n$n : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ and a real $L$ with $x_{n_j} \\to L$.\n\nEquivalently: the subsequential limit set of a bounded sequence is nonempty\n([[def-subsequential-limit]]).\n\nThe theorem is the exact repair of the false claim that a bounded sequence\nconverges. A bounded\nsequence need not converge, and the alternating sequence is the standing witness;\nwhat boundedness does force is that *some* subsequence converges. The converse of\nthe theorem is false, and badly so: a sequence with a convergent subsequence need\nnot be bounded.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all vectors $x,y$ in a real or complex inner-product space,\n\n$$|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\| ,$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $x$ and $y$ are linearly dependent.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a real or complex Hilbert space and let $f$ be a bounded linear functional on $H$ (The dual space X^* of a normed space and its dual norm). Then there is a unique $y\\in H$ with\n\n$$f(x)=\\langle x,y\\rangle\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in H,$$\n\nand $\\|f\\|=\\|y\\|$, where $\\|f\\|$ is the dual norm (The operator norm as the least bound and as the unit-sphere or unit-ball supremum). Under the first-variable-linear convention the representing vector depends conjugate-linearly and isometrically on $f$: if $f_i$ is represented by $y_i$ and $a,b$ are scalars, then $af_1+bf_2$ is represented by $\\overline a\\,y_1+\\overline b\\,y_2$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. The map $f\\mapsto u_f$, $u_f(\\varphi)=\\int_\\Omega f\\varphi$, is a complex-linear injection from $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$ modulo almost-everywhere equality into $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$. It is continuous from the local $L^1$ topology, generated by $f\\mapsto\\int_K|f|$ for compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$, to the strong distribution topology. In particular local $L^1$ convergence implies strong distribution convergence. The continuity estimate itself is choice-free; Countable Choice is used in the cited $L^1$ approximate-identity theorem proving injectivity.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
