          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Suppose $z_n\\to z_\\infty$ in $L^2(U;\\mathbb R)$ and $v_n=z_n+e_n$ with real-valued $e_n\\in L^2(U;\\mathbb R)$ satisfying $\\|e_n\\|_2\\le\\eta$. Include $v_\\infty=z_\\infty$ with zero perturbation if desired. For each index, either sign $\\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, and any finite-measure finite-selected-perimeter set $E\\subseteq U$ globally minimizing the selected perimeter minus $\\int_E\\sigma v_n$, use its ambient density-one representative $E^{(1)}$ and ambient boundary. The selected branch is full-space perimeter on $U=\\mathbb R^2$; zero-extension Dirichlet perimeter on bounded Lipschitz $U=\\Omega$ with the obstacle curvature and exterior lower bound $g\\in L^2(\\Omega^c;\\mathbb R)$; or relative Neumann perimeter on bounded connected Lipschitz $U=\\Omega$ with specified $C_\\Omega$ satisfying $\\|u-u_\\Omega\\|_2\\le C_\\Omega TV(u;\\Omega)$.\n\nIf $\\eta<2\\sqrt\\pi$ in the full-space and Dirichlet branches, or $\\eta<1/C_\\Omega$ in the Neumann branch, there exist $c,r_0>0$, independent of the index, sign and minimizing set, such that\n$$|E\\cap B(x,r)|\\ge c\\pi r^2,\\qquad |B(x,r)\\setminus E|\\ge c\\pi r^2\\quad(x\\in\\partial E^{(1)},\\ 0<r<r_0).$$\nAll the full-space representatives and their boundaries lie in one common closed ball; in bounded branches they lie in $\\overline\\Omega$. The full-space sets also have uniform area and perimeter bounds. These conclusions include the limiting field and the empty set. They require no connected-component or Jordan decomposition of finite-perimeter sets.",
