    "thm-ip-2d-tv-level-set-convergence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-tv-vanishing-noise-primal-compactness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and let $Y=L^2(\\Sigma,\\mu;\\mathbb R)$ be real Hilbert spaces. Let $A:X\\to Y$ be bounded and real-linear, let $f=Au^\\dagger$, and assume $u^\\dagger$ is an exact solution minimizing a proper, convex, lower semicontinuous, positively one-homogeneous TV functional $R:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ with a source certificate $p^\\dagger\\in Y$, so $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u^\\dagger)$. Use full-space $R=TV_2(\\cdot;\\mathbb R^2)$ if $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$; for a bounded connected Lipschitz $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$, use either Neumann $R(u)=TV(u;\\Omega)$ or Dirichlet $R(u)=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^2)$ for the zero extension $\\widetilde u$, assigning $+\\infty$ outside the respective finite-variation domain. A bounded connected Lipschitz domain here is a bounded open set with a finite Lipschitz graph cover of its boundary, with the domain on one side of each graph, and it has no separation into two disjoint nonempty relatively open sets.\n\nLet $\\alpha_n>0$ tend to zero, let $w_n\\in Y$, and suppose $\\|A^*\\|\\,\\|w_n\\|_Y/\\alpha_n\\le\\eta$ for some finite $\\eta$. For each $n$, suppose a supplied global minimizer $u_n\\in X$ minimizes\n$$J_n(u)=\\tfrac12\\|Au-(f+w_n)\\|_Y^2+\\alpha_nR(u).$$\nIn the Neumann case, if $A\\mathbf1_\\Omega=0$, take $u_n$ after subtracting its mean $|\\Omega|^{-1}\\int_\\Omega u_n$; this leaves $J_n$ unchanged. Then a subsequence converges weakly in $L^2(\\Omega)$ and, in addition, strongly in $L^1(\\Omega)$ when $\\Omega$ is bounded or strongly in $L^1$ on every bounded ball when $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$. Its limit $u_0$ is an exact solution $Au_0=f$ minimizing $R$ among exact solutions, and $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u_0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ip-tv-level-set-representatives-and-boundaries",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $u\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb R)$, with functions identified when they agree almost everywhere. For full-space TV, use $u$ itself. For a bounded-domain Dirichlet or Neumann problem, use the zero extension $\\widetilde u$ only to define the geometric level sets; this does not change the selected regularizer, and for each nonzero level the resulting set lies inside $\\Omega$. For $t>0$, the positive $t$-level class is the measurable-set class of $E_t^+(u):=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:u(x)>t\\}$; for $t<0$, the negative $t$-level class is the class of $E_t^-(u):=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:u(x)<t\\}$. These classes do not depend on the representative of $u$. For any measurable set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$, its density-one representative is\n\n$$E^{(1)}:=\\left\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\frac{|E\\cap B(x,r)|}{|B(x,r)|}=1\\right\\}.$$\n\nThis representative depends only on the almost-everywhere class of $E$. The canonical level set $U_u^{(t)}$ is $(E_t^+(u))^{(1)}$ when $t>0$ and $(E_t^-(u))^{(1)}$ when $t<0$. Its canonical boundary is the ordinary topological boundary $\\partial U_u^{(t)}$ in $\\mathbb R^2$. For two nonempty boundary sets, use the usual extended Hausdorff distance induced by Euclidean distance; set $d_H(\\varnothing,\\varnothing)=0$ and $d_H(E,F)=+\\infty$ if exactly one set is empty. Hausdorff convergence uses this convention and is invariant under changes to a function on a null set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-and-fubini-for-completed-product-measures",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), and let\n$\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu}$ be the completed product of two sigma-finite\nmeasure spaces.\n\n1. If $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ is $\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu}$-measurable, there are measurable null sets $A\\subseteq X$ and $B\\subseteq Y$ such that $f_x$ is $\\overline{\\mathcal B}$-measurable for $x\\notin A$ and $f^y$ is $\\overline{\\mathcal A}$-measurable for $y\\notin B$. Define $I_f(x)=\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\overline\\nu$ for $x\\notin A$ and $I_f(x)=0$ on $A$; define $J_f(y)=\\int_X f^y\\,d\\overline\\mu$ for $y\\notin B$ and $J_f(y)=0$ on $B$. Then $I_f,J_f$ are measurable and\n$$\\int f\\,d\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu}=\\int_X I_f\\,d\\mu=\\int_Y J_f\\,d\\nu.$$\n2. If $f \\in L^1(\\overline{\\mu \\times \\nu})$, there are measurable null sets $A\\subseteq X$ and $B\\subseteq Y$ such that the sections are measurable and integrable outside them. Define $I_f,J_f$ by the same section integrals outside $A,B$ and by zero on $A,B$. They are measurable and integrable, and the same equality holds.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-bolzano-weierstrass",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every bounded sequence of reals has a convergent subsequence: if $(x_k)$ is a\nsequence of reals and there is $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \\le M$ for every\n$k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ ([[def-sequence]]), then there is a strictly increasing\n$n : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ and a real $L$ with $x_{n_j} \\to L$.\n\nEquivalently: the subsequential limit set of a bounded sequence is nonempty\n([[def-subsequential-limit]]).\n\nThe theorem is the exact repair of the false claim that a bounded sequence\nconverges. A bounded\nsequence need not converge, and the alternating sequence is the standing witness;\nwhat boundedness does force is that *some* subsequence converges. The converse of\nthe theorem is false, and badly so: a sequence with a convergent subsequence need\nnot be bounded.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-ip-tv-level-set-uniform-density-and-hausdorff",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $A:L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)\\to L^2(\\Sigma,\\mu;\\mathbb R)$ be bounded and real-linear, let $f=Au^\\dagger$, and suppose $u^\\dagger$ is a minimum-TV exact solution with a source certificate $p^\\dagger$. For $\\alpha_n>0$ tending to zero and noises $w_n\\in L^2(\\Sigma,\\mu;\\mathbb R)$, assume $\\|A^*\\|\\|w_n\\|_2/\\alpha_n\\le\\eta$ and let $u_n$ be supplied global minimizers of $\\frac12\\|Au-(f+w_n)\\|_2^2+\\alpha_n TV(u)$. Define $p_n=(f+w_n-Au_n)/\\alpha_n$ and $v_n=A^*p_n$. Use $TV_2$ on $L^2(\\mathbb R^2)$ for full-space TV, zero-extension Dirichlet TV on a bounded Lipschitz domain, and relative Neumann TV on a bounded connected Lipschitz domain; for bounded-domain geometry use the ambient zero-extended level sets and their topological boundaries in $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nFor full-space or Dirichlet TV, assume $\\eta<2\\sqrt\\pi$; in the bounded Dirichlet case also assume $\\Omega$ has global obstacle curvature $\\kappa_\\Omega\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb R)$ with $\\kappa_\\Omega\\ge g$ almost everywhere outside $\\Omega$ for some real-valued $g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. For Neumann TV, let $C_\\Omega$ be a constant from the BV Sobolev\u2013Poincar\u00e9 inequality $\\|u-u_\\Omega\\|_2\\le C_\\Omega TV(u;\\Omega)$ and assume $\\eta<1/C_\\Omega$.\n\nFor almost every nonzero $t$, the level set $E_n(t)$ has finite measure and perimeter, minimizes the branch-specific perimeter minus $\\int_{E}\\operatorname{sgn}(t)v_n$ under compactly supported perturbations, and satisfies $P(E_n(t))=\\operatorname{sgn}(t)\\int_{E_n(t)}v_n$. There are constants $c>0$ and $r_0>0$, independent of $n$ and almost every such $t$, such that for every $x\\in\\partial E_n(t)$ and $0<r<r_0$,\n$$|B(x,r)\\cap E_n(t)|\\ge c|B(x,r)|,\\qquad |B(x,r)\\setminus E_n(t)|\\ge c|B(x,r)|.$$\nIn the full-space branch all these level sets are contained in one common ball; in the bounded branches they lie in $\\overline\\Omega$. The same density and support conclusions are asserted for any selected minimum-TV exact limit that carries the inherited source certificate, using zero noise. Whenever the corresponding level sets converge in measure, their canonical boundaries converge in Hausdorff distance.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] to the supplied minimizers, retaining its subsequence, and call its limit $u_0$. Then $Au_0=f$, $R(u_0)$ is minimal among exact solutions, and $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u_0)$. In bounded domains the subsequence already converges strongly in $L^1(\\Omega)$. In full space, [F5] supplies a common ball containing the regularized and limit level sets for almost every nonzero level. By [F2] and the positive and negative layer-cake identities, for each fixed $n$ the integral of $|u_n|$ outside that ball is the integral over levels of the measures of those level sets outside it; Tonelli [F3] makes this integral zero. The same argument applies to $u_0$, so local $L^1$ convergence from [F1] is global $L^1$ convergence.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
