          "source": "def-ip-boundary-tv-conventions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain, meaning a bounded open set whose boundary has a finite cover by coordinate neighborhoods in which it is the graph of a Lipschitz function and $\\Omega$ lies on one side of that graph. For $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ define the Neumann total variation by $TV_N(u;\\Omega):=|Du|(\\Omega)$. Define its zero extension $\\widetilde u\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n)$ by $\\widetilde u=u$ almost everywhere on $\\Omega$ and $\\widetilde u=0$ almost everywhere on $\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\Omega$. Define the Dirichlet total variation by $TV_D(u;\\Omega):=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^n)$ when $\\widetilde u\\in BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ and by $TV_D(u;\\Omega):=+\\infty$ otherwise. For $v\\in BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ define the full-space total variation by $TV_F(v):=TV(v;\\mathbb R^n)$. Thus Neumann variation counts only the distributional derivative inside $\\Omega$, while Dirichlet variation also counts any jump created by zero extension across $\\partial\\Omega$.",
          "uses": [
