    "lem-maximal-abelian-cover-link-exterior-retraction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-maximal-abelian-cover-alexander-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be a nonempty oriented smooth link with $r\\ge1$ components, and let $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$ be its open complement. For each component choose a smooth fibrewise norm on its normal plane bundle $\\nu(K_i)$ and a tubular map $\\Phi_i$ from an open neighborhood of the zero section, fixing the zero section, together with a radius $\\epsilon_i>0$ whose closed disk subbundle lies in that neighborhood. Require the resulting closed tubes $N(K_i)=\\Phi_i(D_{\\epsilon_i}\\nu(K_i))$ to be pairwise disjoint, and put $N(L)=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rN(K_i)$ and $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}_{S^3}N(L)$, the resulting compact smooth $3$-manifold with boundary. The tube choices are part of the input. Choose a basepoint $b\\in E_L\\subset X_L$. Orient each normal disk so that the positive tangent to $K_i$ followed by its positive normal basis agrees with the fixed orientation of $S^3$. A **positive meridian** $\\mu_i$ of $K_i$ is the oriented boundary of the radius-$\\epsilon_i$ normal disk in one fibre of $\\nu(K_i)$; the curve lies on $\\partial E_L$ and is disjoint from every component of $L$; its spanning normal disk meets $K_i$ transversely once with positive sign and misses every other component. Alexander duality identifies $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\mathbb Z^r$ and identifies the classes of $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r$ with its standard basis. Write $G=\\pi_1(X_L,b)$ and let $\\phi:G\\to H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ be the first Hurewicz homomorphism, equivalently abelianization. The subgroup $\\ker\\phi$ is normal. The connected covering $p:X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\phi$ is the **maximal abelian cover**; it is regular and its deck group is identified with $G/\\ker\\phi\\cong H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$. Put $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)]\\cong\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$, where $t_i$ is the deck transformation corresponding to $[\\mu_i]$. The isomorphism sends each variable to its group-ring basis element; monomials $t_1^{n_1}\\cdots t_r^{n_r}$ correspond bijectively to the basis elements indexed by $n_1[\\mu_1]+\\cdots+n_r[\\mu_r]$. Define the absolute multivariable Alexander module by\n\n$$A_L=H_1(X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}};\\mathbb Z),$$\n\nas a left $\\Lambda_r$-module under the deck action. \u201cAbsolute\u201d means this is ordinary homology of the covering space, not relative homology with respect to a fibre over the basepoint.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-homotopy-lifting-for-covering-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p:E\\to B$ be a covering, $H:Y\\times I\\to B$ a homotopy, and $\\widetilde H_0:Y\\to E$ a lift of $H(-,0)$. There is a unique lift $\\widetilde H:Y\\times I\\to E$ of $H$ extending $\\widetilde H_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-path-lifting-for-covering-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p:E\\to B$ be a covering, let $\\alpha:I\\to B$ be a path, and let $e_0\\in E$ satisfy $p(e_0)=\\alpha(0)$. There is a unique path $\\widetilde\\alpha:I\\to E$ with $\\widetilde\\alpha(0)=e_0$ and $p\\circ\\widetilde\\alpha=\\alpha$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-classification-of-connected-covering-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $B$ be nonempty, path-connected, locally path-connected, and semilocally simply connected, fix $b_0\\in B$, and put $G=\\pi_1(B,b_0)$.\n\n1. The assignment $$[p:(E,e_0)\\to(B,b_0)]\\longmapsto p_*\\pi_1(E,e_0)$$ is a bijection from based-isomorphism classes of based connected coverings of $B$ to subgroups of $G$.\n2. After forgetting the chosen point in the fibre, the assignment to the conjugacy class of $p_*\\pi_1(E,e_0)$ is a bijection from isomorphism classes of connected coverings of $B$ to conjugacy classes of subgroups of $G$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-continuity-is-local-and-pastes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$, $Y$ and $Z$ be topological spaces, with subspaces carrying the subspace\ntopology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). Then:\n\n1. **Composites.** If $f : X \\to Y$ and $g : Y \\to Z$ are continuous\n   ([[def-continuous-map-top]]) then $g \\circ f : X \\to Z$ is continuous.\n2. **Open cover.** Let $f : X \\to Y$ be a function and let\n   $\\{\\, U_i : i \\in I \\,\\}$ be a family of open subsets of $X$ with\n   $\\bigcup_{i \\in I} U_i = X$. If $f|_{U_i} : U_i \\to Y$ is continuous for every\n   $i \\in I$, then $f$ is continuous.\n3. **Finite closed cover.** Let $f : X \\to Y$ be a function, let $n \\ge 1$ and\n   let $F_1, \\dots, F_n$ be closed subsets of $X$ with\n   $F_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup F_n = X$. If $f|_{F_k} : F_k \\to Y$ is continuous for\n   every $k$, then $f$ is continuous.\n\nThe converses of claims 2 and 3 hold with no hypothesis on the cover at all:\nevery restriction of a continuous map to a subspace is continuous\n([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). The finiteness in claim 3 is not removable; see\nthe remarks.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-the-complement-of-an-oriented-link-is-a-connected-smooth-three-manifold",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be an\noriented link (a finite disjoint union of oriented smoothly embedded circles,\n[[def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy]]) with $r\\ge1$ components,\nand let $X_L:=S^3\\setminus L$. Then $X_L$ is a connected smooth $3$-manifold\nwithout boundary, and in particular is path-connected, locally path-connected\nand semilocally simply connected; moreover $X_L$ has the homotopy type of a\nfinite CW complex\n([[def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology]]). Consequently the\ncovering-space classification\n([[thm-classification-of-connected-covering-spaces]]) applies to $X_L$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-regular-covering-characterizations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p:(E,e_0)\\to(B,b_0)$ be a covering with path-connected total space and path-connected locally path-connected base. Put\n\n$$G=\\pi_1(B,b_0),\\qquad H=p_*\\pi_1(E,e_0).$$\n\nThe following are equivalent:\n\n1. $p$ is regular ([[def-regular-covering]]);\n2. $H\\trianglelefteq G$;\n3. $\\operatorname{Deck}(E/B)$ acts transitively on the fibre $p^{-1}(b_0)$.\n\nNo finiteness hypothesis is imposed on the fibre or on the index of $H$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-retraction-and-deformation-retract",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $A\\subseteq X$ carry the subspace topology, and let $i:A\\hookrightarrow X$ be the inclusion ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).\n\nA continuous map $r:X\\to A$ is a **retraction of $X$ onto $A$** if\n\n$$r\\circ i=\\operatorname{id}_A,$$\n\nequivalently, if $r(a)=a$ for every $a\\in A$. When such an $r$ exists, $A$ is a **retract** of $X$.\n\nThe subspace $A$ is a **deformation retract** of $X$ if there are a retraction $r:X\\to A$ and a homotopy\n\n$$H:\\operatorname{id}_X\\simeq_A i\\circ r.$$\n\nThus $H(x,0)=x$, $H(x,1)=i(r(x))$, and $H(a,t)=a$ for all $a\\in A$ and $t\\in I$ ([[def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy]]). The pair $(r,H)$ is a **deformation retraction**.\n\nSome sources call the pointwise-fixed condition a *strong deformation retract*. In this library the word *deformation retract* always includes it.",
