    "lem-maximal-abelian-cover-cellular-chain-model": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-maximal-abelian-cover-alexander-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be a nonempty oriented smooth link with $r\\ge1$ components, and let $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$ be its open complement. For each component choose a smooth fibrewise norm on its normal plane bundle $\\nu(K_i)$ and a tubular map $\\Phi_i$ from an open neighborhood of the zero section, fixing the zero section, together with a radius $\\epsilon_i>0$ whose closed disk subbundle lies in that neighborhood. Require the resulting closed tubes $N(K_i)=\\Phi_i(D_{\\epsilon_i}\\nu(K_i))$ to be pairwise disjoint, and put $N(L)=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rN(K_i)$ and $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}_{S^3}N(L)$, the resulting compact smooth $3$-manifold with boundary. The tube choices are part of the input. Choose a basepoint $b\\in E_L\\subset X_L$. Orient each normal disk so that the positive tangent to $K_i$ followed by its positive normal basis agrees with the fixed orientation of $S^3$. A **positive meridian** $\\mu_i$ of $K_i$ is the oriented boundary of the radius-$\\epsilon_i$ normal disk in one fibre of $\\nu(K_i)$; the curve lies on $\\partial E_L$ and is disjoint from every component of $L$; its spanning normal disk meets $K_i$ transversely once with positive sign and misses every other component. Alexander duality identifies $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\mathbb Z^r$ and identifies the classes of $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r$ with its standard basis. Write $G=\\pi_1(X_L,b)$ and let $\\phi:G\\to H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ be the first Hurewicz homomorphism, equivalently abelianization. The subgroup $\\ker\\phi$ is normal. The connected covering $p:X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\phi$ is the **maximal abelian cover**; it is regular and its deck group is identified with $G/\\ker\\phi\\cong H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$. Put $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)]\\cong\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$, where $t_i$ is the deck transformation corresponding to $[\\mu_i]$. The isomorphism sends each variable to its group-ring basis element; monomials $t_1^{n_1}\\cdots t_r^{n_r}$ correspond bijectively to the basis elements indexed by $n_1[\\mu_1]+\\cdots+n_r[\\mu_r]$. Define the absolute multivariable Alexander module by\n\n$$A_L=H_1(X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}};\\mathbb Z),$$\n\nas a left $\\Lambda_r$-module under the deck action. \u201cAbsolute\u201d means this is ordinary homology of the covering space, not relative homology with respect to a fibre over the basepoint.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-maximal-abelian-cover-link-exterior-retraction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the data in the definition of the maximal-abelian-cover Alexander module for a nonempty oriented smooth link $L$. Let $i:E_L\\hookrightarrow X_L$ be the inclusion of the compact exterior into the open complement, and let $p:X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to X_L$ be the maximal abelian cover. Then $E_L$ is a strong deformation retract of $X_L$, the restricted covering $p^{-1}(E_L)\\to E_L$ is the connected maximal abelian cover of $E_L$, and the lifted deformation retract is equivariant under the deck group. The inclusion identifies $H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)$ with $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ and preserves the positive-meridian classes. In particular, inclusion induces a $\\Lambda_r$-module isomorphism\n\n$$\nH_1(p^{-1}(E_L);\\mathbb Z)\\cong A_L.\n$$",
          "uses": [
