    "lem-multivariable-alexander-order-and-specialization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-maximal-abelian-cover-alexander-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be a nonempty oriented smooth link with $r\\ge1$ components, and let $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$ be its open complement. For each component choose a smooth fibrewise norm on its normal plane bundle $\\nu(K_i)$ and a tubular map $\\Phi_i$ from an open neighborhood of the zero section, fixing the zero section, together with a radius $\\epsilon_i>0$ whose closed disk subbundle lies in that neighborhood. Require the resulting closed tubes $N(K_i)=\\Phi_i(D_{\\epsilon_i}\\nu(K_i))$ to be pairwise disjoint, and put $N(L)=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rN(K_i)$ and $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}_{S^3}N(L)$, the resulting compact smooth $3$-manifold with boundary. The tube choices are part of the input. Choose a basepoint $b\\in E_L\\subset X_L$. Orient each normal disk so that the positive tangent to $K_i$ followed by its positive normal basis agrees with the fixed orientation of $S^3$. A **positive meridian** $\\mu_i$ of $K_i$ is the oriented boundary of the radius-$\\epsilon_i$ normal disk in one fibre of $\\nu(K_i)$; the curve lies on $\\partial E_L$ and is disjoint from every component of $L$; its spanning normal disk meets $K_i$ transversely once with positive sign and misses every other component. Alexander duality identifies $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\mathbb Z^r$ and identifies the classes of $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r$ with its standard basis. Write $G=\\pi_1(X_L,b)$ and let $\\phi:G\\to H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ be the first Hurewicz homomorphism, equivalently abelianization. The subgroup $\\ker\\phi$ is normal. The connected covering $p:X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\phi$ is the **maximal abelian cover**; it is regular and its deck group is identified with $G/\\ker\\phi\\cong H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$. Put $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)]\\cong\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$, where $t_i$ is the deck transformation corresponding to $[\\mu_i]$. The isomorphism sends each variable to its group-ring basis element; monomials $t_1^{n_1}\\cdots t_r^{n_r}$ correspond bijectively to the basis elements indexed by $n_1[\\mu_1]+\\cdots+n_r[\\mu_r]$. Define the absolute multivariable Alexander module by\n\n$$A_L=H_1(X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}};\\mathbb Z),$$\n\nas a left $\\Lambda_r$-module under the deck action. \u201cAbsolute\u201d means this is ordinary homology of the covering space, not relative homology with respect to a fibre over the basepoint.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-multivariable-laurent-ring-noetherian-ufd",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every integer $r\\ge1$, the commutative ring\n$$\n\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]\n$$\nis Noetherian and a unique factorisation domain. Its units are exactly the elements $\\pm t_1^{k_1}\\cdots t_r^{k_r}$ with $k_i\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-elementary-ideals-of-a-finitely-presented-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring ([[def-commutative-ring]]) and let $M$ be a\nfinitely presented $R$-module ([[def-finitely-presented-module-and-algebra]]),\nso that there are $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$ and a presentation\n\n$$R^m\\xrightarrow{\\ A\\ }R^n\\xrightarrow{\\ \\beta\\ }M\\longrightarrow0 .$$\n\nFix the standard bases $e_1,\\dots,e_m$ of $R^m$ and $f_1,\\dots,f_n$ of $R^n$\n([[def-finitely-presented-module-and-algebra]]) and let $A=(a_{ij})$ be the\n$n\\times m$ matrix of the $R$-linear map $\\alpha$ with respect to them: the\n$j$-th column of $A$ is the coordinate vector of $\\alpha(e_j)$, so\n$\\alpha(e_j)=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_{ij}f_i$. Such a matrix is called a **presentation\nmatrix** of $M$ (for the chosen presentation). A **minor** of $A$ of size\n$r\\times r$ is the determinant $\\det(a_{i_sj_t})_{s,t=1}^r$\n([[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]]) of the square submatrix obtained by\nchoosing rows $i_1<\\dots<i_r$ and columns $j_1<\\dots<j_r$; determinants of\n$1\\times1$ and $0\\times0$ submatrices are allowed, with the empty determinant\nequal to $1$.\n\nFor $k\\ge0$ the **$k$-th elementary ideal** $E_k(M)$ of the presented module $M$\nis the ideal of $R$ generated by the determinants of **all**\n$(n-k)\\times(n-k)$ minors of $A$ ([[def-generated-and-principal-ideals]],\n[[def-left-right-and-two-sided-ideal]]): if $n-k>n$ or $n-k>m$ there is no such\nminor, and the ideal generated by the empty set is $0$. In particular no minor\nis nonzero-by-convention: the conventions are\n$$E_k(M)=R\\quad\\text{for }k\\ge n,\\qquad E_k(M)=0\\quad\\text{for }k<n-m .$$\nFor $0\\le k\\le\\min(n,m)$ the ideal $E_k(M)$ is the ideal generated by the\n$(n-k)\\times(n-k)$ minors; e.g. $E_1(M)$ is generated by the\n$(n-1)\\times(n-1)$ minors of $A$, and $E_n(M)=R$ because the $0\\times0$ minor\nis $1$. This is the $k$-th **Fitting ideal** $\\operatorname{Fit}_k(M)$ of the\nStacks Project (Tag 07Z6, Lemma 15.8.2 and Definition 15.8.3), which is\nindexed by the size $n-k$ of the complementary minors; the two conventions\n$0=E_{-1}\\subset E_0\\subset\\cdots$ agree on the range $0\\le k\\le\\min(n,m)$.\n\n**Caveats.** (i) The notation $E_k(M)$ suppresses the chosen presentation; the\nideal defined here is a priori attached to the presentation and it is the\ncontent of [[lem-elementary-ideals-are-independent-of-the-presentation]] that\nit is in fact an invariant of $M$. (ii) The conventions in the degenerate\nranges are conventions, not theorems; they are the ones compatible with the\nStacks numbering and with independence of the presentation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-elementary-ideals-are-independent-of-the-presentation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $M$ be a finitely presented $R$-module.\nThen for every $k\\ge0$ the elementary ideal $E_k(M)$ of\n[[def-elementary-ideals-of-a-finitely-presented-module]] is independent of the\nchosen finite presentation of $M$; in particular it is an invariant of the\nisomorphism class of $M$, and isomorphic modules have the same elementary\nideals.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
