          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and let $L$ be a nonempty oriented $r$-component link with $r\\ge1$ and maximal-abelian-cover module $A_L$ over $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$. Then $A_L$ is finitely presented, every elementary/Fitting ideal $E_j(A_L)$ is independent of the chosen finite triangulation and of the chosen lifts of its cells, and the order $\\operatorname{ord}(A_L)$\u2014the greatest common divisor of the generators of $E_q(A_L)$, where $q=\\dim_{\\operatorname{Frac}(\\Lambda_r)}(A_L\\otimes_{\\Lambda_r}\\operatorname{Frac}(\\Lambda_r))$\u2014is defined up to a unit $\\pm t_1^{k_1}\\cdots t_r^{k_r}$. The diagonal ring map $\\eta:\\Lambda_r\\to\\Lambda_1=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$, $t_i\\mapsto t$, specializes the lifted chain complex by coefficient extension to the total-linking cyclic-cover chain complex. This statement concerns the chain complex only; it does not identify $A_L\\otimes_{\\Lambda_r,\\eta}\\Lambda_1$ with the first homology of the specialized complex. For $r\\ge2$, the diagonal map is nonflat and such an identification need not hold because homology need not commute with nonflat base change.",
          "uses": [
