          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "alg-abelian-cover-chain-model-from-triangulated-exterior",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Inputs.** A nonempty oriented $r$-component link $L$; its compact exterior $E_L$; a finite oriented simplicial triangulation $K$ of $E_L$ with connected 1-skeleton, an ordering of its vertices and simplices, and least vertex $v_0$ as the based point used to classify the cover; one certified positive-meridian edge loop in the boundary triangulation for each component, each loop oriented as the boundary of a normal disk whose positive tangent followed by its positive normal basis agrees with the orientation of $S^3$; and a label $\\ell(e)\\in\\mathbb Z^r$ for every oriented edge $e$ of $K$.\n\n**Admissible input conditions.** The input is admissible when edge reversal gives $\\ell(\\bar e)=-\\ell(e)$, the sum of the labels around every oriented 2-simplex is zero, and the sum around the $i$th certified positive meridian loop is the standard basis vector $e_i\\in\\mathbb Z^r$. The last check verifies the marking: by the Alexander-dual meridian basis and the compact-exterior retraction, these loops give the standard basis of $H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)$, so their evaluations determine the full abelianization. The edge labels define a covering model by assigning to each oriented edge $e:v\\to w$ the transition $(v,g)\\mapsto(w,g+\\ell(e))$ on vertex exponents $g\\in\\mathbb Z^r$; the face check makes all simplex lifts compatible. The procedure takes the geometric status of the supplied triangulation and peripheral curves as input data; it does not recognize a 3-manifold or discover meridians.\n\n**Output.** Either `invalid-labeling`, or the finite free graded $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$ modules with one basis element per simplex orbit and the prescribed Laurent-polynomial boundary matrices. The main theorem on this page proves that these matrices make the output a chain complex and that its first homology is $A_L$.\n\n**Instructions.**\n\n1. Check the edge-reversal rule, each oriented 2-simplex sum, and each meridian-loop evaluation. Return `invalid-labeling` if any check fails.\n2. Order the 1-simplices by their supplied simplex order. Start with no tree edges and with each vertex in its own component. Scan the edges in that order, adding an edge exactly when its endpoints lie in different current components and then merging those components. Because the 1-skeleton is connected, this finite scan produces the lexicographically first spanning tree. Use the least-ordered based vertex $v_0$ as root, assign its lift to the identity deck element, and assign every other vertex lift by summing edge labels along its unique tree path from the root.\n3. For each oriented simplex $\\sigma$, choose its least-ordered vertex as anchor and use the label sums along edges of $\\sigma$ to determine the lift incident to the already chosen anchor lift. For each oriented face $\\tau$ of $\\sigma$, compute the unique deck exponent $g(\\sigma,\\tau)\\in\\mathbb Z^r$ taking the chosen lift of $\\tau$ to that face of the chosen lift of $\\sigma$.\n4. If $\\tau$ occurs with incidence sign $\\epsilon(\\sigma,\\tau)\\in\\{-1,1\\}$ in $\\partial\\sigma$, place the coefficient $\\epsilon(\\sigma,\\tau)t_1^{g_1(\\sigma,\\tau)}\\cdots t_r^{g_r(\\sigma,\\tau)}$ in the corresponding matrix entry. Repeat for every simplex and dimension.\n5. Return the chain groups and boundary matrices in the input simplex order. Coning a closed edge path in a simplex to its anchor expresses it as a sum of triangular face boundaries, so the face-sum check makes its edge-path sum independent of path; the meridian checks ensure the deck group is the prescribed full abelianization.\n\nThe following triangle records the local consistency check. Each arrow is oriented as drawn and its label lies in $\\mathbb Z^r$. The face check in instruction 1 is exactly the equation $g_{01}+g_{12}+g_{20}=0$.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]\n  \\coordinate (v0) at (0,0);\n  \\coordinate (v1) at (3,0);\n  \\coordinate (v2) at (1.5,2);\n  \\draw[->] (v0) -- (v1) node[midway,below] {$g_{01}$};\n  \\draw[->] (v1) -- (v2) node[midway,right] {$g_{12}$};\n  \\draw[->] (v2) -- (v0) node[midway,left] {$g_{20}$};\n  \\fill (v0) circle (1.5pt) node[below left] {$v_0$};\n  \\fill (v1) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$v_1$};\n  \\fill (v2) circle (1.5pt) node[above] {$v_2$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-maximal-abelian-cover-alexander-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be a nonempty oriented smooth link with $r\\ge1$ components, and let $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$ be its open complement. For each component choose a smooth fibrewise norm on its normal plane bundle $\\nu(K_i)$ and a tubular map $\\Phi_i$ from an open neighborhood of the zero section, fixing the zero section, together with a radius $\\epsilon_i>0$ whose closed disk subbundle lies in that neighborhood. Require the resulting closed tubes $N(K_i)=\\Phi_i(D_{\\epsilon_i}\\nu(K_i))$ to be pairwise disjoint, and put $N(L)=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rN(K_i)$ and $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}_{S^3}N(L)$, the resulting compact smooth $3$-manifold with boundary. The tube choices are part of the input. Choose a basepoint $b\\in E_L\\subset X_L$. Orient each normal disk so that the positive tangent to $K_i$ followed by its positive normal basis agrees with the fixed orientation of $S^3$. A **positive meridian** $\\mu_i$ of $K_i$ is the oriented boundary of the radius-$\\epsilon_i$ normal disk in one fibre of $\\nu(K_i)$; the curve lies on $\\partial E_L$ and is disjoint from every component of $L$; its spanning normal disk meets $K_i$ transversely once with positive sign and misses every other component. Alexander duality identifies $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\mathbb Z^r$ and identifies the classes of $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r$ with its standard basis. Write $G=\\pi_1(X_L,b)$ and let $\\phi:G\\to H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ be the first Hurewicz homomorphism, equivalently abelianization. The subgroup $\\ker\\phi$ is normal. The connected covering $p:X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\phi$ is the **maximal abelian cover**; it is regular and its deck group is identified with $G/\\ker\\phi\\cong H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$. Put $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)]\\cong\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$, where $t_i$ is the deck transformation corresponding to $[\\mu_i]$. The isomorphism sends each variable to its group-ring basis element; monomials $t_1^{n_1}\\cdots t_r^{n_r}$ correspond bijectively to the basis elements indexed by $n_1[\\mu_1]+\\cdots+n_r[\\mu_r]$. Define the absolute multivariable Alexander module by\n\n$$A_L=H_1(X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}};\\mathbb Z),$$\n\nas a left $\\Lambda_r$-module under the deck action. \u201cAbsolute\u201d means this is ordinary homology of the covering space, not relative homology with respect to a fibre over the basepoint.",
