          "source": "thm-fundamental-group-of-finite-wedge-of-circles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be pointed at $[0]$, and for $r\\in\\mathbb N$ put\n\n$$W_r:=\\bigvee_{j<r}(Q,[0]).$$\n\nThen $\\pi_1(W_r,w)$ is the free group on the $r$ standard loops, one traversing each circle summand once. In particular it has rank $r$ ([[def-rank-of-a-finite-rank-free-group]]). For $r=0$, $W_0$ is a point and the basis is empty.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-homotopic-maps-induce-the-same-map-on-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g:X\\to Y$ are homotopic continuous maps, then for every $n\\geq 0$ and\nevery abelian group $G$ the induced homomorphisms on singular homology agree:\n$$H_n(f_\\#)=H_n(g_\\#):H_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G)\\to H_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(Y;G).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a topological space $X$ and an abelian group $G$, the singular chain groups\nand boundary maps of [[def-singular-boundary-operator]] form the\n**singular chain complex**\n$$C_\\bullet(X;G):=\\bigl(\\cdots\\to C_n(X;G)\\xrightarrow{\\partial_n}C_{n-1}(X;G)\\to\\cdots\\bigr),$$\nbecause [[thm-the-singular-boundary-squares-to-zero]] gives\n$\\partial_{n-1}\\partial_n=0$.\n\nIts degree-$n$ cycles and boundaries are\n$$Z_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G):=\\ker\\partial_n,\\qquad B_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G):=\\operatorname{im}\\partial_{n+1},$$\nin the sense of [[def-cycle-and-boundary-subobjects-of-a-complex]].\n\nThe **$n$th singular homology group** is the homology object of this chain\ncomplex:\n$$H_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G):=Z_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G)\\big/B_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G),$$\nequivalently $H_n(C_\\bullet(X;G))$ in the notation of\n[[def-homology-object-of-a-chain-complex]]. When the coefficient group is\n$\\mathbb Z$, write simply $C_n(X)$ and $H_n(X)$ when no confusion can arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
