        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-a-finitely-punctured-disk-retracts-to-a-wedge-of-circles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $k\\in\\mathbb N$, let $\\operatorname{int}D^2=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|<1\\}$ be\nthe open unit disc and let $Q\\subseteq\\operatorname{int}D^2$ be a set of\n$k$ distinct points. Then $\\operatorname{int}D^2\\setminus Q$ is homotopy\nequivalent to a wedge of $k$ circles, and for each puncture there is a\npositively oriented meridian loop such that these $k$ meridian classes form a\nfree basis of the fundamental group. Moreover $\\pi_j(\\operatorname{int}D^2\n\\setminus Q)=0$ for every $j\\ge2$. For $k=0$ the wedge is a point and\n$\\operatorname{int}D^2$ is contractible. The same conclusions hold for\n$\\mathbb C$ minus $k$ points under the explicit radial homeomorphism\n$h:\\mathbb C\\to\\operatorname{int}D^2$, $h(w)=w/(1+|w|)$. No choice axiom is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "prop-the-first-hurewicz-map-in-degree-one-is-abelianization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every path-connected topological space $X$ and $x_0\\in X$, the Hurewicz map\n$$h:\\pi_1(X,x_0)\\longrightarrow H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$$\nis surjective and has kernel $[\\pi_1(X,x_0),\\pi_1(X,x_0)]$. Hence it induces a natural isomorphism\n$$\\pi_1(X,x_0)^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\cong H_1(X;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe proof is choice-free and requires no CW, separation, local path-connectivity or local simple-connectivity assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
