          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $P=D^2\\setminus\\bigcup_i\\operatorname{int}D_i$ be either endpoint surface. The supplied product tubes parametrize the pairwise disjoint endpoint disks by $\\phi_i:D^2\\to D_i$, with $\\phi_i(0)=p_i$. Put $Q=D^2\\setminus\\{p_1,\\ldots,p_r\\}$. On $D_i\\setminus\\{p_i\\}$ write a point as $\\phi_i(ru)$ with $0<r\\le1$, $|u|=1$, and set $$R_t(\\phi_i(ru))=\\phi_i(((1-t)r+t)u).$$ Put $R_t=\\mathrm{id}$ outside the interiors of these disks. At $r=1$ the formulas agree, so finite pasting gives a deformation retraction $Q\\to P$. This uses the supplied disk coordinates, not a triangulation of an arbitrary smooth planar region. A collar of the outer unit circle misses all $p_i$. In its radial coordinate a continuous inward push, equal to the identity off that collar, gives a homotopy inverse to $\\operatorname{int}D^2\\setminus\\{p_i\\}\\hookrightarrow Q$; throughout the homotopy no puncture is crossed. By [F8] the open punctured disk has a finite circle-wedge model and a positive meridian free basis. Transfer it across these displayed equivalences, changing basepoint along the recorded collar path when needed. Small loops about $p_i$ become the positive endpoint meridians: the annulus $\\phi_i(ru)$ carries their orientation to the tube boundary. The accesses are the jointly transported stem paths of that finite spine; their images need not be arbitrary preselected accesses. If $a_i$ and $a_i'$ are compatible and arbitrary accesses to the same meridian circle, then $c_i=a_i'a_i^{-1}$ is a based loop and $a_i'\\mu_i(a_i')^{-1}=c_i(a_i\\mu_i a_i^{-1})c_i^{-1}$. Thus independent basings conjugate generators, without asserting they remain a free basis. Conjugation leaves their homology classes unchanged by [F9]. The homotopy type and abstract free rank are unchanged.",
