    "lem-bridge-sphere-normalization": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "incomplete-or-escalated",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-knot-exterior-is-irreducible",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth knot with a chosen closed tubular neighborhood $N(K)$, and let $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$. Every smoothly embedded two-sphere in the interior of $E_K$ bounds a smoothly embedded closed three-ball contained in $E_K$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-smooth-schoenflies-for-embedded-two-sphere-in-s-three",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $S\\subset S^3$ be the image of a smooth embedding of the standard $S^2$. There is a smooth ambient isotopy $H_t:S^3\\to S^3$, with $H_0=\\operatorname{id}$, such that $H_1(S)$ is the standard equatorial sphere $E$. In particular $S^3\\setminus S$ has exactly two components. Their closures $B_+$ and $B_-$ are smoothly embedded standard closed $3$-balls with common boundary $S$. If $D_+$ and $D_-$ denote the standard closed hemispheres bounded by $E$, the labels can be chosen so that the restrictions $H_1:B_\\pm\\to D_\\pm$ are diffeomorphisms with the same boundary restriction $H_1|_S:S\\to E$. Thus the ball identifications are compatible on their boundary and on a common two-sided collar of that boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-choice-free-smooth-parametrized-ball-windows-and-disk-absorption",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n=2,3$, a given smooth ball embedding $e:D^n\\to N$ into a connected boundaryless smooth $n$-manifold is isotopic through embeddings to a small coordinate round ball, and that embedding isotopy extends ambiently with compact support. For a parametrized smooth $n$-ball $B$ and a parametrized smooth boundary $(n-1)$-disk $W$, the pair $(B,W)$ is diffeomorphic to the standard ball with a hemispherical window. The diffeomorphism can retain the specified window parameter, using stereographic parameters on the standard hemisphere and allowing the necessary orientation of that parameter. No choice axiom is assumed.\n\nConsequently, attaching a parametrized smooth $n$-ball to a smooth $n$-manifold along such a window does not change its diffeomorphism type, provided an attaching collar and corner rounding are supplied. The resulting diffeomorphism is supported in the added ball and an arbitrarily small receiving collar. The inverse assertion holds for removing a ball that meets the boundary in exactly one such window with the supplied collar charts. In dimension two the windows are intervals.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-isotopy-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$.\n\n1. **Compact main case.** Let $M$ be a compact smooth manifold, possibly with boundary, let $N$ be a smooth manifold without boundary, let $F:M\\times I\\to N$ be a smooth isotopy of embeddings that is constant near the ends of $I$, and let $W$ be an open neighbourhood of $F(M\\times I)$ in $N$. Then there is an ambient isotopy $H:N\\times I\\to N$ with $H_0=\\mathrm{id}_N$, every $H_t$ a diffeomorphism, $H_t\\circ F_0=F_t$ for all $t\\in I$, $H_t=\\mathrm{id}_N$ outside $W$ for every $t$, and $H_t$ stationary near the ends; if $F$ is constant near the ends with parameter $\\varepsilon$, then $H_t=\\mathrm{id}_N$ for $t\\le\\varepsilon/2$ and $H_t=H_1$ for $t\\ge1-\\varepsilon/2$.\n2. **Relative form.** Let $N$ be boundaryless, $U\\subseteq N$ open, $A\\subseteq U$ compact, and let $F:U\\times I\\to N$ be a smooth isotopy of embeddings whose track image is open in $N\\times I$. Then there is a compactly supported ambient isotopy $H$ of $N$ with $H_t\\circ F_0=F_t$ on a neighbourhood of $A$ for every $t$.\n3. **Boundary stratum.** If $N$ has boundary and $F(M\\times I)\\subseteq\\partial N$, then the ambient isotopy of clause 1 can be chosen with every $H_t$ carrying $\\partial N$ onto itself; if $F(M\\times I)\\subseteq N\\setminus\\partial N$, it can be chosen compactly supported in $N\\setminus\\partial N$.\n4. **General isotopies.** For the same compact source (possibly with boundary) and boundaryless target as in clause 1, every smooth isotopy extends with support in a compact subset of any prescribed neighbourhood $W$ of $F(M\\times I)$. The endpoint-constancy hypothesis and the stationary-end conclusion are both omitted; all other conclusions of clause 1 hold.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-knot-decomposing-sphere-realizes-connected-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in $S^3$, and let $S\\subset S^3$ be a smoothly embedded sphere transverse to $K$ in exactly two points. Cutting along $S$ gives two properly embedded arcs; cap each arc by an orientation-compatible simple arc on $S$, pushed slightly into the corresponding boundary collar to obtain oriented knots $K_1$ and $K_2$. Then the oriented isotopy class of $K$ is the connected sum $K_1\\#K_2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-knot-connected-sum-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For two smooth oriented knots, changing either representative by oriented ambient isotopy, changing the sufficiently small coordinate ball and trivial subarc used for either knot, or changing the positive local frame used to identify that ball with the standard oriented ball does not change the oriented ambient-isotopy class of the connected-sum knot, when the boundary gluing map is the fixed standard orientation-reversing map from the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $B_+,B_-$ for the fixed bridge balls, $D_i^\\pm$ for their spanning disks, and $T_\\pm=P\\cap B_\\pm$. Arrange finite transverse intersections, with $P\\cap S\\cap K=\\varnothing$, and take the knot tube sufficiently small and adapted to these intersections. A product neighborhood of each $D_i^\\pm$ identifies a neighborhood of its bridge with the usual boundary-parallel ball-arc. Cutting $B_\\pm\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ along the disks $D_i^\\pm\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ gives a ball: successively slide each bridge along its displayed disk into a boundary collar, and remove its displayed half-tube; each cut opens that collar tunnel. These moves use disjoint same-side disk neighborhoods. In particular this is a *complete* meridian-disk system, not merely a collection of compressing disks. Throughout the proof the knot and $S$ themselves stay fixed; a puncture of $P$ is allowed to travel along the fixed knot.",
