        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-bridge-sphere-normalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $K=K_1\\#K_2$ be a smooth oriented connected sum with both factors nontrivial. Let $S$ be a regular level sphere giving a bridge position: $S$ divides $S^3$ into two balls, and in each ball the knot is a finite collection of properly embedded arcs, each of which cobounds an embedded disk with an arc on $S$; the disks in each ball are pairwise disjoint. Let $P$ be a smooth decomposing sphere meeting $K$ transversely in two points and cutting it into the given two oriented factors. Put $E=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ and let $A=P\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ be the resulting annulus, where $N(K)$ is a small closed tubular neighborhood. Assume the inclusion-induced map $\\pi_1(A)\\to\\pi_1(E)$ is injective and that there is no embedded disk in $E$, with interior disjoint from $A\\cup\\partial E$, whose boundary is the union of a properly embedded arc in $A$ that does not cobound a disk in $A$ with a subarc of $\\partial A$ and an arc in $\\partial E$. Then, after isotoping $P$ through spheres meeting $K$ twice and changing only the bridge-spanning disk systems for the fixed pair $(K,S)$, one can arrange that $P\\cap S$ is exactly one simple closed curve and that the union of the upper and lower bridge-spanning disks meets $P$ in exactly two arcs, each joining one point of $P\\cap K$ to a point of $P\\cap S$.",
