          "claim": "There is no boundary compression of $A$. If there were, [F5] would give a parallelism to one of the two annuli $A_0$ on the boundary torus between its meridional boundaries. This implies that one factor is the unknot, as can be checked with the markings: write the tube over the corresponding subinterval of the core knot as $D^2\\times I$, with side $A_0=\\partial D^2\\times I$ and end disks the two disks $P\\cap N(L)$. Attach the annulus parallelism to this side, retaining its rim collars. It merely enlarges the radial disk coordinate in each $I$-fiber, so the result is a ball with boundary the original $P$ after rounding. Its knot arc is the core $\\{0\\}\\times I$, and it cobounds with an arc on that ball's boundary a disk given by a fixed radial interval times $I$, rounded at its ends. Capping this arc on $P$ produces an unknot. The same construction works for either complementary annulus of the torus; it uses its actual product parameter, not just a homological slope. By [F8] the resulting unknot is one of the given factors, contradicting nontriviality. Thus all hypotheses of [F3] hold for this particular $P$, and we may apply its isotopy and disk changes without changing $(L,S)$ or the specified factors.",
