    "thm-unknotting-number-at-least-half-levine-tristram-signature": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "incomplete-or-escalated",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF,\n\n$$\\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{DC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,\\mathrm{fin}}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_2.$$\n\nDC here includes a prescribed initial point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-knot-bridge-position-and-unknotting-number",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Identify the oriented $S^3$ with the unit sphere in $\\mathbb R^4$ by an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism and fix the standard height $h(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)=x_4$. Its only critical points are the two poles, and each regular level is a 2-sphere. A **Morse representative** of a smooth oriented knot $K$ is a knot ambient isotopic to $K$ on which $h$ has only nondegenerate critical points. Define the **bridge number** $b(K)$ as the minimum number of local maxima of $h$ on a Morse representative of $K$. The standard unknot is the oriented unit circle in a fixed coordinate 2-plane of $S^3$.\n\nA **regular oriented diagram** is a projection of a smooth oriented knot with finitely many transverse double points, no other multiple points, and an over/under choice at each double point. A **crossing change** switches which branch is over at one chosen double point and leaves the projection and strand orientations unchanged. Define the **unknotting number** $u(K)$ as the minimum number of crossing changes needed to turn some regular diagram of a representative of $K$ into a diagram of the standard unknot. Both minima are taken in subsets of $\\mathbb N$. The Morse-representative set is nonempty by the first well-definedness result below; under countable choice the regular-diagram existence result and the descending-diagram theorem below make the unknotting set nonempty. The bridge-number definition and the least-element argument in $\\mathbb N$ require no choice; countable choice is used only to invoke the stated regular-projection supplier for $u(K)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-seifert-matrices-differ-rank-one-under-crossing-change",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $D_+$ and $D_-$ be finite regular oriented diagrams that agree outside one crossing neighborhood and differ inside it by switching which branch is over. Let $V_+$ and $V_-$ be the Seifert matrices obtained from their oriented disk-and-band surfaces. There are corresponding integral bases in which either $V_+=V_-$ or $V_+-V_-=\\pm vv^T$ for some integral column vector $v$. In particular, $\\operatorname{rank}(V_+-V_-)\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-rank-one-hermitian-signature-perturbation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A$ and $B$ be Hermitian forms on the same finite-dimensional complex vector space, and suppose that the linear map representing $A-B$ has rank at most one. Then $|\\operatorname{sign}(A)-\\operatorname{sign}(B)|\\le 2$, where the signature is the number of positive eigenvalues minus the number of negative eigenvalues, with multiplicity and with zero eigenvalues omitted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-levine-tristram-hermitian-form-and-signature",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in the oriented standard $3$-sphere, let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented surface with oriented boundary $K$, let $V$ be the matrix of the Seifert pairing $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$ in an ordered integral basis, and let $\\omega\\in S^1\\setminus\\{1\\}$. Define the **Levine–Tristram Hermitian matrix** by\n$$H_\\omega(V)=(1-\\omega)V+(1-\\bar\\omega)V^T.$$\nIt is Hermitian because $V$ is real and $\\bar\\omega=\\omega^{-1}$. Its signature is the number of positive eigenvalues minus the number of negative eigenvalues, both counted with multiplicity and with zero eigenvalues omitted. When $\\Delta_K(\\omega)\\ne0$, define $\\sigma_\\omega(K)$ to be this signature. At a root $\\omega_0\\ne1$ of $\\Delta_K$, define $\\sigma_{\\omega_0}(K)$ to be the arithmetic mean of the two one-sided limits of $\\sigma_{e^{i\\theta}}(K)$ as $\\theta$ approaches an argument of $\\omega_0$ from below and above. The invariance and one-sided-existence lemmas justify treating these values as independent of the chosen surface and basis.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-seifert-stabilization-preserves-levine-tristram-signature",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. For every $\\omega\\in S^1\\setminus\\{1\\}$, changing an ordered basis by an integral unimodular congruence or changing a knot Seifert matrix by either elementary enlargement or reduction preserves the signature of $H_\\omega$. Consequently, the nonroot Levine–Tristram signature and its one-sided-average value at every Alexander root are independent of the spanning surface and basis.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-levine-tristram-hermitian-form-singular-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot, let $F$ be any connected compact smooth oriented spanning surface of genus $g$, let $V$ be its positive-push Seifert matrix, and let $\\omega\\in S^1\\setminus\\{1\\}$. The Hermitian matrix\n$$H_\\omega=(1-\\omega)V+(1-\\bar\\omega)V^T$$\nis singular if and only if the normalized knot Alexander polynomial $\\Delta_K$ vanishes at $\\omega$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-seifert-matrix-skew-part-is-surface-intersection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented embedded circle in the oriented standard three-sphere, let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, and let $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ be any ordered integral basis of $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$ with Seifert matrix $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. Let $J_F$ be the matrix of the algebraic intersection form on $F$, with a transverse intersection of oriented curves $u,v$ counted positively exactly when their ordered tangent vectors form a positive basis of $TF$. Then $V-V^T=J_F$. The matrix $J_F$ is skew-symmetric and unimodular; when $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ is a standard handle basis, it is a direct sum of blocks $\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "At $\\omega=-1$ the form is nonsingular for every knot. Indeed $V+V^T\\equiv V-V^T\\pmod2$, so its determinant is odd by [F5] and in particular is not zero. Thus $H_{-1}=2(V+V^T)$ is nonsingular. By [F4], $-1$ is not an Alexander root and the actual matrix signature is precisely $\\sigma_{-1}(K)$; its surface and basis independence also follows from [F4]. This removes any need to use a root-average convention at $-1$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
