          "claim": "The standard plumbing has the three crossing bands of the positive two-strand three-crossing closure: its two annular strips use successive pairs of the three bands, and their common disk is the plumbing square with its four alternating attaching edges. In oriented braid coordinates $(x,y,t)$ use $(X,Y,Z)=(x,-t,y)$, of determinant $+1$, to draw the closure on the $(X,Y)$ page with viewer $+Z$. The overpass runs down-left and the underpass down-right at each of the three crossings, while the two outside closure arcs are crossing-free. This is precisely the right-hand trefoil diagram of Rolfsen printed146, identified as right-hand on printed201; the comparison fixes the handedness using actual crossing data. The two core classes have intersection number $a\\cdot_F b=1$ by [F2]. Since $F$ has genus one and one boundary component, its first homology has rank two; the intersection matrix in the core classes has determinant $1$. Therefore $a,b$ form an integral basis.",
