          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\nu,c,\\alpha_*,\\tau>0$, $0<\\theta<1$ and $0<q<1$. Suppose $H$ is the conjugate of a truncated logarithmic source index with $H(u)\\sim C(\\log u)^{-\\nu}$ as $u\\to\\infty$. Starting sufficiently far down the geometric sequence, relabel it as $\\alpha_n=\\alpha_*\\theta^{n+n_0}$, $n\\ge1$, and put $h_n=H(c/\\alpha_n)$ and $\\rho_n=\\alpha_nh_n$. Then one can choose $n_0$ and $C_H>1$, fixed independently of noise, such that\n$$h_n\\le C_Hh_{n+1},\\quad qC_H<1,\\quad \\frac{\\rho_n}{\\rho_{n+1}}\\le M:=\\theta^{-1}C_H\\qquad(n\\ge1).$$\nThe positive sequence $\\rho_n$ decreases strictly to zero. For any positive target $w\\downarrow0$, let $N(w)$ be the last index with $\\rho_{N(w)}\\ge\\tau w$. For sufficiently small $w$ this index exists and is at least two, and\n$$\\tau w\\le\\rho_{N(w)}<M\\tau w,\\qquad h_{N(w)}\\asymp[\\log(1/w)]^{-\\nu},\\qquad \\alpha_{N(w)}\\asymp w[\\log(1/w)]^\\nu.$$\nIn particular $w=\\delta^2$ gives $h_N\\asymp[\\log(1/\\delta)]^{-\\nu}$ and $\\alpha_N\\asymp\\delta^2[\\log(1/\\delta)]^\\nu$. The nominal endpoint target $w=\\delta^2[\\log(1/\\delta)]^{-\\nu}$ gives the same $h_N$ and $\\alpha_N\\asymp\\delta^2$. In that endpoint case, an actual noise bound $\\varepsilon_\\delta^2=O(w)$ and tolerances $\\eta_n\\le C_\\eta\\rho_n$ give exactly the noise/tolerance scale required for applying a frozen recursion at this stopping index.",
