berwise update is defined by this formula when $Z_\\Phi(y)=0$ or $Z_\\Phi(y)=+\\infty$.\n\nFor a data-indexed claim, an observation model and its version convention must also be specified. In the dominated case, let $Q$ be a proper probability kernel from $(D,\\mathcal B(D))$ to $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$, let $\\nu$ be a sigma-finite measure on $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$, and suppose $Q(u,B)=\\int_B L_\\Phi(u;y)\\,\\nu(dy)$ for every $u\\in D$ and $B\\in\\mathcal Y$. Then $Z_\\Phi$ is the density of the prior-predictive data law with respect to $\\nu$; a data-indexed conditional-law version uses $\\Pi^y$ when $0<Z_\\Phi(y)<\\infty$ and the prior $\\mu_{0,D}$ when $Z_\\Phi(y)$ is zero or infinite. These filled fibers have zero prior-predictive mass, and their chosen values are not normalized fiberwise updates. More generally, for a proper Bayesian model with standard-Borel parameter space $(D,\\mathcal B(D))$ and data space $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ (each measurably isomorphic to the Borel space of a Polish space), the Axiom of Choice guarantees a data-indexed posterior kernel; its values are unique as probability measures outside one data-marginal-null set, and values on that null set remain version choices. The dominated construction is choice-free; the separate standard-Borel existence branch assumes the Axiom of Choice.",
