          "quote": "Let $n\\ge0$ and\n$$R=\\mathbb Z[q^{\\pm1},\\rho^{\\pm1},\\delta^{\\pm1}]/((q-q^{-1})(\\delta-1)-(\\rho-\\rho^{-1})),\\qquad z=q-q^{-1}.$$\nLet $\\Lambda_0=\\mathbb Z[\\lambda^{\\pm1},z_0,\\delta^{\\pm1}]/(\\lambda^{-1}-\\lambda-z_0(\\delta-1))$ and map it to $R$ by $\\lambda\\mapsto\\rho$, $z_0\\mapsto-z$, and $\\delta\\mapsto\\delta$. A framed $(n,n)$ tangle is an isotopy class, fixing the boundary, of pairwise disjoint embedded ribbons in a rectangle times an interval, with $n$ prescribed ribbon intervals at each end. An ordinary $(n,n)$ tangle diagram is a generic immersed collection of arcs and circles in the rectangle with those $2n$ fixed endpoints, with each transverse double point marked over/under. Regular isotopy means planar isotopy fixing the boundary together with Reidemeister moves II and III, but not move I; this is the framed-diagram convention of Goodman–Hauschild, §§2.1–2.2. Multiplication is vertical stacking, and the identity is the diagram of $n$ vertical strands. Define $KT_n(R)$ as the quotient of the free $R$-module on regular-isotopy classes by the local rules of Goodman–Hauschild, Definition 2.6, after the displayed parameter substitution: the crossing skein relation is $L_+-L_-=z(L_{\\mathrm{id}}-L_{\\mathrm{cup-cap}})$, the two opposite single-crossing curls evaluate to $\\rho^{-1}$ and $\\rho$ times the straight strand, and an isolated unknotted, untwisted circle is removed with coefficient $\\delta$. Here $L_+$ and $L_-$ differ by the crossing, while $L_{\\mathrm{id}}$ and $L_{\\mathrm{cup-cap}}$ are its identity and cup-cap smoothings; all four diagrams agree outside a small disk. Order the $2n$ boundary labels by $1<\\cdots<n<n'<\\cdots<1'$. The standard orientation of a diagram orients each nonclosed strand from its lower-ordered endpoint to its higher-ordered endpoint and traverses the nonclosed strands by increasing initial endpoint. Such an oriented diagram is totally descending if every crossing is first met 