          "claim": "Take ambient coordinates $(x,y,z)$ with overstrand tangent $+\\partial_x$, viewing normal $+\\partial_z$, and positive projection basis $(\\partial_x,\\partial_y)$. Source has $y<0$ and target $y>0$, with the understrand below the overstrand. Choose its oriented product tube $\\Phi(u,v)$, where $v\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is the normal-disk coordinate, and choose a small positive radius $r$ and a fixed angle $\\theta_0=0$, taking the first normal-frame vector toward the upper side of the understrand. The path used here is the push-off $\\beta(u)=\\Phi(u,r(\\cos\\theta_0,\\sin\\theta_0))$, from source to target; it never lies on the knot core. The two meridian loops are the boundaries $\\mu_u(\\tau)=\\Phi(u,r(\\cos(\\theta_0+2\\pi\\tau),\\sin(\\theta_0+2\\pi\\tau)))$, based at the endpoints of $\\beta$. For exterior paths take $r$ at least the removed tube radius; shrink that radius first if necessary. Choose the product tube disjoint from the overstrand and other arc pieces, and $r$ smaller than the crossing-chart separation bounds. Choose upper-collar paths $\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t$ to these actual boundary points. In the normal $(y,z)$ plane of the overstrand, their concatenation $\\alpha_s\\beta\\alpha_t^{-1}$ follows a small perturbation of the polygon $(0,H),(-d,-h),(d,-h),(0,H)$, which has positive signed area. The polygon and a sufficiently small surrounding strip avoid the origin; the chosen radius makes the perturbation lie in that strip, so its winding is still $+1$. For explicit complement control, straighten the local under-core to $x=0,z=-h$ in the product chart and take its upper push-off at $z=-h+r$. The access triangle is then entirely above $z=-h$, while the over-core projects to the origin. Radial interpolation of its positive polar-distance function to a sufficiently small circle about the origin stays inside that triangle minus the origin, hence above the under-core as well. Retain the positive-radius angular access in the under-tube until reaching these straightened paths. Thus the resulting homotopy is to one counterclockwise over-meridian and avoids both strands throughout. Thus all paths and homotopies lie in the complement, the loop represents $m_o$, and $\\alpha_t\\simeq m_o^{-1}\\alpha_s\\beta$ rel endpoints there.",
