    "lem-knot-group-infinity-collar": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-wirtinger-crossing-ball-cover",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with a regular diagram represented by a valid finite planar code, and let $c$ be its number of crossings. Choose a sufficiently small closed normal tube $N(K)$ and set $E_K=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$. If $c>0$, there is a subspace $Y\\subset E_K$, fixed pointwise by a strong deformation retraction of $E_K$, with a finite relative-open cover\n$$Y=U\\cup V_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup V_c.$$\nThe space $U$ deformation retracts to a wedge of $c$ circles. With a common basepoint in the collar above the diagram and access paths in that collar extended into the arc charts, the positive under-arc meridians are its free basis. Each $V_i$ is contractible, $U\\cap V_i$ is an open annulus around the $i$-th crossing attachment, and distinct $V_i$ are disjoint. Thus $V_i$ meets $U$ and any previously attached crossing pieces precisely in $U\\cap V_i$.\n\nFor $c=0$, the Euclidean complement has homotopy type $S^1\\vee S^2$, with the circle represented by the positive meridian. In particular its fundamental group is infinite cyclic; the extra sphere at infinity must not be discarded. In all cases the punctured normal tube retracts radially to its boundary, giving a strong deformation retraction $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K\\to E_K$. These constructions require only finitely many charts and no choice axiom.",
          "uses": []
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every natural number $n\\ge2$, the unit sphere $S^n\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ is simply connected ([[def-simply-connected]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-simply-connected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A topological space $X$ is **simply connected** when it is nonempty and\npath-connected ([[def-path-connected]]) and, for every $x_0\\in X$, the group\n$\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ has exactly one element.\n\nRequiring every basepoint avoids presuming a change-of-basepoint theorem. For a\npath-connected space that later theorem shows that checking one basepoint is\nequivalent, but no such result is needed for this definition. The empty space\nis path-connected under the published convention, but it is not simply\nconnected here because nonemptiness is explicit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=U\\cup V$, where $U$ and $V$ are open path-connected subsets of $X$, let $U\\cap V$ be path-connected, and fix $x_0\\in U\\cap V$. For the inclusion-induced maps in the diagram\n\n$$\\pi_1(U,x_0)\\longleftarrow\\pi_1(U\\cap V,x_0)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(V,x_0),$$\n\nthe group $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$, together with the two inclusion-induced homomorphisms, is a pushout ([[def-pushout-of-group-homomorphisms]]). Equivalently, the canonical homomorphism from any pushout $P$ of the displayed diagram to $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ is an isomorphism. No injectivity of either map from the overlap group is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-seifert-van-kampen-simply-connected-overlap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Under the hypotheses of [[thm-seifert-van-kampen]], if $U\\cap V$ is simply connected, then the inclusion-induced homomorphisms give an isomorphism\n\n$$\\pi_1(U,x_0)*\\pi_1(V,x_0)\\cong\\pi_1(X,x_0).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-induced-homomorphism-on-fundamental-groups",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to Y$ be continuous and let $x_0\\in X$. Composition sends a loop\n$\\alpha$ at $x_0$ to the loop $f\\circ\\alpha$ at $f(x_0)$. Using the loop\nclasses and fundamental group of [[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]],\nthe proposed\n**induced homomorphism** is\n\n$$f_*:\\pi_1(X,x_0)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(Y,f(x_0)),\\qquad f_*([\\alpha]):=[f\\circ\\alpha].$$\n\nThe next theorem proves that this value is independent of the representative,\nthat it is a group homomorphism in the sense of [[def-group-homomorphism]], and\nthat induced maps respect identities, composition and homotopies that fix the\nbasepoint.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-induced-fundamental-group-map-functoriality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:(X,x_0)\\to(Y,y_0)$ be a continuous map with $f(x_0)=y_0$. Then\n\n$$f_*([\\alpha])=[f\\circ\\alpha]$$\n\nis a well-defined group homomorphism. For pointed continuous maps,\n\n$$\\operatorname{id}_*=\\operatorname{id},\\qquad (g\\circ f)_*=g_*\\circ f_*.$$\n\nIf $f_0,f_1:(X,x_0)\\to(Y,y_0)$ are homotopic through a homotopy that keeps\n$x_0$ at $y_0$, then $(f_0)_*=(f_1)_*$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-fundamental-group-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every pointed topological space $(X,x_0)$, the product\n\n$$[\\alpha][\\beta]=[\\alpha*\\beta]$$\n\nis well defined and makes $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ a group. Its identity is the class of\nthe constant loop $c_{x_0}$, and $[\\alpha]^{-1}=[\\bar\\alpha]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
