          "quote": "Assume countable choice. On the flat complex torus $T=\\mathbb C/(\\mathbb Z+i\\mathbb Z)$ let $\\alpha$ be the image of the real line, and for $\\varepsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ let $\\beta_\\varepsilon$ be the image of the line of direction $1+3\\varepsilon i$. Then\n$$\\alpha\\cap\\beta_\\varepsilon=\\{(0,0),(1/3,0),(2/3,0)\\}\\pmod{\\mathbb Z^2}.$$\nFor any basepoint off the circles, every integral periodic domain with zero coefficient at that basepoint is zero; here a periodic domain is a region two-chain whose boundary is an integral sum of the two full circles. Thus this diagram satisfies even the global mixed-sign admissibility condition.\n\nBy a relative disk from $x$ to $y$ mean a continuous map from a closed disk with two marked boundary points to $T$, taking the two boundary arcs into $\\alpha,\\beta_\\varepsilon$ and their endpoints to $x,y$. If $x\\ne y$, no such disk exists. If $x=y$, every such disk is homotopic to the constant disk at $x$, through maps with the same boundary conditions and fixed endpoints.\n\nEvery actual smooth finite-energy strip for the flat equation $u_s+i u_t=0$, with its two boundary edges on these circles and uniform intersection-generator ends, is constant. The actual linearization at any constant strip is a bounded real Hilbert isomorphism $H^1_{\\alpha,\\beta_\\varepsilon}\\to L^2$, and therefore has real index zero. In particular every moduli space of index-one finite-energy strips is empty, for any basepoint away from the curves. The corresponding diagram-level mod-two count operator is zero on its three generators, so its chain homology has dimension three. This is an actual calculation for the specified diagram; it does not supply closed-manifold move invariance or identify that homology for every diagram.",
