      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-khovanov-frobenius-algebra-and-cube-of-resolutions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D$ be a nonempty finite regular oriented link diagram in an oriented $3$-sphere, with finite crossing set $C(D)$. Fix a unit viewing normal $n$ and orient the projection plane $P$ so $(u,v)$ is positive exactly when $(n,u,v)$ is positive in the ambient $3$-space. At a crossing $c$, define its sign as the sign of $\\det_P(v_{\\rm over},v_{\\rm under})$, where the two nonzero projected tangent vectors follow the oriented over- and under-strands. Let $n_+$ and $n_-$ be the numbers of positive and negative crossings.\n\nSet $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$, with $\\deg(1)=1$ and $\\deg(X)=-1$. Its unit, multiplication, counit, and comultiplication are\n$$\n\\eta(1)=1,\\qquad m(1\\otimes1)=1,\\quad m(1\\otimes X)=m(X\\otimes1)=X,\\quad m(X\\otimes X)=0,\n$$\n$$\n\\epsilon(1)=0,\\quad\\epsilon(X)=1,\\qquad \\Delta(1)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1,\\quad\\Delta(X)=X\\otimes X.\n$$\nThus $m$ and $\\Delta$ have quantum degree $-1$, while $\\eta$ and $\\epsilon$ have degree $+1$.\n\nChoose a total order $c_1<\\cdots<c_n$ on $C(D)$. A resolution state is a function $v:C(D)\\to\\{0,1\\}$; at $v(c)=0$ use the A-smoothing and at $v(c)=1$ use the B-smoothing from the Kauffman source chart. Write $|v|=\\sum_c v(c)$, let $D_v$ be the crossingless resolution, and let $k(v)$ be its number of circles. Assign the state module $\\mathcal A^{\\otimes k(v)}$. For an edge $v\\to v+e_j$ changing crossing $c_j$ from $0$ to $1$, use $m$ if the local smoothing merges two circles and $\\Delta$ if it splits one circle, tensored with the identity on all other factors, and multiply this map by $(-1)^{\\sum_{i<j}v(c_i)}$. The resulting direct sum has cohomological degree\n$$i(v)=|v|-n_-$$\nand the generator with circle labels $1$ or $X$ has quantum degree\n$$j(v)=|v|+n_+-2n_-+\\sum_{\\text{circle labels }a}\\deg(a).$$\nThis defines the normalized integral Khovanov cube and its candidate degree-zero cochain differential; the assertion that the differential squares to zero is a separate lemma.\n\nThe Frobenius identity that controls a merge-split face is represented by the two equal composites below. The diagram records the maps only; the proof verifies the equality on the basis $\\{1,X\\}^{\\otimes2}$ and does not rely on the drawing.\n\n```tikzcd\n\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal A \\arrow[r,\"m\"] \\arrow[d,\"\\Delta\\otimes\\mathrm{id}\"'] & \\mathcal A \\arrow[d,\"\\Delta\"] \\\\\n\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal A \\arrow[r,\"\\mathrm{id}\\otimes m\"'] & \\mathcal A\\otimes\\mathcal A\n```",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "alg-khovanov-chain-complex-from-diagram",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Input:** a nonempty finite regular oriented link diagram $D$ and a total order $c_1<\\cdots<c_n$ on its crossing set. A diagram object is supplied as mathematical input; this procedure does not parse a serialized string.\n\n**Output:** the normalized bigraded Khovanov cochain module $C^{i,j}(D)$ and its candidate differential $d:C^{i,j}(D)\\to C^{i+1,j}(D)$, constructed by the following finite instructions.\n\n1. Enumerate every state $v:C(D)\\to\\{0,1\\}$ once. Form the crossingless resolution by using the A-smoothing at $v(c)=0$ and the B-smoothing at $v(c)=1$.\n2. For each state, create one tensor factor of the integral Frobenius algebra for each resolution circle and place the resulting tensor product in cohomological degree $|v|-n_-$ and quantum degree $|v|+n_+-2n_-+\\sum\\deg(a)$ for its circle labels $a$.\n3. For each state and each crossing $c_j$ with $v(c_j)=0$, create the edge to $v+e_j$. Apply multiplication if that smoothing change merges two circles, comultiplication if it splits one circle, the identity on other tensor factors, and the sign $(-1)^{\\sum_{i<j}v(c_i)}$. Sum the edge maps out of every state to obtain $d$.\n4. Return the finite direct sum of the state modules with these degrees and maps. The square-zero property is the separate local lemma named by `justified_by`.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-khovanov-cube-differential-squares-to-zero",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D$ be a finite regular oriented link diagram, order its crossings as $c_1<\\cdots<c_n$, and form the direct sum of state modules $\\mathcal A^{\\otimes k(v)}$ over all functions $v:C(D)\\to\\{0,1\\}$. Here $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$, each crossing is changed from its A-smoothing at $0$ to its B-smoothing at $1$, and the edge map is the multiplication $m$ for a merge or comultiplication $\\Delta$ for a split, multiplied by $(-1)^{\\sum_{i<j}v(c_i)}$ on the edge at $c_j$. Then the sum $d$ of all edge maps satisfies $d^2=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-khovanov-chain-complex-from-diagram-procedure-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a finite regular oriented link diagram $D$ with $n$ ordered crossings, the procedure that enumerates all $2^n$ smoothing states, assigns one copy of $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$ per resolution circle, places a generator in degrees $i=|v|-n_-$ and $j=|v|+n_+-2n_-+\\sum\\deg(a)$, and sums the signed merge or split edge maps terminates after finitely many operations. Its output is a finite direct sum of finite-rank free abelian groups with a differential of bidegree $(1,0)$ satisfying $d^2=0$; hence it is a bounded bigraded cochain complex.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-reduced-khovanov-cohomology-of-a-based-link-over-f2",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(D,p)$ be a finite regular oriented link diagram with a marked point $p$ away from crossings, and let $C(D;\\mathbb F_2)$ be its normalized Khovanov cochain complex over $\\mathbb F_2$ obtained from the algebra $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2)$ with $\\deg_q(1)=1$ and $\\deg_q(X)=-1$. On each resolution state, let $X_p$ multiply by $X$ on the circle containing $p$ and act as the identity on the other circle factors. Define the reduced complex by $\\widetilde C(D,p)=C(D;\\mathbb F_2)/\\operatorname{im}X_p$, with the surviving based-circle label $1$ assigned reduced quantum degree $0$ by the shift $\\widetilde j=j-1$. Its cohomology is $\\widetilde{Kh}^{i,\\widetilde j}(D,p;\\mathbb F_2)$. The basepoint may lie on any component; for a link, independence of the chosen component is a separate theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
          "claim": "On $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb F_2\\{1,X\\}$ define $N(1)=0$ and $N(X)=1$, and on a state tensor define $N$ as the sum of this operation over its circle factors. For multiplication, direct evaluation on $(1,1),(1,X),(X,1),(X,X)$ gives $N m=m(N\\otimes\\mathrm{id}+\\mathrm{id}\\otimes N)$. For comultiplication, evaluation at $1$ gives $(N\\otimes\\mathrm{id}+\\mathrm{id}\\otimes N)\\Delta(1)=0=\\Delta N(1)$, and evaluation at $X$ gives $(N\\otimes\\mathrm{id}+\\mathrm{id}\\otimes N)\\Delta(X)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1=\\Delta N(X)$. Thus $N$ commutes with each saddle map and hence with $d$. Also $N^2=0$: applying $N$ twice to one circle gives zero, and every pair of distinct circles occurs twice, hence cancels over $\\mathbb F_2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
