        {
          "id": "step-2.1",
          "claim": "For a basepoint $p$, multiplication $X_p$ is a chain map and $X_p^2=0$. The local operators on the circle containing $p$ satisfy $NX_p+X_pN=\\mathrm{id}$ on that factor; terms of $N$ on all other circles commute with $X_p$ and occur twice in the sum, so the identity holds on every state tensor.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3.1",
          "claim": "Put $P=X_pN$ and $Q=NX_p$. The identity in step 2.1 and $X_p^2=N^2=0$ imply $P+Q=\\mathrm{id}$, $P^2=P$, $Q^2=Q$, and $PQ=QP=0$. Both maps commute with $d$ by step 1.1. Moreover $\\operatorname{im}P=\\operatorname{im}X_p$: one inclusion follows from $P=X_pN$, and $PX_p=X_pNX_p=X_p$ gives the reverse inclusion. Hence the chain module splits as $\\operatorname{im}X_p\\oplus\\operatorname{im}Q$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4.1",
          "claim": "The quotient map identifies $C/\\operatorname{im}X_p$ with $\\operatorname{im}Q$. The restriction $X_p:\\operatorname{im}Q\\to\\operatorname{im}P$ is an isomorphism with inverse $N$, since $NX_p$ is the identity on $\\operatorname{im}Q$ and $X_pN$ is the identity on $\\operatorname{im}P$. The map $X_p$ lowers raw quantum degree by two, while [F3] lowers the quotient grading by one. Thus, for each cohomological degree $i$, if $h_{i,j}$ is the dimension of unreduced cohomology in raw degree $j$ and $r_{i,j}(p)$ the dimension of reduced cohomology in reduced degree $j$, then $h_{i,j}=r_{i,j-1}(p)+r_{i,j+1}(p)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
