          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-oriented-reidemeister-equivalence-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $D$ and $D'$ be regular oriented link diagrams.\nThen $D$ and $D'$ represent equivalent oriented links if and only if $D'$ can be\nobtained from $D$ by a finite sequence of planar isotopies and oriented\nReidemeister moves R1, R2, R3.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-based-link-reduced-khovanov-reidemeister-equivalences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(D,p)$ and $(D',p')$ be finite regular diagrams of nonempty oriented links related by a planar isotopy or one oriented Reidemeister move, with the marked point on an unchanged exterior arc and carried to the corresponding point. The normalized integral signed Khovanov complexes have explicit bigraded chain homotopy equivalences whose maps and inverse homotopies commute with the marked actions $X_p,X_{p'}$. Consequently the quotients $C(D)/\\operatorname{im}X_p$ and $C(D')/\\operatorname{im}X_{p'}$, with their reduced quantum degree $j-1$, are bigraded chain homotopy equivalent. The conclusion holds over $\\mathbb F_2$ by reducing these same integral formulas. It permits arbitrary other link components and arbitrary exterior connectivity. This local statement does not assert a relative movie theorem or canonical identifications between different move sequences.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-reduced-khovanov-over-f2-basepoint-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D$ be a finite regular diagram of an oriented link and let $p,q$ be any two points of $D$ away from crossings, possibly on different link components. Over $\\mathbb F_2$, the reduced Khovanov cohomology groups defined using $p$ and $q$ are isomorphic as bigraded vector spaces. The isomorphism need not be canonical; no diagram-change invariance is asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
