          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-link-surgery-filtered-complex-for-double-branched-cover",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D$ be a finite regular diagram of a nonempty oriented link $L\\subset S^3$ with ordered crossing set $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$. The resolution cube has vertices $I\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, and $D(I)$ is obtained by applying the indicated local smoothing at every crossing. The link-surgery filtered module associated to $D$ is the direct sum\n$$\nC_D=\\bigoplus_{I\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\widehat{CF}(\\Sigma(D(I));\\mathbb F_2),\n$$\nwith the summand for $I$ in increasing filtration degree $n-|I|$. Choose the subordinate Heegaard multi-diagram, compatible perturbation data and top-generator cycles for successive attaching-curve systems. The diagonal component of the candidate differential is the ordinary hat disk differential. For $I<J$ coordinatewise, sum over every ordered path $I=I_0<I_1<\\cdots<I_r=J$ changing one coordinate at each step. Its summand counts the zero-dimensional holomorphic $(r+2)$-gon spaces with input at the $I$ vertex, the selected top-generator cycles between each pair of successive attaching systems, and output at the $J$ vertex. The count includes the allowed source conformal parameters and imposes zero basepoint multiplicity. These are the surgery polygon maps; the component is zero if $I\\nleq J$. Thus changing several crossings is a sum over paths and polygon counts, not an arbitrary direct edge map. The increasing filtration is $F_pC_D=\\bigoplus_{|I|\\ge n-p}\\widehat{CF}(\\Sigma(D(I));\\mathbb F_2)$, extended by zero below $0$ and by $C_D$ at and above $n$. Thus a component increasing $|I|$ lowers its filtration degree and preserves every $F_p$. The cube direction and the reduced Khovanov cochain degree remain separate indices. The polygon-count finiteness, square-zero, and total-homology identifications are justified by the surgery lemma, which labels its actual Ozsváth–Szabó construction and top-input/filling calculations as external inputs, separately from the retained local analytic theorem and finite filtration algebra.\n\nThe local smoothing labels are those of Ozsváth–Szabó Figure1: in the chart with northwest–southeast over-strand, 0 joins the two left and two right endpoints, and 1 joins the two top and two bottom endpoints. These become A and B on the mirror chart, respectively. This fixes the convention for the consumers without a diagram-invariance assertion. The general surgery lemma also applies to links in arbitrary closed connected oriented smooth three-manifolds; this definition is only its branched-cover instance.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-os-link-surgery-filtered-complex-and-triangle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the countable-choice Heegaard Floer analytic package. Let $Y$ be any closed connected oriented smooth three-manifold and let $\\mathbb L$ be an ordered framed $n$-component link in $Y$. On its $i$th exterior boundary torus let $\\mu_i$ be the meridian and $\\lambda_i$ the chosen framing longitude. Use the three filling slopes $\\lambda_i$, $\\mu_i+\\lambda_i$ and $\\mu_i$, labelled $0,1,\\infty$, respectively; $\\infty$ restores the original unfilled component. For $I\\in\\{0,1,\\infty\\}^n$ let $Y(I)$ be the simultaneous filling specified by $I$. Fix an admissible pointed Heegaard multi-diagram subordinate to these surgeries and the compatible perturbation and top-generator choices used by the polygon construction. The mod-two polygon maps on\n$$C(\\mathbb L)=\\bigoplus_{I\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\widehat{CF}(Y(I);\\mathbb F_2)$$\nform a differential with components only from $I$ to $J\\ge I$ coordinatewise. It preserves the increasing filtration\n$$F_pC(\\mathbb L)=\\bigoplus_{|I|\\ge n-p}\\widehat{CF}(Y(I);\\mathbb F_2),$$\nextended by zero below zero and by the whole module at and above $n$. Its total homology is isomorphic to $\\widehat{HF}(Y(\\infty,\\ldots,\\infty);\\mathbb F_2)=\\widehat{HF}(Y;\\mathbb F_2)$. For $n=1$, the total complex is the mapping cone of the $0$-to-$1$ surgery map and is quasi-isomorphic to the hat complex of the third, $\\infty$ filling; this yields the long exact surgery sequence for every surgery triad, with its cyclic ordering. For $n=0$ the cube is the single hat complex of $Y$. No filtered quasi-isomorphism between distinct surgery presentations is included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-hat-heegaard-floer-is-a-closed-three-manifold-invariant",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume ACω. For every closed connected oriented smooth three-manifold $Y$, the homology of the hat Heegaard Floer complex over $\\mathbb F_2$ is independent, up to isomorphism, of the pointed admissible Heegaard diagram used to define it. The assertion is invariance of the group $\\widehat{HF}(Y;\\mathbb F_2)$; it does not assert a diagram-independent filtered chain homotopy type.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-link-surgery-e-one-differential-is-reduced-khovanov",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a fixed finite regular diagram $D$ of a nonempty oriented link $L\\subset S^3$, with $n$ crossings and a basepoint $p\\in D$ away from the crossings, choose an admissible pointed Heegaard multi-diagram subordinate to the branched-cover surgeries and the compatible perturbation data and top-generator cycles of the link-surgery construction. Its first page and first differential are isomorphic as a cochain complex over $\\mathbb F_2$ to the reduced Khovanov cochain complex of the mirror diagram $\\overline D$, based at the corresponding point, in the specified normalized grading shifts. The resulting second page is therefore the based reduced Khovanov cohomology of $\\overline D$. The isomorphism is for this fixed diagram and makes no claim about filtered chain complexes for different diagrams.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-os-filtered-convergence-and-invariance-scope",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(C,d,F)$ be a filtered chain complex over a field with an increasing filtration $0=F_{-1}C\\subseteq F_0C\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq F_nC=C$ and $d(F_pC)\\subseteq F_pC$. Its spectral sequence stabilizes and abuts to $H(C)$ with the image filtration, without any uniform bound outside the finite range $0\\le p\\le n$. If a particular filtered complex has been constructed from a fixed link diagram, this convergence assertion does not itself identify filtered complexes arising from different diagrams.",
