    "thm-os-khovanov-spectral-sequence-to-branched-cover-floer": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-double-branched-cover-of-an-oriented-link-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For every nonempty finite oriented smooth link $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$, the homomorphism $\\epsilon_L:\\pi_1(S^3\\setminus L)\\to\\mathbb Z/2$ sending every component meridian to $1$ is well-defined and surjective. Its connected cover extends over the link with a simple order-two branch model, independently of the tubular charts used to describe that model. If $L'$ is ambient isotopic to $L$, the resulting oriented branched covers $\\Sigma(L')$ and $\\Sigma(L)$ are orientation-preservingly diffeomorphic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-os-spectral-sequence-for-a-fixed-link-diagram",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and the ACω Heegaard Floer analytic package. Let $D$ be any finite regular diagram of a nonempty oriented link $L\\subset S^3$, with a marked point away from crossings. There is a spectral sequence over $\\mathbb F_2$ associated to the link-surgery filtered complex for this fixed diagram whose $E_2$ page is isomorphic, with the normalized grading shifts of the chosen smoothing convention, to $\\widetilde{Kh}(\\overline L,p;\\mathbb F_2)$ computed from the mirror diagram, and whose abutment is $\\widehat{HF}(\\Sigma(L);\\mathbb F_2)$ with its image filtration. This is a fixed-diagram construction; it does not assert a diagram-independent filtered quasi-isomorphism type.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-reduced-khovanov-over-f2-link-invariance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume ACω. For every nonempty oriented link $L\\subset S^3$, the bigraded $\\mathbb F_2$ vector spaces $\\widetilde{Kh}^{i,j}(L;\\mathbb F_2)$ obtained by reducing the normalized Khovanov complex at a point on any chosen component are independent, up to noncanonical bigraded isomorphism, of the diagram and the chosen component. No filtered structure from a Floer construction is part of this invariance statement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
