    "lem-trefoil-knot-group-is-nonabelian-with-abelianization-z": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-knot-wirtinger-presentation-correctness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot, let $D$ be a valid finite planar code for a regular diagram of $K$, choose a basepoint $b$ in the complement, and designate a starting under-arc. The finite procedure $\\mathsf{Wirtinger}(D,a_0)$ terminates and returns a presentation with one positive-meridian generator for each under-arc and one crossing conjugacy relator for each crossing. For the based meridians determined by the diagram's common upper-collar paths from $b$, the homomorphism from the presented group to $\\pi_1(S^3\\setminus K,b)$ that sends each generator to its corresponding based positive meridian is an isomorphism. Changing the designated starting under-arc only relabels the same finite cyclic arc set and gives an isomorphic presentation. If all basing paths are changed by prepending the same based loop, every generator image is conjugated by the same element, so the isomorphism type is unchanged.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-wirtinger-local-meridian-conjugacy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Orient the projection plane by the viewing normal and the ambient orientation. At an oriented crossing let $o$ be the over-arc, and let $n_o$ be its positive in-plane normal: the ordered pair consisting of the over-tangent and $n_o$ is positive. Call the under-side from which $n_o$ points the source $s$, and the side toward which it points the target $t$. A positive meridian is the oriented boundary of a small normal disk whose orientation, preceded by the oriented knot tangent, is the ambient orientation. With the common upper-collar basing paths, the based positive meridians satisfy\n$$m_t=m_o^{-1}m_s m_o.$$\nThis side-based equation is independent of the direction of the understrand. In an oriented chart with over-tangent rightward, the target is the upper side $L$ and source the lower side $R$, so equivalently $m_R=m_o m_Lm_o^{-1}$. In incoming/outgoing notation, the outgoing under-meridian is $m_o^{-1}m_{\\rm in}m_o$ if the under-tangent points along $n_o$, and is $m_om_{\\rm in}m_o^{-1}$ otherwise. Reversing a chosen meridian loop inverts its class. Changing every common access path by the same based loop conjugates all three classes simultaneously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "trefoil-relations",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Applying [F2] to the three crossing triples gives $a=bcb^{-1}$, $b=cac^{-1}$, and $c=aba^{-1}$. Substitute the third equation into the first to obtain $a=baba^{-1}b^{-1}$; right multiplication by $ba$ gives $aba=bab$. Under this relation, $cac^{-1}=aba^{-1}aab^{-1}a^{-1}=abab^{-1}a^{-1}=bab b^{-1}a^{-1}=b$, so the second crossing equation follows. Therefore eliminating $c$ from the presentation in [F1] yields exactly $\\langle a,b\\mid aba=bab\\rangle$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "abelianization",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "In the abelianization, the relation becomes $2[a]+[b]=[a]+2[b]$, hence $[a]=[b]$. The abelianization is the free abelian group on $[a],[b]$ modulo this single relation, so it is $\\mathbb Z$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "symmetric-quotient",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Map $a\\mapsto(12)$ and $b\\mapsto(23)$ in $S_3$. Both $aba$ and $bab$ map to $(13)$, so the relation is satisfied. These two transpositions generate $S_3$, making the homomorphism surjective. Since $S_3$ is nonabelian, its surjective preimage $G_T$ is nonabelian.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
