          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-oriented-slope-and-knot-dehn-filling",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in the oriented $3$-sphere, let $N(K)$ be a chosen closed tubular solid-torus neighborhood, let $E_K=\\overline{S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)}$, and let $T_K=\\partial E_K=\\partial N(K)$. Let $\\mu$ be the positive oriented meridian, meaning the boundary of a positive normal disk in $N(K)$, and let $\\lambda$ be a preferred longitude, meaning a degree-one parallel of the core $K$ in $N(K)$ that is null-homologous in $E_K$. Assume that $[\\mu],[\\lambda]$ are an ordered basis of $H_1(T_K;\\mathbb Z)$, with the boundary-orientation convention $\\mu\\cdot\\lambda=-1$. A slope is the unoriented isotopy class of an essential simple closed curve on $T_K$ whose homology class is $p[\\mu]+q[\\lambda]$ for coprime integers $p,q$ not both zero; $(p,q)$ and $(-p,-q)$ represent the same slope. For a slope represented by $p[\\mu]+q[\\lambda]$, the Dehn filling $E_K(p/q)$ is obtained by gluing an oriented solid torus $D^2\\times S^1$ to $E_K$ by an orientation-reversing boundary homeomorphism that sends $[\\partial D^2\\times\\{1\\}]$ to that slope; the filled orientation agrees with the fixed orientation on $E_K$. If $q\\ne0$, the coefficient is written $p/q$; the meridional slope is $1/0$, and integer $n$-framed filling means slope $n[\\mu]+[\\lambda]$. The oriented homeomorphism type is independent of the gluing map once its meridian image is fixed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice as in the chosen tubular-neighbourhood data. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot with chosen exterior $E_K$ and boundary torus $T_K=\\partial E_K$, and let $p:T_K\\to K$ be the projection of the chosen normal-disk tube. Orient each normal disk so that its positive tangent direction followed by its oriented normal basis agrees with the orientation of $S^3$. A **meridian** $\\mu$ is the oriented boundary of one such normal disk. A **degree-one parallel curve** is a simple closed curve $\\lambda$ in $T_K$ for which $p|_\\lambda:\\lambda\\to K$ has degree $+1$. A **preferred longitude** is a degree-one parallel curve satisfying $i_*[\\lambda]=0$ in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$, where $i:T_K\\hookrightarrow E_K$ is inclusion. This is a condition on a curve; this definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. Reversing the orientation of $K$ reverses the orientations of both $\\mu$ and any degree-one parallel curve. If a subgroup $P\\le\\pi_1(E_K,x)$ is specified using a path from $x$ to $T_K$, record it only up to replacing it by $gPg^{-1}$ for $g\\in\\pi_1(E_K,x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
