          "source": "thm-smooth-loop-theorem-and-incompressibility-criterion-in-orientable-three-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable smooth three-manifold, let $F$ be a compact connected smooth subsurface of $\\partial M$, and let $N\\lhd\\pi_1(F)$ be normal. If a continuous disk map into $M$ has its boundary in $\\operatorname{Int}F$ with class outside $N$, there is a smoothly properly embedded disk in $M$ whose boundary lies in $\\operatorname{Int}F$ and has class outside $N$. No pointwise preservation of the original boundary loop is asserted.\n\nConsequently a compact neatly properly embedded two-sided smooth surface $S$ in such a compact orientable smooth three-manifold is incompressible if and only if its inclusion induces an injection on fundamental groups, component by component. Here incompressibility means absence of a smooth embedded compressing disk, with the conventions of the indicated definition.",
