    "lem-root-jones-wenzl-recursion-and-negligible-ideal": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-jones-wenzl-idempotents-exist-below-root-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p\\ge4$ be even, let $A$ be a primitive $2p$-th root of unity, and use the quantum integers and Temperley–Lieb loop value $\\delta=-A^2-A^{-2}$ specified above. Write $r=p/2$. The standard Jones–Wenzl recursion defines nonzero idempotents $p_n$ for $0\\le n\\le r-1$; their unnormalized closure traces are $[n+1]$. In particular, $p_{r-1}$ has trace zero and is negligible, while the next standard recursive formula for $p_r$ is undefined because its denominator is $[r]=0$. This statement concerns that recursion and does not assert that no differently constructed normalized idempotent can exist at rank $r$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-temperley-lieb-diagram-category",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring with unit ([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]) and let $\\delta\\in R$. The object labels are the nonnegative integers $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$. For labels $m,n$, place bottom points $b_1,\\ldots,b_m$ and top points $t_1,\\ldots,t_n$ along the rectangle from left to right on their respective edges. Their cyclic order is $t_1,\\ldots,t_n,b_m,\\ldots,b_1$. When $m=n=0$, the empty matching is included.\n\nAn $(m,n)$ **diagram** is a perfect matching of these $m+n$ boundary labels which is noncrossing: there are no four labels $a,b,c,d$ in cyclic order whose matched pairs are $\\{a,c\\}$ and $\\{b,d\\}$. A picture consists of disjoint arcs realizing the matching; equality is equality of the matching, so planar deformation that fixes the boundary does not change a diagram. If $m+n$ is odd, there are no such diagrams. Write $\\mathcal D(m,n)$ for this set and define the $R$-module\n\n$$M_R(m,n):=R^{(\\mathcal D(m,n))},$$\n\nthe free $R$-module on the set of diagrams ([[def-free-module-on-a-set-and-standard-basis]]). In particular, its diagram basis is empty when $m+n$ is odd.\n\nFor basis diagrams $D:m\\to n$ and $E:n\\to p$, stack $E$ above $D$ and identify the $n$ matching boundary points. The resulting finite graph has degree two at each identified point, where degree means the number of incident arcs. Each component is a path joining two exterior boundary points or a closed cycle. Remove every cycle and multiply the remaining exterior matching by $\\delta^c$, where $c$ is the number of removed cycles. Denote this output by $E\\circ D$ and extend the rule $R$-bilinearly to a map $M_R(n,p)\\times M_R(m,n)\\to M_R(m,p)$. A following result proves closure of these operations on the diagram modules and the category axioms.\n\nThe matching $1_n:n\\to n$ pairs each bottom point to the top point directly above it. Horizontal juxtaposition, with the left diagram placed first, defines $m\\otimes m'=m+m'$ on object labels and $D\\otimes D'$ on basis diagrams; extend it $R$-bilinearly to the modules. A following result proves that these operations give a strict monoidal category.\n\nFor $1\\le i<n$, let $d_i\\in M_R(n,n)$ be the diagram with a cap joining top points $i,i+1$, a cup joining bottom points $i,i+1$, and vertical through strands at every other position. Define $\\mathrm{TL}_n(R,\\delta)$ to be the $R$-module $M_R(n,n)$ with bilinear multiplication $xy:=x\\circ y$ and distinguished element $1_n$. A following result proves that it is a unital associative $R$-algebra and identifies it with the endomorphism algebra at object $n$.\n\nThe left factor is stacked above the right factor. The loop value is $\\delta$; no invertibility or nonzero condition on $\\delta$ is part of these data.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-temperley-lieb-negligible-morphisms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Work over $\\mathbb C$ with a fixed loop parameter $\\delta$. Let $\\mathcal T_\\delta$ be the strict Temperley–Lieb diagram category of [[def-temperley-lieb-diagram-category]]: its objects are $n\\in\\mathbb N$, and its morphism space $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal T_\\delta}(m,n)$ is the complex span of the noncrossing matching diagrams with $m$ bottom and $n$ top boundary points, composed by stacking and loop removal.\n\nFor a basis endomorphism $D:n\\to n$, let $c(D)$ be the number of components after joining each top point to the corresponding bottom point outside the rectangle. Define the unnormalized closure trace by\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}_n(D)=\\delta^{c(D)},$$\nand extend linearly. This definition is valid for every $\\delta\\in\\mathbb C$, including $\\delta=0$.\n\nFor $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$, the subspace of **negligible morphisms** is\n$$\\mathcal N_\\delta(m,n)= \\{f\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal T_\\delta}(m,n): \\operatorname{Tr}_m(g\\circ f)=0\\text{ for every }g\\in\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal T_\\delta}(n,m)\\}.$$\nThis definition specifies subspaces only. Their closure under composition and tensor product, and hence the quotient category, are supplied separately.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-temperley-lieb-negligible-morphisms-form-tensor-ideal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\delta\\in\\mathbb C$. The subspaces $\\mathcal N_\\delta(m,n)$ of [[def-temperley-lieb-negligible-morphisms]] are closed under composition on either side by arbitrary Temperley–Lieb morphisms and under tensoring on either side by arbitrary Temperley–Lieb morphisms. Thus they form a tensor ideal.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-temperley-lieb-closure-trace-component-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For any commutative ring $R$, loop value $\\delta\\in R$, and $n\\ge0$, the unnormalized trace of [[def-temperley-lieb-closure-trace-and-partial-trace]] satisfies\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}_n(xy)=\\operatorname{Tr}_n(yx),\\qquad \\operatorname{Tr}_{n+1}(x\\otimes1)=\\delta\\operatorname{Tr}_n(x)$$\n\nfor $x,y\\in\\mathrm{TL}_n(R,\\delta)$. For $n\\ge1$ and $x\\in\\mathrm{TL}_n(R,\\delta)$, it also satisfies\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}_{n+1}((x\\otimes1)U_n)=\\operatorname{Tr}_n(x),$$\n\nwhere $U_n$ is the cup-cap generator on the last two strands of $\\mathrm{TL}_{n+1}$. For $n\\ge1$, $x\\in\\mathrm{TL}_{n-1}$ and $h\\in\\mathrm{TL}_n$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}_n\\bigl(h(x\\otimes1)\\bigr)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{n-1}\\bigl(\\operatorname{ptr}_n(h)x\\bigr).$$\n\nNo division by $\\delta$ occurs.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-temperley-lieb-jones-wenzl-partial-trace-and-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a nonzero commutative ring with unit, $\\delta\\in R$, and suppose $[2],\\ldots,[n]$ are units. The projector $p_n$ of [[def-temperley-lieb-jones-wenzl-recursion-over-unit-denominators]] has coefficient $1$ on the identity diagram, is nonzero and idempotent, and is annihilated on both sides by each $U_i$ for $1\\le i<n$. Its corner is $p_n\\mathrm{TL}_n p_n=R p_n$, so it is the unique idempotent with identity coefficient $1$ and those two-sided annihilation conditions. For $n\\ge1$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{ptr}_n(p_n)=\\frac{[n+1]}{[n]}p_{n-1},\\qquad \\operatorname{Tr}_n(p_n)=[n+1].$$\n\nOnly the displayed denominator units are assumed; $\\delta$ need not be a unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-temperley-lieb-rank-one-pivotal-ribbon-conversion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use all $\\ell\\ge3$, $q=e^{\\pi i/\\ell}$, $s=e^{\\pi i/(2\\ell)}$, $\\delta=q+q^{-1}$ and the full tilting category of [[def-temperley-lieb-rank-one-lusztig-tilting-category]]. Put $V=\\Delta(1)$ and let $P_0$ be its trivial-channel projector in $V\\otimes V=\\Delta(2)\\oplus\\Delta(0)$. The maps\n\n$$\\rho_n:\\mathrm{TL}_n(\\mathbb C,\\delta)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathcal T_\\ell}(V^{\\otimes n}),\\qquad U_i\\longmapsto\\delta P_{0,i,i+1}$$\n\nare well defined. Their composite with the negligible quotient has kernel exactly the embedded boundary ideal $I_n=(p_{\\ell-1})$ and induces\n\n$$\\mathrm{TL}_n(\\mathbb C,\\delta)/I_n\\ \\cong\\ \\operatorname{End}_{\\mathcal T_\\ell/\\mathcal N_\\ell}(V^{\\otimes n}).$$\n\nEquivalently the target is the full centralizer on a chosen sum of the retained tilting summands. The unreduced map does **not** kill $p_{\\ell-1}$: its image is the nonzero simple $\\Delta(\\ell-1)$.\n\nThe precise categorical comparison is with the additive Karoubi negligible quotient of $\\mathrm{TL}(-\\delta)$, equipped with its ordinary diagram braiding $c=s1+s^{-1}e$ and with planar pivotal structure multiplied by tensor parity $(-1)^n$. This is ribbon-equivalent to $\\mathcal T_\\ell/\\mathcal N_\\ell$. Its endomorphism algebras are identified with positive-loop TL algebras by $U_i=-e_i$. Its pivotal dimensions are $[a+1]_q$, and its twists on retained labels are $s^{a(a+2)}$. The quantum self-duality cup/cap have loop $-\\delta$ and Frobenius–Schur sign $-1$, whereas the parity-corrected pivotal closure has value $+\\delta$ on $V$. Thus no equivalence of the unmodified positive-loop unoriented **pivotal** TL category is asserted.\n\nOn the highest-weight-$2$ and trivial prequotient channels, the quantum crossing eigenvalues are $s,-s^{-3}$; the Hecke element $T_i=qU_i-1=-s c_i^{-1}$ has eigenvalues $-1,q^2$. The highest-weight-$2$ channel is retained only when $\\ell\\ge4$. Pivotal traces, twists and all framed ribbon closures agree under the stated corrected comparison. Assume countable choice only when invoking the cited free framed-tangle evaluation theorem; the local matrix and path arguments use no choice axiom.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-temperley-lieb-root-negligible-quotient-is-semisimple",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $q=e^{\\pi i/\\ell}$, $\\ell\\ge3$, $\\delta=q+q^{-1}$ and $\\tau_n=\\delta^{-n}\\operatorname{Tr}_n$. Put\n\n$$\\operatorname{Rad}(\\tau_n)=\\{x\\in\\mathrm{TL}_n:\\tau_n(xy)=0\\text{ for every }y\\in\\mathrm{TL}_n\\}.$$\n\nLet $I_n$ be the two-sided ideal generated by $p_{\\ell-1}\\otimes1_{n-\\ell+1}$ for $n\\ge\\ell-1$, and $I_n=0$ below that rank. Then $I_n=\\operatorname{Rad}(\\tau_n)$, and $\\mathrm{TL}_n/I_n$ is the direct sum of full matrix algebras with multiplicities given by length-$n$ paths from $0$ of heights $0,\\ldots,\\ell-2$. The additive idempotent-complete negligible quotient has simple labels $0,\\ldots,\\ell-2$, represented by $p_a$, and\n\n$$a\\otimes b\\cong\\bigoplus_{\\substack{|a-b|\\le c\\le\\min(a+b,2\\ell-4-a-b)\\\\c\\equiv a+b\\ (2)}}c.$$\n\nAt $n=\\ell-1$ the unreduced algebra is semisimple, while its trace radical is the nonzero one-dimensional block generated by $p_{\\ell-1}$. Trace radical and Jacobson radical are therefore distinct at this first boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1.1",
          "claim": "By the boundary-projector result, $p_{r-1}$ is an idempotent with trace zero. The diagram category [F2] supplies the endomorphism algebra $\\mathrm{TL}_{r-1}$ and its closure trace. For any $g\\in\\mathrm{TL}_{r-1}$, [F6] gives $p_{r-1}gp_{r-1}=\\lambda p_{r-1}$ for some $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C$. Cyclicity [F5] gives $\\operatorname{Tr}_{r-1}(g\\circ p_{r-1})=\\operatorname{Tr}_{r-1}(p_{r-1}gp_{r-1})=\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}_{r-1}(p_{r-1})=0$. Thus $p_{r-1}$ itself is negligible by [F3].",
