          "id": "homological-linking",
          "claim": "**Symmetry of linking.** Let $c,d\\subset S^3$ be disjoint oriented embedded circles. Choose compact oriented embedded surfaces $A,B$ with boundaries $c,d$ by [F8], and a regular diagram $D$ of their boundary link by [F7]. For $X=\\operatorname{Int}A$, let $f:X\\to S^3\\subset\\mathbb R^4$ be the inclusion. A sufficiently small boundary collar misses $d$; choose an open collar region $G$ on which $f$ is transverse to $d$, and protect a smaller collar. Then $K=X\\setminus G$ is compact. Choose a relatively compact open $W\\subset X$ containing $K$ and avoiding the protected smaller collar, using finitely many coordinate neighbourhoods of $K$. By [F12] choose $\\chi:X\\to[0,1]$, equal to one near $K$ and with compact support in $W$. Where $\\chi=0$, $f$ is already transverse to $d$. For $v$ in a small open ball in $\\mathbb R^4$ put $\\mathcal F(p,v)=(f(p)+\\chi(p)v)/\\|f(p)+\\chi(p)v\\|$. The denominator is nonzero for $\\|v\\|<1/2$. Where $\\chi>0$, the derivative in $v$ is $\\chi/\\|f+\\chi v\\|$ times the orthogonal projection onto $T_{\\mathcal F(p,v)}S^3$, hence is surjective. Where $\\chi=0$, nonnegativity gives $d\\chi=0$, and the old source transversality applies. Thus the total family is transverse to $d$. Parametric transversality and the dense-complement statement [F12] give a transverse slice in every sufficiently small parameter ball. The family equals $f$ off the compact support and converges uniformly in value and first derivative to $f$ there as $v\\to0$, directly by differentiation of the displayed smooth formula on the compact set. It therefore extends by the identity across $\\partial A$ and remains an embedding for sufficiently small $v$: finite compact coordinate subcharts retain a uniform differential lower bound for nearby source points, and compact pairs outside those chart neighbourhoods retain positive image separation. The surface still has oriented boundary $c$ and is transverse to $d$. Apply the identical explicit family to $\\operatorname{Int}B$, protecting a collar of $d$ and taking target $c$. No closed double or disjointness of an added half is asserted. Now [F5] applies to these actual transverse spanning surfaces in both orders, with the same boundary diagram $D$. It equates $\\operatorname{lk}(c,d)$ and $\\operatorname{lk}(d,c)$ with the two diagram half-sums, which agree by [F11]. Linking is therefore symmetric. It is additive in its first cycle by the homological coefficient homomorphism [F3], and symmetry gives additivity in the other cycle.",
