          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-grid-bridge-like-refinement",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite nonempty planar marked grid with vertical-over crossing convention. A horizontal segment is a cap if its two incident vertical segments both extend above it or both extend below it; otherwise it is monotone. A marked stabilization splits a marked cell into a two-by-two block with the chosen marking type twice at opposite corners, the other type once and one empty corner, preserving one of each type per row and column. A legal column commutation exchanges adjacent columns with disjoint or strictly nested closed vertical marking intervals. There is a finite sequence of marked stabilizations and legal commutations carrying $G$ to a grid in which every cap has no crossing and every monotone horizontal segment has exactly one crossing. Thus the resulting grid is bridge-like: slightly tilt its crossing-containing horizontal segments in the direction of height travel and round its empty caps to obtain a bridge diagram. Every operation respects the oriented $O,X$ convention of the grid-diagram definition, and the number of link components is unchanged. The decorated planar diagram before and after the entire refinement is planar isotopic after rounding corners; in particular no Reidemeister move is needed for this refinement. No global grid-move generation theorem is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-oriented-reidemeister-equivalence-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $D$ and $D'$ be regular oriented link diagrams.\nThen $D$ and $D'$ represent equivalent oriented links if and only if $D'$ can be\nobtained from $D$ by a finite sequence of planar isotopies and oriented\nReidemeister moves R1, R2, R3.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-planar-isotopy-preserves-grid-move-class",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $D_0,D_1$ be nonempty oriented bridge diagrams that are planar isotopic with crossing decorations transported by the isotopy. Then any bridge-like grid rectification of $D_0$ is connected to any bridge-like grid rectification of $D_1$ by finitely many marked grid stabilizations, legal commutations and destabilizations. Here vertical strands pass over horizontal strands, a legal commutation exchanges adjacent rows or columns with disjoint or strictly nested closed marking intervals, and a stabilization is a three-mark two-by-two split preserving one $O$ and one $X$ per row and column. In particular the grid-move class assigned by bridge rectification depends only on the oriented decorated planar-isotopy class whenever that class is represented by bridge diagrams. No Reidemeister-move or global grid-isotopy generation theorem is used.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-grid-reidemeister-moves-with-arbitrary-exterior",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Suppose two nonempty oriented decorated planar diagrams differ by one Reidemeister move inside a disk, retaining the entire exterior and its boundary incidence. Each endpoint admits a bridge representative in its decorated planar-isotopy class. Let $D_0^\\prime,D_1^\\prime$ be any bridge diagrams planar isotopic to the respective endpoints. Then any bridge-like grid rectifications of $D_0^\\prime,D_1^\\prime$ are connected by finitely many marked grid stabilizations, legal commutations and destabilizations. All signs, strand orientations and consistent strict over/under orders of R3 are included. The exterior diagram is arbitrary. A legal commutation exchanges adjacent rows or columns whose closed marking intervals are disjoint or strictly nested; a stabilization is a three-mark two-by-two split preserving one mark of each type in each row and column. No shared-endpoint switch is assumed as a primitive move. The conclusion uses no global grid-isotopy generation theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
