    "thm-grid-commutation-stabilization-invariance-proof": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-grid-filtered-pentagon-hexagon-equivalences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G,G'$ be knot grids related by exchanging two consecutive columns whose marking-height intervals are disjoint or strictly nested. The same assertion holds for rows, using intervals of marking abscissae, and for a switch, where an $X$ and an $O$ of the exchanged columns share a row. Identify variables by their individual $O$-markings, retaining the full polynomial ring. There are explicit filtered chain maps $P:C^-(G)\\to C^-(G')$ and $Q:C^-(G')\\to C^-(G)$ given by empty pentagon counts with their $O$-monomial weights, and filtered homotopies $H,H'$ given by empty hexagon counts, satisfying\n$$dH+Hd=\\operatorname{id}+QP,\\qquad d'H'+H'd'=\\operatorname{id}+PQ.$$\nThe maps preserve Maslov grading and do not increase Alexander filtration; the homotopies have Maslov degree $+1$ and do not increase filtration. Taking the associated graded retains precisely the polygons avoiding $X$. Setting all $O$ variables to zero in that associated-graded equivalence gives the tilde equivalence. The maps and homotopies remain valid under any coefficient specialization.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-grid-filtered-destabilization-domain-map",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G'$ be an $X$ stabilization of type southwest of a knot grid $G$. In the four cells incident to the new crossing $c$, the northwest cell contains $X_1$, the northeast cell contains the new $O_1$, the southeast cell contains $X_2$, and the southwest cell is empty. Let $O_2$ be the old $O$ in the row of $X_2$. Write $I$ for states containing $c$, and identify such a state $\\mathbf y$ with $e(\\mathbf y)=\\mathbf y\\setminus\\{c\\}$ in $G$. Retain the independent variables of all markings.\n\nAn incoming left domain is either the zero domain or a nonnegative domain from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf y\\in I$ with the following properties. At every state corner other than $c$, at least three adjacent cell multiplicities vanish; near $c$, three multiplicities equal $k$ and the southwest multiplicity is $k-1$; exactly $2k+1$ coordinates of $\\mathbf y$ are absent from $\\mathbf x$. For an incoming right domain, replace the southwest exception by southeast multiplicity $k+1$, and the number of absent coordinates by $2k+2$; the zero domain is not of right type. Denote these sets by $\\pi^{iL},\\pi^{iR}$. Define $D^{iL},D^{iR}$ by counting their targets $e(\\mathbf y)$, with weight $\\prod_{j\\ne1}U_j^{O_j(p)}$, omitting the power of the new variable. Then\n$$D=(D^{iL},D^{iR}):C^-(G')\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Cone}(U_1+U_2:C^-(G)[U_1]\\to C^-(G)[U_1])$$\nis a finite polynomial-module chain map preserving Maslov degree and not increasing Alexander filtration. The first cone summand is shifted down by one in both degree and filtration. Its associated-graded map counts only domains avoiding $X_1,X_3,\\ldots$; these have complexity at most two and give exactly the usual graded destabilization map: deletion of $c$ on $I$ in the first component, and rectangles through $X_2$ on the other states in the second component.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-grid-destabilization-filtered-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a southwest $X$ stabilization, the incoming-domain map from the full filtered grid complex to the cone of $U_1+U_2$ on the polynomial extension of the smaller grid complex is a filtered chain homotopy equivalence over the full polynomial ring. Its inverse and both inverse homotopies can be computed by finite polynomial matrix formulas from empty rectangles and incoming domains. Composing with the explicit cone retraction gives filtered equivalence with the smaller complex over any retained old variable action, without imposing relations on its other variables. After simultaneous ordinary collapse of the marking variables, stabilization contributes the zero-differential factor $V$ of bidegrees $(0,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$. The same $V$ factor occurs on tilde homology.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-grid-changing-chosen-variable-action",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C$ be the full filtered complex of a knot grid with independent marking variables. The same underlying complex, regarded as a module over $\\mathbb F_2[U]$ with $U$ acting as $U_i$, has filtered chain homotopy type independent of $i$. This assertion concerns restriction of scalars; no marking variables are identified or set to zero. For consecutive markings the equivalence and its inverse homotopies are explicit polynomial divided-difference formulas and finite weighted rectangle counts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-pointed-basepoint-cone-and-variable-collapse",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R=\\mathbb F_2[U,Z_1,\\ldots,Z_s]$, and let $(C,d)$ be a graded, Alexander-filtered chain complex of free $R$-modules. Every variable has Maslov degree $-2$ and Alexander filtration degree $-1$, and $d$ has Maslov degree $-1$ and does not increase filtration. Extend scalars to $C[T]$, with $T$ having the same degrees. Put $f=T+U$ and form the cone $B=C[T]^{\\mathrm{low}}\\oplus C[T]$, with differential $D(a,b)=(da,db+fa)$; the first copy is shifted down by one in both Maslov degree and filtration. Regarded over $R$, this cone has an explicit filtered chain deformation retraction onto $C$. Its projection is evaluation of the second component at $T=U$, its inclusion is $c\\mapsto(0,c)$, and its homotopy is polynomial divided difference in the second component, placed in the first component.\n\nIn contrast, the ordinary scalar quotient $T=U$ taken before homology gives the zero-map cone $C\\oplus C^{\\mathrm{low}}$, equivalently $C\\otimes V$, where $V$ has zero differential and bidegrees $(0,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$. Any further ordinary quotient of the old variables has the same direct-sum description with the corresponding quotient of $C$. Thus an equivalence of a basepoint insertion complex with this cone over the full polynomial ring implies invariance for the chosen single-variable action and an extra $V$ factor after simultaneous collapse. These are different operations.\n\nMore generally, let $E$ be free over $R[T]$ on a homogeneous filtered basis, and let its differential $d(T)$ have the same degree and filtration behavior, with polynomial matrix entries. Then $\\operatorname{Cone}(T+U:E\\to E)$ admits an explicit filtered deformation retraction over $R$ onto the ordinary quotient $(E/(T+U)E,d(U))$. If multiplication by $T+U$ on $E$ has a polynomial-linear homotopy $L$ with $dL+Ld=T+U$, Maslov degree $-1$, and filtration degree at most $-1$, this quotient is filtered chain homotopy equivalent over $R$ to $E\\otimes V$. Iterating applies to independent marking variables and survives every earlier scalar quotient. Thus pairwise such homotopies imply that simultaneous collapse of $n$ variables to one has type $E\\otimes V^{\\otimes(n-1)}$ over the chosen variable action.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "A cyclic permutation is a change of fundamental cut on the same grid torus. It identifies states, rectangles, and marking monomials bijectively. The finite pair-count gradings also agree under this change. To check this directly for a column cut, use the signed set $P=\\mathbf x-\\mathbb O$ in $M_O=I(P,P)+1$. The wrapping column contains one state point of sign $+1$ and one $O$ of sign $-1$. Only their comparisons with nonwrapping points change. At an integer state height the signed number of all points below is zero, and at the height of a marking in row $r$ it is one, since the states have heights $0,\\ldots,n-1$ and the markings have one height in each open row. Thus the signed difference of the below/above counts for either wrapping point, excluding itself, is one. Multiplying by the two signs and adding gives zero; the comparisons between the two wrapping points cancel from this sum and do not change. Hence $I(P,P)$ is unchanged. Apply the same count to $\\mathbf x-\\mathbb X$, and interchange coordinates for a row cut. Both Maslov functions and the Alexander formula are unchanged. The torus-identification map is therefore a filtered chain isomorphism, with inverse the opposite cut change and zero inverse homotopies.",
