          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The other local stabilization types reduce to this one using the following finite operations. An $O$ stabilization introduces a vertical segment joining marks in consecutive rows. Commute this short segment horizontally toward the next $X$ endpoint; while passing another vertical segment, its length-one interval is either disjoint or nested, since no marking height lies strictly between its two endpoints. At a shared endpoint level the exchange is a switch. Stop at the next $X$, where deleting that short segment is an $X$ destabilization back to the original grid. Tracking the empty corner gives the pairs $O{:}NE$ with $X{:}SW$, $O{:}SE$ with $X{:}NW$, $O{:}NW$ with $X{:}SE$, and $O{:}SW$ with $X{:}NE$. Among the four $X$ types, exchanging the two new adjacent rows moves the empty corner across a horizontal side, and exchanging the two new adjacent columns moves it across a vertical side; these are switches because the central opposite-type marking shares a column or row with the adjacent same-type marking. One or two such switches carry any empty corner to the southwest corner. These operations concern only the added row, added column, and their endpoint segments; after deleting them the original row and column orders are unchanged. Thus every stabilization is a southwest $X$ stabilization followed by finitely many commutations, switches, and cut changes.",
