          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-balanced-multipointed-knot-heegaard-moves-generate-diagram-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let two balanced multipointed Heegaard diagrams be compatible with the same oriented knot $K\\subset S^3$. Each has its actual embedded splitting, simultaneous trivial-arc systems and compression data, a genus-$h$ surface, $k\\ge1$ ordered pairs $w_i,z_i$, and $m=h+k-1$ curves in each attaching system. The lower arcs run from $w_i$ to $z_i$ and the upper arcs from $z_i$ to $w_{i-1}$, with cyclic indices. The two diagrams may have different genera and different numbers of pairs. Include the full smooth input convention allowing tangential marked knot crossings and auxiliary proper compression-disk witnesses; no additional neatness hypothesis is imposed.\n\nThere is a finite comparison between them using curve isotopies and same-system handleslides avoiding all marks, genus-one stabilizations or destabilizations in knot-disjoint disks, and actual basepoint-pair stabilizations or destabilizations along the represented knot. The latter keep the genus fixed and change $k$ by one; their inverse configuration consists of surface disks bounded by $\\alpha_0,\\beta_0$ meeting twice and no other opposite-system curve, with their common bigon containing just the new $w_0$, the first disk outside the second containing just the new $z_0$, and the second outside the first containing the next old $z$-point in the cyclic pairing. Removing that configuration concatenates the adjacent actual trivial arcs. Thus no isolated marked knot component is added in an empty disk.\n\nThe comparison permits the explicit orientation-preserving diagram isomorphisms carrying the ordered marks, attaching systems and actual arc systems, and the fixed-splitting arc normalizations of the pointed-data definition, before or after its geometric moves. Every intermediate geometric diagram remains balanced with $m=h+k-1$, the same oriented knot, and the stated simultaneous bridge data. It is admissible in that definition's integral periodic-domain sense. The case $k=1$, including $h=0,m=0$, is included. This theorem establishes geometric move generation; it makes no assertion about analytic continuation maps or disk counts.",
