      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-grid-alexander-grading-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a toroidal grid of grid number $n$ representing one oriented knot, and let $M_{\\mathbb O}$, $M_{\\mathbb X}$, and $A_G$ be the functions defined from its finite point sets and the pair-counting function $J$. Then $M_{\\mathbb O}$ and $M_{\\mathbb X}$ are integer-valued, and every rectangle $r$ from a grid state $\\mathbf x$ to a grid state $\\mathbf y$ satisfies $M_{\\mathbb O}(\\mathbf x)-M_{\\mathbb O}(\\mathbf y)=1-2n_{\\mathbb O}(r)+2\\#(\\mathbf x\\cap\\operatorname{Int}r)$ and $M_{\\mathbb X}(\\mathbf x)-M_{\\mathbb X}(\\mathbf y)=1-2n_{\\mathbb X}(r)+2\\#(\\mathbf x\\cap\\operatorname{Int}r)$. Consequently $A_G$ is integer-valued and $A_G(\\mathbf x)-A_G(\\mathbf y)=n_{\\mathbb X}(r)-n_{\\mathbb O}(r)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-realized-grid-whitney-classes-have-the-full-relative-grading-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $G$ be an oriented knot grid of size $n\\ge2$, with its actual flat torus and product generator end charts. Write $M_O,M_X$ and $A_G=(M_O-M_X)/2-(n-1)/2$ for the grid grading functions. For every actual continuous Whitney class $\\phi:x\\to y$ in $\\operatorname{Sym}^n(T)$, define its domain and marked multiplicities by the relative point-divisor degrees of any smooth model. Its actual fixed-end Fredholm index $\\mu(\\phi)$, before source translation, satisfies\n$$\\mu(\\phi)=M_O(x)-M_O(y)+2\\sum_{i=1}^n n_{O_i}(\\phi),$$\nand\n$$A_G(x)-A_G(y)=\\sum_{i=1}^n n_{X_i}(\\phi)-\\sum_{i=1}^n n_{O_i}(\\phi).$$\nThese identities hold for all realized classes, with positive, zero or negative marked multiplicities. In particular no condition $n_X=0$ is imposed. They remain valid for the actual linearized index of a smooth finite-energy holomorphic strip with generator ends and the protected product end data, under the auxiliary complex-structure homotopies in the suppliers.\n\nConsequently, an actual index-one holomorphic strip counted with weight $\\prod_i U_i^{n_{O_i}}$, if all its marked multiplicities are nonnegative, lowers $M_O$ by one and drops the $A_G$ filtration by exactly $\\sum_i n_{X_i}$, when $U_i$ has bidegree $(-2,-1)$. This is the full filtered relative grading assertion, rather than only its zero-$X$ associated graded specialization. It does not assert existence or finiteness of counts, square-zero, move invariance, a grid-to-analytic equivalence, or identification of an absolute analytic grading normalization.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-realized-flat-grid-index-one-positive-domains-are-empty-rectangles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $G$ be a knot grid of size $n\\ge2$ on its actual flat torus, with horizontal alpha and vertical beta circles and the product-integrable end data. Let $u:x\\to y$ be any REALIZED continuous Whitney class, with integral domain $D$. At a grid vertex $v$ write $\\nu_v(D)$ for one quarter of the sum of its four adjacent cell coefficients, and put $n_x(D)=\\sum_{v\\in x}\\nu_v(D)$. Then its actual fixed-end real index before translation is\n$$\\mu(u)=n_x(D)+n_y(D).$$\nThis includes signed domains and positive marked degrees. If every cell coefficient is nonnegative and $\\mu(u)=1$, then $x,y$ differ in exactly two points and $D$ is exactly one embedded torus rectangle from $x$ to $y$, of multiplicity one and with no state point in its interior. No exclusion of $O$ or $X$ marks is imposed. Its row and column intervals are both proper cyclic intervals, so it is an ordinary rectangle among the four choices for those changed corners.\n\nThis proves actual index and domain classification, not existence or uniqueness of holomorphic representatives, their count, equality of equal-domain homotopy classes, or a grid-to-analytic chain equivalence.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
