            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-integrable-flat-grid-rectangle-counts-equal-the-full-filtered-grid-differential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Take an actual knot grid of size $n\\ge2$, its flat complex torus and the constant path $J_t=\\operatorname{Sym}^n(j_{\\mathrm{flat}})$. Use its actual row and column product tori, $w=O,z=X$, and their flat product generator charts. Every empty-state oriented rectangle $R:x\\to y$, with arbitrary $O$ and $X$ markings in its interior, has exactly ONE orbit of actual finite-energy holomorphic strips of domain $R$ under longitudinal translation. That strip has actual fixed-end index one and its actual linearization is onto. Every actual index-one strip is one of these rectangle strips. Thus all actual index-one orbits, across all endpoints and classes, are finite and regular for this constant path.\n\nConsequently the full filtered polynomial disk-count matrix for this chosen analytic datum is EXACTLY the full filtered grid differential, on the identical state basis over $\\mathbb F_2[U_1,\\ldots,U_n]$, with each rectangle weighted by $\\prod U_i^{O_i(R)}$ and filtration drop $n_X(R)$. It squares to zero by the finite rectangle-composition proof. Taking its zero-drop part gives the associated-graded minus matrix; then setting all variables to zero gives the tilde matrix. The identity preserves the relative Maslov and Alexander differences when assigned by the grid grading formulas.\n\nThis is a chosen-data analytic count and chain identification. It does not assert independence from auxiliary data, normalized absolute analytic gradings, a singly pointed reduction or its stable factors, move invariance, or identification with an independently constructed invariant. Equal-domain Whitney homotopy classes are not presumed equal: uniqueness is for actual strip orbits and therefore also accounts for every class represented by such a strip.",
