          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-proper-compression-disks-admit-neat-witnesses-with-standard-cut-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $U\\subset S^3$ be a compact smooth three-manifold with smooth boundary and let a finite disjoint family of smooth rank-two embedded disks be proper in the following broad sense: each disk has its boundary in $\\partial U$ and its interior in $\\operatorname{Int}U$. No transversality at its boundary is required. Suppose the topological cut components are three-balls. Then the disks can be replaced by arbitrarily $C^1$-close neat compression disks with exactly the SAME boundary curves, unchanged outside arbitrarily small boundary collars. The new topological cut components correspond to the old ones. After ordinary finite smooth corner rounding the new components are smoothly parametrized standard balls, with labelled disk faces and compatible product collars. Reattaching the disk neighborhoods recovers the actual $U$ and the new disks as their cocores.\n\nThe replacement can be supported away from any fixed compact set disjoint from the disk boundary collars, in particular the actual knot arcs and any supplied simultaneous bridge witnesses disjoint from the disks. These objects, their endpoints, ordered pairing and face avoidance are retained. Only the auxiliary compression witnesses change; the surface, attaching curves and actual knot stay fixed. The transport across the initial tangency is a boundary-fixing homeomorphism, smooth separately off its tangency edges; a smooth ambient boundary-preserving diffeomorphism changing tangency is not claimed. The smooth standard-ball assertion uses the full smooth three-dimensional Schoenflies theorem under Countable Choice, rather than a general topological smoothing theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
