        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-marked-planar-cut-surface-regions-are-realized-by-disk-slides",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $U$ be either handlebody of a balanced multipointed knot diagram in the sense of the balanced pointed-diagram definition, with its specified compression disks $D_1,\\ldots,D_m$, marked endpoints, and disjoint simultaneously boundary-parallel knot arcs. Cutting $U$ along all these disks gives $k$ balls. Each disk gives two distinguished disk faces on the boundaries of these balls.\n\nSuppose that one cut-ball boundary contains a compact planar subsurface $R$, supplied with a disk-with-holes parametrization, whose boundary consists of an outer circle $c$ and $r+1$ inner circles bounding disk faces $E_0,\\ldots,E_r$. These faces come from distinct disks $D_{i_0},\\ldots,D_{i_r}$. The interior of $R$ misses every other disk face, its closure misses all marked points, and the faces have disjoint interiors from $R$. Then $D_{i_0}$ can be replaced by a disk with boundary isotopic to $c$ by exactly $r$ disk slides over $D_{i_1},\\ldots,D_{i_r}$ in some order, followed by a curve isotopy. The slide bands and the final isotopy miss all marks and all other attaching curves of this system. The other disks remain unchanged, and at each completed slide the disks cut $U$ into $k$ balls, each containing the same one trivial arc under an identification fixing the actual arcs. Thus every resulting full diagram is balanced and represents the same oriented knot. The assertion concerns completed slides; it does not require the sliding curves to be disjoint during a band-sum operation.\n\nFor $r=0$ only an isotopy is needed. Opposite-system curves may cross these bands; a small perturbation makes the two systems transverse again without crossing marks. No relative generic-family existence assertion is part of this lemma.",
          "uses": [
