          "source": "thm-fixed-background-zero-marked-continuation-counts-give-chain-homotopy-equivalences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Fix a globally weakly admissible complete pointed Heegaard diagram of ANY closed connected oriented smooth three-manifold, one integrable surface background $j$, area form and protected small Cayley coefficient family with common exact product generator charts. Let $J^0,J^1$ be two genuine time-collar paths with the same background endpoint structures, each regular at EVERY nonconstant zero-basepoint strip. Their actual index-one counts define the finite hat complexes $C^0,C^1$.\n\nFor a compact-transition source-dependent choice $K$ from $J^0$ to $J^1$ regular at every zero-basepoint solution, count its actual index-zero solutions WITHOUT translation quotient to obtain $\\Phi_K:C^0\\to C^1$. Then:\n\n1. $d^1\\Phi_K=\\Phi_Kd^0$.\n2. Two such compact-transition choices $K_0,K_1$ are joined by an ACTUAL regular relative parameter family. Its finite zero-basepoint index-minus-one solutions define a finite map $H$ with $\\Phi_{K_1}+\\Phi_{K_0}=d^1H+Hd^0$.\n3. Continuation matrices compose up to this actual chain homotopy; their identity choice is the constant longitudinal path, whose matrix is the identity. Reversing the endpoints gives a continuation map which is a chain-homotopy inverse.\n\nThe one-dimensional moduli used for the first two identities have actual compactifications as compact topological one-manifolds with exactly the corresponding endpoint and broken boundary sets, with the actual exhaustive collars. Consequently all paths in this fixed-background protected family satisfying the stated regularity have chain-homotopy equivalent chosen hat complexes.\n\nThis compares AUXILIARY PATHS on the same globally weak diagram and the same integrable surface background. It does not compare different $j$, every merely sector-admissible diagram, attaching-circle moves, integral orientations, absolute gradings, Spin-c decompositions or plus/infinity/cobordism packages. No polygon counts are asserted. Genus zero gives the identity of the one-generator zero complex.",
