          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-source-dependent-nearly-symmetric-polygons-admit-countable-choice-universal-regularity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Use the actual polygon map charts and nearly symmetric target geometry of the two named suppliers, with a fixed finite list of boundary labels and corners. Work over a finite-dimensional smooth open source/conformal/auxiliary parameter space $B$ of dimension $d$, with all neck lengths finite. Let $K_{b,z}$ be a smooth nearly symmetric coefficient family, equal to the specified transverse end structures near corner images, with uniform coefficient bounds on compact parameter subsets. Choose disjoint closed protected target neighborhoods $D,N$, with $D$ containing the diagonal and all chosen incidence divisors and $N$ a neighborhood of all the boundary tori and corner charts. The family is integrable on a neighborhood of $D$ and is tame elsewhere. Perturbations below fix neighborhoods of BOTH $D$ and $N$.\n\nThere is an explicitly constructed separable real Hilbert perturbation ball whose coefficient map is smooth into every fixed smooth compact-source norm, decays with every derivative faster than any exponential on each strip end, and has dense tangent fields on each compact subset of $B\\times S\\times(X\\setminus(D\\cup N))$. At EVERY actual nonconstant solution its universal Cauchy–Riemann derivative is onto on the actual $H^3_\\delta\\to H^2_\\delta$ realizations. Consequently a residual set in this ball gives regular universal nonconstant polygon moduli of dimension $\\mu+d$. In particular the spaces of negative such dimension are empty. When $d=0$ these are fixed-data regularity assertions; when $d=1$ slice counts are taken at regular parameter values, while the parameter is included in the universal dimension.\n\nThe construction can be restricted to an arbitrary open parameter region by multiplying its bumps by smooth parameter cutoffs, with universal surjectivity wherever those cutoffs are positive. It retains the chosen end limits and exact coefficients near every corner image; it does not claim that an infinite sum of source bumps is globally equal to the end data outside one common compact source subset. Nor does it claim regularity of constant polygons, compactness, coherent extension over nodal conformal faces, or count identities. Constant polygons must be treated or excluded separately in the application.",
