        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-nearly-symmetric-polygons-have-positive-domains-and-degree-energy-bounds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Fix a closed complex surface curve $(\\Sigma,j)$ with positive area form $\\eta$, and put $X=\\operatorname{Sym}^m\\Sigma$, $m>0$. Let $L_0,\\ldots,L_{n-1}$ be finitely many attaching product tori. Choose one interior point $q_R$ of each region cut out by all their attaching curves, and include any prescribed basepoints in this finite protected list. Let $D$ be a closed neighborhood of the repeated-point locus and these incidence divisors, disjoint from all the product tori. Write $\\omega$ for the descended product area form off the repeated-point locus.\n\nLet $u$ be a smooth finite-energy polygon on an actual punctured half-plane source with these successive boundary labels and uniform transverse corner limits. Suppose its source-dependent structure $J_z$ equals $\\operatorname{Sym}^m(j)$ on one fixed open neighborhood of $D$ and tames $\\omega$ elsewhere, with uniform taming and coefficient bounds. Then every region and marked multiplicity is a nonnegative integer and\n$$E(u)\\le C\\sum_R n_R(u)\\operatorname{Area}_\\eta(R).$$\nThe constant depends on the target metrics, protected neighborhoods and uniform structure bounds, but not on the conformal positions of the punctures. The same bound applies to a family with these common bounds. Domains are locally constant under boundary-preserving fixed-corner homotopies.\n\nChoose a closed neighborhood $N$ of the union of the tori disjoint from $D$. Every nonconstant such polygon has a source-interior point where $du\\ne0$ and $u$ lies outside $D\\cup N$. Thus a nonconstant polygon has a nonempty open set of nonzero derivatives in a free target region even when perturbations fix a whole neighborhood of all boundary tori. The statement does not assert that a constant polygon can be regularized by changing $J$.",
