        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-bounded-energy-polygons-have-stable-source-bubble-and-strip-limits",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Fix finitely many compact attaching product tori with pairwise transverse finite intersections, common exact product generator charts, fixed full boundary tensors on each torus and fixed nearly symmetric protected target geometry. Consider actual polygons on the sources in the finite-tree conformal charts. Suppose their source-dependent coefficient families have uniform smooth bounds on vertex compact subsets and unit neck windows. On every neck and external end they equal the common product structure near each relevant generator. Along any sequence receding from both vertex cores on a neck, all translated coefficient derivatives on compact windows converge to the specified autonomous end path. On an external end assume the same end convergence. Coefficient convergence is uniform in the target. On each labelled physical boundary its full tensor on that torus is the fixed boundary tensor, independently of the longitudinal source coordinate. Families assembled by the explicit vertex sewings with rapidly convergent end coefficients have these properties.\n\nEvery sequence of actual smooth polygons with fixed external corner labels and uniformly bounded energy has a subsequence with a finite stable limit: polygon components on a finite conformal source tree; finite chains of autonomous strips on its internal edges and external ends; and finite trees of nonconstant spheres and one-torus boundary disks at concentration points, with stable constant components where needed. Convergence is smooth on compact subsets away from the finite nodal points after the actual conformal rescalings and strip translations. All bubble and strip necks have vanishing lost energy and vanishing image diameter; attached values agree. Total energy is the sum of the component energies. For the fixed protected incidence points their nonnegative degrees add exactly across these components. No regularity, dimension or index assumption is used.\n\nIn particular, if every prescribed basepoint degree is zero and each attaching-system complement component contains one such basepoint, all sphere and one-torus disk bubbles are constant and are removed from the stable description. Only polygon vertices and strip chains remain. The theorem does not classify low-dimensional ends, prove transversality or construct gluing collars.\n\nThe exact end charts may have arbitrary constant product angles $0<\\theta_j<\\pi$. All transverse Fourier and cutoff estimates below use the positive finite minimum of $\\theta_j$ and $\\pi-\\theta_j$; no orthogonal crossing normalization is required.",
