      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The admissibility inputs are actual domain bounds, not a formal assumption that the desired moduli are compact. For a full single-diagram hat module, [F1] supplies a zero-Chern sector independently of whether it has generators; the required all-sector test therefore tests EVERY normalized periodic domain and gives the global weak pair condition. For several systems the multiperiodic condition in the Statement is imposed on the actual chosen auxiliary multidiagram; pairwise conditions alone are not substituted for it. Its nonnegative zero-mark domains are finite before any index or regularity choice, as proved in [F3]. For the ordinary weak specified-sector branch, fix the finitely many required corner tuples, arities and count dimensions zero and one. If a source has $d$ inputs and $p$ allowed family parameters, its fixed-map index is its count dimension minus $d-2+p$; the finite strata of a compact parameter family therefore require only a finite list of actual indices. Strips and anchored profiles have their stated respective index shifts. At zero entire mark vector [F2] supplies finitely many nonnegative domains for each such fixed Spin-c family and index list, independently of coefficients. Include the actual continuous concatenations representing the total classes of the required boundary identities: their total index is the same prescribed finite list by actual cap additivity, their marks are zero, and their total Spin-c extensions are the specified ones. Thus their domains belong to those same affine slices, not to a hypothetical finite class list. If a slice is nonempty choose one actual reference in it; there are only finitely many slices, so this uses finite choice. Their union is finite. The domain-energy estimate of [F6] supplies a uniform energy bound $E_0$ before the coefficient choices, on a small family with uniformly bounded geometric constants. Enlarge the finite list for the finitely many separated compositions and relative homotopies required, and choose a strict energy budget $E>E_0$. In the strict branch [F2] separately supplies its stronger finite upper-index domains and lower strip index. No lower index bound is claimed for ordinary weak data.",
