    "lem-tau-preserved-by-filtered-chain-homotopy-equivalence": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-knot-floer-filtration-and-tau",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere. Let $F$ be a compact connected oriented Seifert surface with boundary $K$, and let $\\mathcal H=(\\Sigma,\\boldsymbol\\alpha,\\boldsymbol\\beta,w,z)$ be an admissible doubly pointed Heegaard diagram representing $(K,F)$. The associated one-pointed hat chain complex $\\widehat{CF}(\\mathcal H)$ is the finite-dimensional chain complex over $\\mathbb F_2$ obtained from the minus complex by setting its basepoint variable to zero; its differential counts index-one disks with zero multiplicity at $w$. Assume for this specified data that the differential is nonincreasing in the Alexander filtration, that the absolute Alexander formula below takes integer values, and that $H(\\widehat{CF}(\\mathcal H))\\cong\\mathbb F_2$. Give a generator $\\mathbf x$ its absolute Alexander filtration\n$$A_F(\\mathbf x)=\\frac12\\left\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak s_{w,z}(\\mathbf x)),[\\widehat F]\\right\\rangle,$$\nwhere $\\mathfrak s_{w,z}(\\mathbf x)$ is the homology class of the nowhere-vanishing vector field assigned to $\\mathbf x$ by the pointed Morse construction, with its prescribed boundary data near the two basepoints; $c_1$ is the first Chern class of the oriented plane field perpendicular to that vector field. To form the zero-framed surgery, remove a tubular neighborhood of $K$ and glue in a solid torus whose meridian is the longitude induced by $F$. The surface $\\widehat F$ is obtained by deleting a boundary collar of $F$ and capping its longitude by the meridian disk of that solid torus. This is the standard integer normalization, with the one-generator diagram of the standard unknot at filtration zero. The subcomplex $F_s\\widehat{CF}(\\mathcal H)$ is spanned by generators of Alexander filtration at most $s$.\n\nMore generally, a finite integer-filtered chain complex is a finite-dimensional chain complex $C$ over $\\mathbb F_2$ with subcomplexes $F_sC$ such that $F_sC=0$ for all sufficiently small integers $s$ and $F_sC=C$ for all sufficiently large integers $s$. If $H(C)\\cong\\mathbb F_2$, define\n$$\\tau(C):=\\min\\{s\\in\\mathbb Z:\\operatorname{im}(H(F_sC)\\longrightarrow H(C))\\ne0\\}.$$\nFor the fixed diagram and surface above, write $\\tau_{\\mathcal H,F}(K)=\\tau(\\widehat{CF}(\\mathcal H),F_*\\widehat{CF}(\\mathcal H))$. Once diagram and surface independence is proved, their common value is the knot invariant $\\tau(K)$. Until that comparison is proved, $\\tau_{\\mathcal H,F}(K)$ denotes only the value for the specified data.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
