          "claim": "Fix an integer $m\\ge1$ and $a\\in\\mathbb R^n$. Since $\\sigma$ is not a polynomial, $\\sigma^{(m)}$ is not identically zero; choose $b_m$ with $\\sigma^{(m)}(b_m)\\ne0$. For $h\\ne0$ define $R_{m,h}(x):=\\frac{1}{\\sigma^{(m)}(b_m)h^m}\\sum_{j=0}^{m}(-1)^{m-j}\\binom mj\\sigma(b_m+jh(a\\cdot x))$. This is a finite linear combination of permitted units. The repeated-integral formula for the $m$th forward difference gives $R_{m,h}(x)=\\frac{(a\\cdot x)^m}{\\sigma^{(m)}(b_m)}\\int_{[0,1]^m}\\sigma^{(m)}(b_m+h(a\\cdot x)(t_1+\\cdots+t_m))\\,dt$. Because $a\\cdot x$ is bounded on compact $K$ and $\\sigma^{(m)}$ is uniformly continuous on a compact interval around $b_m$, $R_{m,h}\\to(a\\cdot x)^m$ uniformly on $K$ as $h\\to0$.",
