          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $(U_j,\\phi_j)_{j=1}^J$ and $(\\psi_j)_{j=1}^J$ be the boundary charts and square partition supplied by [F1], with $\\phi_j(U_j)\\Subset(1/4,3/4)^n$. Define\n$$\\mathcal Gf=\\bigl((\\psi_j f)\\circ\\phi_j^{-1}\\bigr)_{j=1}^J,\\qquad \\mathcal L(v_1,\\ldots,v_J)=\\sum_{j=1}^J\\psi_j(v_j\\circ\\phi_j),$$\nwhere each chart component is extended by zero outside its chart image and each summand by zero off $U_j$. Since every $\\psi_j$ is compactly supported in $U_j$, the zero extensions preserve smoothness for smooth inputs. Change of variables, the bounded chart Jacobians, and the product and chain rules show that $\\mathcal G$ and $\\mathcal L$ are bounded on $L^2$ and $H^1$. Direct substitution gives $\\mathcal L\\mathcal Gf=\\sum_j\\psi_j^2f=f$ almost everywhere. For $N\\ge1$, Fourier Parseval [F4] gives componentwise\n$$\\|(I-P_N)\\mathcal Gf\\|_{\\oplus L^2(\\mathbb T^n)}^2\\le (2\\pi N)^{-2}\\|\\nabla\\mathcal Gf\\|_{\\oplus L^2(\\mathbb T^n)}^2\\le C N^{-2}\\|f\\|_{H^1(M)}^2.$$\nFor $N=0$, boundedness of $\\mathcal G$ on $H^1$ gives the same estimate after increasing $C$. Therefore\n$$\\|f-\\mathcal L P_N\\mathcal Gf\\|_2\\le C(1+N)^{-1}\\|f\\|_{H^1(M)}.$$\nThe finite Fourier sums $\\mathcal L P_N\\mathcal Gf$ are smooth functions on $M$ and converge to $f$ in $L^2$, so smooth functions are dense in $L^2(M)$.",
