          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-calderon-boundary-trace-quotient-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$ and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a nonempty bounded connected open set whose boundary is covered by finitely many $C^1$ graph charts. Give $H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)=W^{1,2}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ its Sobolev norm, let $H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ be the $H^1$-closure of $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$, and let $W^{1/2,2}(\\partial\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ have the finite-chart Slobodeckij norm. Define $\\mathcal D(\\Omega):=H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)/H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ with its quotient norm. The trace induces a bounded linear bijection $\\overline T:\\mathcal D(\\Omega)\\to W^{1/2,2}(\\partial\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ with bounded inverse, so these two norms are equivalent under the induced identification. The induced map after quotienting by constants is also a bounded linear bijection $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)/\\mathbb K\\mathbf1\\to W^{1/2,2}(\\partial\\Omega;\\mathbb K)/\\mathbb K$, with equivalent quotient norms. A continuous linear functional on $W^{1/2,2}(\\partial\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$ descends to the constant quotient exactly when it annihilates constants, and its descended functional has the quotient dual norm.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-lax-milgram",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a real or complex Hilbert space, let $a$ be a bounded coercive sesquilinear form on $H$ with constants $M,\\alpha$ ([[def-bounded-coercive-and-symmetric-sesquilinear-forms]]), and let $F:H\\to\\mathbb K$ be a bounded conjugate-linear functional with norm $\\|F\\|=\\sup_{\\|v\\|\\le1}|F(v)|$. Then there is a unique $u\\in H$ with $$a(u,v)=F(v)\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H,$$ and it satisfies $\\alpha\\|u\\|\\le\\|F\\|$, that is $\\|u\\|\\le\\|F\\|/\\alpha$. The real bilinear case is the same statement with $a$ symmetric or not, $F$ a bounded linear functional, and the conjugation read as the identity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-extension-theorem-for-bounded-smooth-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $k\\ge1$, $1\\le p\\le\\infty$,\n$\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a\nbounded $C^k$ domain in the graph sense of\n[[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]]. Then for every open set\n$V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\overline\\Omega\\subseteq V$ there is a bounded\nlinear extension operator\n$$E:W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)\\longrightarrow W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K),\\qquad (Eu)|_\\Omega=u\\ \\text{a.e. on }\\Omega,$$\nsuch that $\\operatorname{supp}(Eu)$ is a compact subset of $V$ for every\n$u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. In particular each bounded $C^k$ domain is a\n$W^{k,p}$-extension domain, with an operator whose output is supported in any\nprescribed neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$. For $k=0$, extension by zero\nis an isometric $L^p$ extension on any open $\\Omega$; its output is supported\nin the compact set $\\overline\\Omega\\subseteq V$ when $\\Omega$ is bounded.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ convention of [[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]. Let S be a compact embedded $C^1$ hypersurface in $\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$. Choose finitely many regular injective $C^1$ parametrizations $X_j:V_j\\to S\\cap O_j$ with $C^1$ coordinate transitions, and an ambient partition $\\chi_j$ from [[lem-finite-ambient-partitions-for-euclidean-boundary-integration]], with supports compactly contained in the corresponding chart neighborhoods. Put $J_{X_j}=\\sqrt{\\det(DX_j^TDX_j)}$, using [[def-gram-matrix-and-gram-determinant]].\n\nFor a nonnegative Borel f on S define $\\int_S f\\,dS=\\sum_j\\int_{V_j}(\\chi_j f)(X_j(y))J_{X_j}(y)\\,dy$, where the integrals on the right are those of [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]] and $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Set $S(A)=\\int_S\\mathbf1_A\\,dS$ for Borel A. For signed f with $\\int_S|f|\\,dS<\\infty$ use the difference of the positive and negative integrals. The same chart formula restricts to a compact Borel face contained in a regular patch. The empty surface has zero integral. Independence and finiteness are established by the following chart-independence lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-surface-integral-is-independent-of-c-one-boundary-charts",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Every regular chart density is continuous and strictly positive. The chart integral on a compact embedded $C^1$ hypersurface defines a finite Borel measure independent of the finite charts and subordinate partitions. In graph coordinates $X(y)=(y,h(y))$ its density is $\\sqrt{1+|Dh|^2}$. On a one-sided domain boundary the outward unit normal agrees on chart overlaps and is continuous.",
          "uses": [
