          "claim": "Here is the needed half-order identification, without assuming an unproved equivalence for the full spectral scale. For a Hilbert pair $(E_0,E_1)$ use $K(t,f)^2=\\inf_{f=f_0+f_1}(\\|f_0\\|_{E_0}^2+t^2\\|f_1\\|_{E_1}^2)$. In the spectral $L^2,H^1$ pair, independent minimization of each coefficient gives $K(t,f)^2=\\sum_j|f_j|^2t^2w_j/(1+t^2w_j)$. Integration against $t^{-2}\\,dt$ yields a fixed positive multiple of $\\sum_jw_j^{1/2}|f_j|^2$, by substituting $t\\sqrt{w_j}$ and Tonelli. Use the single-boundary versions of the chart maps: $Gf=((\\psi_jf)\\circ\\phi_j^{-1})_j$ and $L(v)=\\sum_j\\psi_j(v_j\\circ\\phi_j)$, with zero extension. The finite chart Jacobian bounds and product rule give their $L^2,H^1$ bounds, while $\\sum_j\\psi_j^2=1$ gives $LG=I$, exactly as in [F3] with one chart factor. These maps bound the respective $K$ functionals in both directions (apply either map to every decomposition, using its endpoint bounds and its left inverse). For a compactly supported chart function the identical Fourier calculation gives weight $(1+|\\xi|^2)^{1/2}$. This is equivalent to its Slobodeckij norm: Parseval applied to $f(\\cdot+h)-f$ and integration against $|h|^{-n-1}\\,dh$ give the homogeneous weight $c_n|\\xi|$, with $0<c_n<\\infty$ by splitting $|h|\\le1$ and $|h|>1$. Smooth compact chart cutoffs and bi-Lipschitz changes of coordinates preserve this norm by the difference definition, using $|\\psi(x)-\\psi(y)|\\le C|x-y|$ for the product term. Thus [F2] identifies the trace quotient with $H^{1/2}_\\Delta/\\mathbb R$, and duality identifies its mean-zero dual with $H^{-1/2}_\\Delta$. Subtracting the total surface mean is bounded in every norm here, even when the boundary is disconnected.",
