          "claim": "Write $f=f_{\\mathrm R}+if_{\\mathrm I}$ and $F_\\nu=f_\\nu\\circ T$ for $\\nu\\in\\{\\mathrm R,\\mathrm I\\}$. Each $f_\\nu$ is real smooth; since $DT$ is constant, repeated applications of [F6] give $\\partial_s^kF_\\nu(s,t)=(\\theta\\cdot\\nabla)^k f_\\nu(T(s,t))$. Thus, for each integer $k\\ge0$, $\\partial_s^kF$ is continuous. On every compact $s$-interval, $\\partial_s^{k+1}F$ is uniformly continuous on the product with $[-M,M]$. For each real component, apply [F5] on $[0,1]$ to $u\\mapsto\\partial_s^kF_\\nu(s+uh,t)$. By [F6] its derivative is $h\\,\\partial_s^{k+1}F_\\nu(s+uh,t)$, continuous and hence integrable by [F2], with the same one-sided derivatives at the endpoints. Combining the two real equalities and dividing by $h$ gives, for sufficiently small nonzero $h$ of either sign,\n$$\\frac{\\partial_s^kF(s+h,t)-\\partial_s^kF(s,t)}{h}=\\int_0^1\\partial_s^{k+1}F(s+uh,t)\\,du.$$\nThe right side converges uniformly for $t\\in[-M,M]$ to $\\partial_s^{k+1}F(s,t)$ as $h\\to0$. Integrating in $t$ therefore proves inductively that $\\frac{d^k}{ds^k}Rf(\\theta,s)=\\int_{-M}^{M}\\partial_s^kF(s,t)\\,dt$ for every $k$, and uniform continuity also makes each derivative continuous in $s$. Thus $Rf(\\theta,\\cdot)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$, so its Fourier transform in [F1] is defined.",
