          "claim": "Since $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$, it is a Schwartz function. Choose $R>0$ with $\\operatorname{supp}f\\subset B_R(0)$. For every $\\theta\\in S^1$ and $s\\in\\mathbb R$, the integrand $f(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)$ vanishes for $|t|>R$, because $|s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp|^2=s^2+t^2$. Thus the line integral is over the fixed interval $[-R,R]$. On every bounded $s$-interval, $(\\theta,s,t)\\mapsto f(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)$ is uniformly continuous on the compact parameter product with $S^1$ and $[-R,R]$, so its integral is jointly continuous in $(\\theta,s)$. For fixed $\\theta$, write $f=f_{\\mathrm R}+if_{\\mathrm I}$ and $F_\\nu(s,t)=f_\\nu(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)$ for $\\nu\\in\\{\\mathrm R,\\mathrm I\\}$. The inner coordinate map is linear with constant derivative, so repeated use of [F12] gives $\\partial_s^kF_\\nu(s,t)=(\\theta\\cdot\\nabla)^k f_\\nu(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)$ for every $k$. Apply [F11] on $[0,1]$ to each real function $u\\mapsto\\partial_s^kF_\\nu(s+uh,t)$: by [F12] its derivative is $h\\,\\partial_s^{k+1}F_\\nu(s+uh,t)$, which is continuous and Riemann integrable by [F13], including at the endpoints. Dividing the resulting identity by any nonzero $h$ gives the difference quotient as the $u$-integral of $\\partial_s^{k+1}F_\\nu(s+uh,t)$. Uniform continuity on each compact $(s,t)$ rectangle makes this converge uniformly in $t\\in[-R,R]$; integrating and recombining the two real components therefore gives $\\partial_s^k Rf(\\theta,s)=\\int_{-R}^{R}(\\theta\\cdot\\nabla)^k f(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)\\,dt$ for every $k\\ge0$, with each derivative continuous. Also $Rf(\\theta,s)=0$ for $|s|>R$, so $Rf(\\theta,\\cdot)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ and is Schwartz. For every $x$, joint continuity makes $\\theta\\mapsto Rf(\\theta,x\\cdot\\theta)$ continuous; [F4] makes $\\mu$ finite, so the defining backprojection integral exists. By [F6] and [F7], the Lebesgue Fourier transforms of $f$ and $Rf(\\theta,\\cdot)$ are Schwartz and satisfy pointwise inversion; [F8] identifies them with the compact-support Riemann transforms in [F1]. Also $|\\widehat f(\\xi)|\\le\\int|f|$, and [F4] shows $|\\xi|^{-1}\\widehat f(\\xi)$ is integrable near $0$; outside the unit disk it is dominated by the integrable Schwartz function $|\\widehat f|$. Rapid decay of $\\widehat f$ and finiteness of $\\mu$ make the later signed-frequency integrals absolutely convergent.",
