            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "We prove that a rounded polygonal region is a smooth disk. Take the finite triangulation in [F3]. The dual adjacency graph of its triangles, with an exterior root adjacent across every boundary edge, is connected. Indeed the triangles at any one vertex form a consecutive cyclic fan or a consecutive interval fan, so all their sectors are joined through shared edges, and connectedness of the triangulated region joins all fans. Choose a finite spanning tree of this dual graph rooted at the exterior. Process triangles in increasing distance from that root. Remove each triangle and its edge toward its parent: this edge is free, since its only other incident triangle has already been removed or is exterior. Removing a triangle with its free side leaves its two other sides and vertices, preserves connectedness, and preserves $V-E+F$. Plane Euler in [F3], with the exterior counted as one extra face, gives initial value $V-E+F=1$. At the end the remaining connected graph $K$ therefore has $V-E=1$, so it is a tree: a spanning tree already has $V-1$ edges, leaving no edge outside that spanning tree.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Inscribe a polygon with a sufficiently fine cyclic mesh in $\\gamma$. Uniform continuity of $\\gamma'$ gives positive scalar product of each chord direction with the tangent at every nearby normal projection, and the chord error tends uniformly to zero. Hence the normal projection of each chord is strictly increasing in its cyclic coordinate; the whole polygon has degree one under normal projection and is a normal graph. In particular it is embedded. Smooth its finitely many corners in disjoint small graph charts: convolution of the local graph function with a smooth compactly supported kernel on a smaller interval, joined by a smooth cutoff on a larger interval, leaves it unchanged outside that chart and can be made arbitrarily small in height and tangent direction. The smoothed curve $P'$ is still a normal graph $r=f(s)$, with $f$ smooth and arbitrarily small. Choose a smooth cutoff $\\chi$ supported in $(-a,a)$ and equal to one near zero. For $\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|\\chi'\\|_\\infty<1$, the map $(s,r)\\mapsto(s,r+\\chi(r)f(s))$ is a diffeomorphism, because its normal derivative is positive, and equals the identity near the ends of the strip. It extends by the identity to a smooth plane diffeomorphism carrying $\\gamma$ onto the rounded polygon $P'$. The same graph construction compares any two sufficiently small choices of corner rounding. By [F2], the polygon has bounded and unbounded regions; rounding preserves them by local graph homeomorphisms: in a graph chart, a continuous vertical displacement, cut off in the transverse direction with positive transverse derivative, carries the original piecewise affine graph to the rounded graph, and transporting them by the displayed plane diffeomorphism proves the corresponding separation and smooth boundary charts for $\\gamma$.",
